window.COURSE = {"lessons":[{"id":"setup","file":"course/lessons/00-setup-guide.md","nav":"Before you begin","title":"Your robotics workbench","tag":"START HERE","minutes":90,"demo":"readiness","outcome":"A working Jazzy environment and a plan for the phone lab.","agenda":["Environment & prerequisites","Install and verify","Build the four packages","Qualify phone networking before lecture five"],"notes":["Send setup five days before class and collect talker/listener and build evidence.","Preconfigure trusted TLS, one qualified phone calibration, the optional engine and a recorded fallback."],"quiz":[["A learner has Ubuntu 22.04 and wants to follow this guide’s ROS 2 Jazzy binary installation commands. Which environment matches the course?",["Ubuntu 24.04 with ROS 2 Jazzy binaries","Ubuntu 22.04 with a renamed Jazzy installation directory","Ubuntu 24.04 with binaries from any ROS distribution"],0,"Jazzy’s Ubuntu binary target is Noble 24.04. Prepare that environment so the supported packages and course commands match."],["The workspace built successfully yesterday. In a newly opened terminal ros2 cannot find ros2_course_py. What is the most direct next check?",["Regenerate every interface without inspecting the environment","Source Jazzy and the workspace install/setup.bash in this terminal","Delete the source package and reinstall the phone app"],1,"The overlay must be in this terminal’s environment. A successful build does not automatically source future terminals."],["A student has installed the Android APK and configured adb reverse tcp:8443 tcp:8443 to a running laptop receiver. Which app address uses this wired USB route?",["wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android, with the receiver fingerprint and token","The public course website URL, because it hosts the APK download","The phone hotspot gateway address, without a receiver port or token"],0,"ADB reverse forwards the phone localhost port over USB to the host receiver. The same APK uses the laptop hotspot address for wireless; both routes retain certificate pinning and the session token."],["A class wants the phone-camera project to show a 3D reconstruction. Which pre-class preparation is required beyond installing the Android APK?",["A qualified backend, matching measured calibration, trusted network path and a rehearsed reference run","Only installation of the APK, with no laptop receiver or calibration","An arbitrary CameraInfo matrix copied from a different phone"],0,"The Android app transports measurements over USB or wireless. Calibration and a working SLAM engine are separate required dependencies."]],"reflections":[["Describe the underlay and overlay you source in a new terminal, including their order and why each is needed.","Source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash for ROS, then ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash for built course packages. Explain that environment changes are per terminal.","Include your executable-list output."],["Explain which laptop components and pairing information are needed after installing the Android APK.","The laptop runs the updated ROS receiver with TLS and a session token. The APK needs its reachable /ws/android endpoint, certificate fingerprint and token. The optional mapper additionally needs its prepared engine and measured settings.","Draw the two machines and identify where each program runs."]],"body":"# Your robotics workbench\n\nInstall Jazzy, learn the terminal, and prepare your phone as a sensor.\n\n## What you need before starting\n\nUse **ROS 2 Jazzy Jalisco on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS (Noble)** with Bash. Jazzy is a long-term-support release supported until May 2029. This operating-system and ROS pairing is used throughout the guide, so the installation commands and examples fit together.\n\nYou need basic Python variables, functions and classes. Robotics knowledge is not assumed. C++ classes and templates help with lecture 3, where a complete scaffold is supplied. A node is a ROS participant, a package is a unit of software, and a workspace is the directory where packages are built. The first lecture explains these ideas through experiments.\n\nAllow 1–2 hours for setup and a separate capstone rehearsal. A laptop with 16 GB RAM and 30 GB free disk is comfortable. The project device is an Android 8.0+ phone with a rear camera and preferably accelerometer/gyroscope sensors. A native APK is supplied. The mapper uses camera images; unsupported motion sensors can be documented without blocking that part of the project.\n\nThe website has two reading modes. **Self-study** shows the full guide with exercises and model explanations. **Lecture** shows one section at a time with presentation controls and a timer. Your progress, notes and written answers are saved in this browser; export them from Resources before changing computers. The interactive labs illustrate concepts, while commands marked Ubuntu · Bash run in your Linux terminal.\n\n## Native Ubuntu or Windows with WSL\n\nNative Ubuntu and Ubuntu 24.04 inside WSL 2 can host the receiver. WSL adds a Windows-to-Ubuntu network boundary. [Annex F](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md) gives a USB route using Windows ADB and two Wi-Fi routes, with explicit checks at each boundary.\n\nFor Windows, run in administrator PowerShell, restart when requested, and create a Linux account:\n\n```powershell\nwsl --update\nwsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04\nwsl -l -v\n```\n\nConfirm VERSION 2. From now on, run the course's Bash commands **inside Ubuntu**, not PowerShell. Store the workspace under the Linux home directory, where Linux file permissions and build tools work consistently. Test WSLg with RViz before class. Rehearse the WSL connection before class. Keep a reference bag or a prepared teaching laptop available if a student machine needs more setup time.\n\n## Install the correct ROS distribution\n\nRun `lsb_release -cs`; expect `noble`. Follow the official [Jazzy Ubuntu installation](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Installation/Ubuntu-Install-Debs.html) for locale, Universe, ROS repository configuration and system updates before installing packages. Those repository instructions are maintained upstream. Then install the course tools:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install ros-jazzy-desktop ros-dev-tools \\\n  ros-jazzy-turtlesim ros-jazzy-rqt-graph ros-jazzy-tf2-tools \\\n  ros-jazzy-cv-bridge ros-jazzy-image-view \\\n  ros-jazzy-camera-calibration python3-aiohttp python3-opencv \\\n  python3-numpy python3-yaml\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\nsudo rosdep init\nrosdep update\n```\n\nRun rosdep init once only; if an existing configuration is reported, continue with update. Use the Ubuntu system Python that matches ROS. The base course does not require Gazebo, RTAB-Map or ORB-SLAM3 to be compiled. ORB-SLAM3 is a separately prepared optional backend for the final live demonstration, not an apt package supplied by this course.\n\n## Use the terminal and source your environment\n\n`pwd` prints your current directory; `ls` lists files; `cd` changes directory; `mkdir -p` creates a directory and missing parents. Tab completes paths, the up arrow recalls a command, and Ctrl+C stops a foreground program. A terminal running a node is busy; open another terminal for inspection commands.\n\nThe ROS installation is your **underlay**. Your built workspace supplies an **overlay** containing your own packages. Source the underlay in every terminal, then the overlay after a successful build. Keep ROS discovery local while the phone uses a WebSocket connection to just one ROS receiver:\n\n```bash\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\nunset ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY\nexport ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST\nexport ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42\nmkdir -p ~/ros2_ws/src\ncd ~/ros2_ws\n```\n\nUse the same values in every lab terminal. ROS_DOMAIN_ID separates graphs; it is not authentication. ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE affects ROS discovery, not the separate HTTPS/WSS server. A phone does not need DDS or ROS installed. For a physical ROS robot on another machine, deliberately reconfigure discovery and networking.\n\n### Save ROS sourcing and DDS settings in .bashrc\n\nRun this **inside Ubuntu as your normal Linux user**. It appends one labelled block only once. The underlay loads first; the overlay loads only when its build exists. Jazzy's default Fast DDS implementation is selected explicitly so all course terminals use the same middleware. No separate DDS XML file is supplied or required for this local lab.\n\n```bash\nif ! grep -q '^# BEGIN ROS2 COURSE$' ~/.bashrc; then\n  cat >> ~/.bashrc <<'EOF'\n\n# BEGIN ROS2 COURSE\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\nexport RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_fastrtps_cpp\nunset ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY\nunset ROS_STATIC_PEERS\nexport ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST\nexport ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42\nif [ -f \"$HOME/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash\" ]; then\n  source \"$HOME/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash\"\nfi\n# END ROS2 COURSE\nEOF\nfi\nsource ~/.bashrc\nprintenv ROS_DISTRO RMW_IMPLEMENTATION ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE ROS_DOMAIN_ID\n```\n\nExpected, on separate lines: jazzy, rmw_fastrtps_cpp, LOCALHOST, 42. Open a fresh Ubuntu terminal and repeat the printenv check. After the first successful workspace build, source ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash in existing terminals; new interactive Bash terminals load it automatically. Keep optional SLAM overlays explicit, after the course overlay. If changing middleware or domain during diagnosis, stop lab nodes and run `ros2 daemon stop`, then reopen terminals with consistent settings. Jazzy warns that ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY is deprecated. Unset that legacy variable and use ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST consistently; do not combine conflicting discovery profiles or DDS XML settings from another lab.\n\n## Build and verify the supplied packages\n\nDownload the ROS workspace ZIP from Resources. Extract it and copy the four package directories from ros/workspace/src into ~/ros2_ws/src. The path `~/ros2_ws/src/ros2_course_py/package.xml` should exist, with the other three packages beside it. Place package.xml directly inside each package directory so colcon can discover the four packages.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/ros2_ws\nrosdep install --from-paths src --ignore-src -r -y\ncolcon build --symlink-install\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 pkg executables ros2_course_py\n```\n\nExpected: four packages finish and the executable list includes talker, listener and phone_bridge. Resolve the first dependency/compiler error before continuing. Rebuild after changing interfaces, package metadata, Python entry points or C++ code. Symlink installation does not remove all rebuild requirements.\n\nTest the installed ROS environment in terminal A:\n\n```bash\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\nros2 run demo_nodes_cpp talker\n```\n\nIn terminal B with the same environment:\n\n```bash\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\nros2 run demo_nodes_py listener\n```\n\nExpected: the listener prints the talker's strings. Also run rviz2 and confirm a usable 3D window. Close the demo nodes before starting the lecture exercises.\n\n\n\n![Simulated setup checkpoint: Jazzy on noble, four packages built, then a sourced overlay makes the executables visible.](../assets/setup-terminal.svg)\n\n\n## Prepare the phone project before lecture five\n\nRead [Phone lab setup](../annexes/dataset-guide.md) first. [Annex A](../annexes/android-development.md) explains the Android app, [Annex B](../annexes/bridge-development.md) traces the ROS receiver and [Annex C](../annexes/calibration.md) walks through calibration. WSL users should complete [Annex F](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md). The [visual guide](../annexes/visual-guide.md) collects expected-output illustrations.\n\nInstall the supplied classroom test APK on Android. Pair using the receiver’s endpoint, session token and certificate SHA-256. Test the chosen Wi-Fi or USB route, camera mode, timestamp source, sensor availability and Stop behavior before class. Keep ROS discovery local to the laptop. [Annex E](../annexes/android-connections.md) gives the two connection options: wired USB and wireless over the phone hotspot.\n\nFor mapping, prepare the optional ORB-SLAM3 backend, measure camera calibration in the exact transmitted image mode and rehearse initialization. The baseline result is a sparse monocular reconstruction with arbitrary scale. Native inertial streams are available for inspection and further development. Supply a consented, qualified reference bag and matching calibration for learners whose phone cannot complete the live exercise.\n\nOfficial references: [Jazzy installation](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Installation/Ubuntu-Install-Debs.html), [Android Camera2](https://developer.android.com/media/camera/camera2), [WSL networking](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/networking), and [WSLg](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/tutorials/gui-apps).\n"},{"id":"graph","file":"course/lessons/01-lecture1-architecture.md","nav":"Nodes & the ROS graph","title":"Think in nodes and messages","tag":"LECTURE 01","minutes":90,"demo":"graph","outcome":"Inspect typed streams and diagnose a broken connection.","agenda":["Mental model · 15 min","Turtlesim & CLI · 25 min","Names & debugging · 20 min","Lab · 20 min","Checkpoint · 10 min"],"notes":["Drive the turtle first, then reveal the publisher, topic and subscriber.","Deliberately remap only one command endpoint and require graph evidence before fixing it."],"quiz":[["A native phone app sends JPEGs over WSS to a Python receiver that publishes Image messages. Which component participates directly in the ROS graph?",["The phone’s hotspot configuration page","The ROS receiver node on the laptop","Each individual JPEG file"],1,"The phone is a web sensor client; phone_bridge is the ROS participant publishing typed topics."],["Two nodes appear in ros2 node list, but the turtle does not move. Which observation best establishes a correctly connected command stream?",["Both nodes use the same source language","The publisher and subscriber resolve to the same topic/type with compatible QoS, and samples are observed","Both programs were launched from the same directory"],1,"Process existence alone does not establish endpoint compatibility or data flow."],["A second turtlesim is launched under namespace /robot2. Which command topic sends velocity commands to /robot2/turtle1?",["/robot2/turtle1/cmd_vel","/turtle1/cmd_vel without any remapping","/robot2 because all namespaced messages use one topic"],0,"The namespace prefixes the relative command-topic name. Namespaces do not merge all interfaces into one stream."],["A publisher’s relative chatter name is remapped to camera_events, while its subscriber is unchanged. What behavior should you predict?",["The subscriber automatically follows the publisher’s new name","Both endpoints connect because message types still match","They are disconnected until their resolved topic names agree"],2,"Remapping is applied to individual endpoints. Type agreement cannot compensate for different resolved names."],["You stop a velocity publisher with Ctrl+C while a physical base is moving. Which statement is appropriate before claiming the base is safely stopped?",["Process exit guarantees a zero command reaches the motor","A zero command and independent stop/watchdog behavior must be considered","Changing ROS_DOMAIN_ID immediately removes motor power"],1,"Software command delivery and actuator safety are different concerns. A stopped publisher need not stop a consumer."]],"reflections":[["Draw the phone-to-laptop data path and label the boundary where a WebSocket packet becomes a ROS message.","The Android app sends WSS packets to phone_bridge, which validates/decodes them and publishes typed ROS topics. The estimator subscribes through ROS.","Name the image topic and type."],["A topic is listed but its subscriber is silent. Describe your diagnosis in an order that can distinguish a naming problem from a QoS problem.","Check live processes, resolved names/types, verbose endpoints and QoS, then data and environment/network configuration. Collect evidence at each stage so the diagnosis identifies the failing contract.","Include one relevant CLI command for each stage."]],"body":"# Think in nodes and messages\n\nA robot is a team of small programs. Learn to see the conversation.\n\n## See the graph\n\nROS 2 (Robot Operating System 2) is a set of libraries, communication tools and conventions for robotic systems. It runs on an operating system; it does not replace Linux. A **node** has a focused job: reading a camera, estimating motion or responding to a command. One process can host one node or several composed nodes.\n\nA **topic** is a named stream. Publishers send typed messages and subscriptions receive them through callbacks. A camera driver can publish frames while a viewer and an odometry node independently subscribe. The publisher does not directly call each subscriber. Endpoints need the same resolved topic name, compatible type and compatible QoS.\n\nMost course traffic uses DDS through ROS's middleware abstraction. Discovery removes the need for a central ROS master. It does not guarantee reachability: network isolation, multicast, domains and endpoint policies can still stop delivery.\n\n**By the end:** inspect a graph, explain what a topic carries, send a command and diagnose a silent subscriber. These same skills will debug camera streams in lecture 5.\n\n## Bring a turtle to life\n\nTerminal A, with Jazzy sourced:\n\n```bash\nros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node\n```\n\nTerminal B, also sourced:\n\n```bash\nros2 run turtlesim turtle_teleop_key\n```\n\nKeep terminal B focused and press the arrow keys. Expected: the turtle moves in its window. Keyboard commands reach the simulator through `/turtle1/cmd_vel`.\n\nRun turtle_teleop_key in an interactive Ubuntu terminal. A piped command, background task or automation without a TTY can abort with `Failed to get old console mode`. Reopen it in a normal Ubuntu terminal; use topic pub below for scripted motion checks.\n\nIn terminal C:\n\n```bash\nros2 node list\nros2 node info /turtlesim\nros2 topic list -t\nros2 topic info /turtle1/cmd_vel --verbose\nros2 topic echo /turtle1/pose --once\nrqt_graph\n```\n\nExpected: `/turtlesim` and `/teleop_turtle` appear. Pose contains x, y, theta and velocities. Infrastructure topics may appear too; rqt_graph filters can hide unused endpoints. Use the topic name and message type to match this graph to your own output.\n\n![Simulated turtlesim and graph view: the keyboard controller publishes Twist to the simulator through cmd_vel.](../assets/turtle-graph.svg)\n\n\n## Read an interface\n\n```bash\nros2 interface show geometry_msgs/msg/Twist\nros2 interface show turtlesim/msg/Pose\nros2 topic hz /turtle1/pose\n```\n\nTwist contains linear and angular Vector3 fields. For the 2D turtle, linear.x drives forward and angular.z turns. Physical robots typically interpret these as metres per second and radians per second. Turtlesim screen coordinates are simulation units, with coordinates defined by the simulator.\n\nStop the keyboard controller before adding another command publisher:\n\n```bash\nros2 topic pub --rate 2 /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist \\\n  \"{linear: {x: 1.0}, angular: {z: 0.5}}\"\n```\n\nExpected: a circle. Press Ctrl+C, then send a zero command:\n\n```bash\nros2 topic pub --once /turtle1/cmd_vel geometry_msgs/msg/Twist \\\n  \"{linear: {x: 0.0}, angular: {z: 0.0}}\"\n```\n\nA zero command is a software request, not an emergency stop. A physical base needs motion limits, a command watchdog and an independent stop. Stopping a publisher alone need not stop its consumer.\n\n## Reconnect without editing code\n\n`chatter` is relative; `/chatter` is absolute. Namespaces group interfaces, while remapping changes connections at runtime. Start another simulator:\n\n```bash\nros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node --ros-args -r __ns:=/robot2\n```\n\nExpected: `/robot2/turtlesim` and `/robot2/turtle1/pose`. A namespace changes graph names. It does not automatically change physical coordinate frames or securely isolate traffic.\n\nCamera connection: launch remappings can connect `/phone/camera/image_raw` to an estimator's input. Inspect the actual stream first. Renaming an image does not rectify pixels or repair calibration.\n\n## Debug from the outside in\n\n1. Check that the intended process is still running and its terminal has no error.\n2. Run node list and topic list in a terminal with the same ROS environment.\n3. Use topic info with --verbose: inspect endpoints, type and QoS.\n4. Echo one sample; select best effort for a best-effort sensor if needed.\n5. Check domain, discovery and network reachability before editing application code.\n\n`ros2 topic hz` measures the samples received by that tool. It is not a precise hardware frequency or end-to-end latency measurement. Topic discovery alone is not proof that useful data arrives.\n\nDeliberately remap the keyboard node's command topic to `/wrong_cmd_vel`. Predict the disconnected graph, test it and repair the remap. Stop that node before restarting the correct teleop.\n\nRead more: the official [nodes guide](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-CLI-Tools/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes/Understanding-ROS2-Nodes.html) shows additional node introspection commands, while the [topics guide](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-CLI-Tools/Understanding-ROS2-Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics.html) explains echo, rate and interface inspection.\n\n## Practice and prove it\n\n**In class, 20 minutes:** drive a circle, inspect Twist and start a second namespaced turtle. Submit the topic names and explain which publisher can move which turtle. Success means the explanation matches the graph.\n\n**Homework, 30 minutes:** publish a straight segment and a turn, send zero velocity and draw the command and pose connections. Record which terminal runs each node and save the CLI evidence.\n\n**Checkpoint:** a publisher and subscriber agree on name and type but exchange no samples. Inspect endpoint QoS, then environment and transport. The name is only part of the contract.\n\nSource: [ROS 2 beginner CLI tutorials](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-CLI-Tools.html). Next: create your own publisher and subscriber in Python.\n"},{"id":"python","file":"course/lessons/02-lecture2-python-nodes.md","nav":"Build with Python","title":"Write your first Python nodes","tag":"LECTURE 02","minutes":90,"demo":"terminal","outcome":"Build a publisher, subscriber and generated interface.","agenda":["Workspace · 10 min","Publisher · 20 min","Packaging · 15 min","Subscriber & interfaces · 20 min","Lab · 25 min"],"notes":["Type the complete minimal publisher and subscriber. Distinguish a Node object from an installed executable.","Use the reference package to catch up, then ask learners to explain callback ownership and interface generation."],"quiz":[["A timer callback contains a two-second sleep in a node spun by a single-threaded executor. What effect should you expect on that executor’s other ready callbacks?",["They can be delayed until the blocking callback returns","They automatically move into separate operating-system processes","Their topic names change to include the timer period"],0,"An executor cannot dispatch another callback on that same thread while the current one blocks."],["You add temperature_c to RobotStatus.msg, but Python cannot import the changed generated type. Which workflow is needed?",["Edit only the publisher’s import statement","Regenerate/build the interface and dependent consumers, then source the overlay","Refresh the course page so it regenerates ROS messages"],1,"The .msg definition must be processed into generated code, and the resulting installation must be sourced."],["The scratch hello.py runs with python3, but ros2 run my_camera_lab hello reports no executable. Which package configuration should you inspect?",["The console_scripts entry point, build/install result and sourced overlay","The phone hotspot SSID only","The name of the String message’s data field"],0,"Direct script execution and an installed ROS executable use different discovery paths."],["A publisher requests a 0.5-second timer. Which measurement would be a reasonable expectation on an ordinary non-real-time student laptop?",["Exactly two samples in every subscriber interval, with no jitter","An approximate 2 Hz stream, with scheduling and measurement variation","A guaranteed 0.5-second network latency for every sample"],1,"A timer requests scheduling; it is not a hard timing or network-latency guarantee."],["A student wants to describe a camera measurement with time and coordinate meaning. Which interface choice best supports downstream ROS tools?",["A String containing arbitrary unlabeled pixel text","A standard Image with a meaningful Header plus matching CameraInfo","A custom message whose only field is the node’s filename"],1,"Standard sensor interfaces make dimensions, encoding, time, frame and calibration contracts explicit."]],"reflections":[["Explain how init, Node construction, subscription/timer callbacks, spin and cleanup cooperate in your smallest node.","init establishes ROS context; the Node owns interfaces; the executor dispatches callbacks during spin; cleanup destroys the node and shuts down the context.","Point to those operations in your code."],["Your message counter grows irregularly at startup. Explain why this need not prove message loss and what extra evidence you would collect.","Discovery/startup delays and report boundaries affect the count. Compare sequence/rate evidence, QoS and process load before concluding that samples were lost.","Provide a short sample of counts and measured topic rate."]],"body":"# Write your first Python nodes\n\nTurn the graph into code, one callback at a time.\n\n## Understand the workspace\n\nA **workspace** contains source packages and generated build, install and log directories. A **package** groups code, interfaces and metadata. An **executable** is a runnable entry point; a **node** participates in the graph. These are related concepts, not synonyms.\n\nThe reference workspace contains ros2_course_py, ros2_course_cpp, ros2_course_interfaces and ros2_course_bringup. Use it to catch up, but type this first experiment yourself. **By the end:** write timer and subscription callbacks, build a package and explain interface generation.\n\n## Write a complete publisher\n\nSave this as `~/ros2_ws/hello.py`. With Jazzy sourced, run `python3 ~/ros2_ws/hello.py`:\n\n```python\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\nclass Hello(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('hello')\n        self.publisher = self.create_publisher(String, 'chatter', 10)\n        self.count = 0\n        self.timer = self.create_timer(0.5, self.tick)\n\n    def tick(self):\n        msg = String()\n        msg.data = f'frame {self.count}'\n        self.publisher.publish(msg)\n        self.count += 1\n\ndef main():\n    rclpy.init()\n    node = Hello()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```\n\nNode gives the object a graph identity. A **callback** is a function ROS invokes when work is ready, such as a timer expiring or a message arriving. The **executor** chooses and runs ready callbacks while `spin` is active. The publisher specifies String and keep-last depth 10 using the default profile. The timer requests a callback roughly twice per second; scheduling can vary on an ordinary laptop.\n\nIn another terminal, `ros2 topic echo /chatter` should show frame 0, frame 1 and increasing counts. Keep callbacks short: a long sleep or expensive computation in a single-threaded callback delays other callbacks on that executor.\n\n## Install a package entry point\n\nCreate a scratch package. The package name comes before --dependencies so it cannot be consumed as a dependency:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/ros2_ws/src\nros2 pkg create my_camera_lab --build-type ament_python \\\n  --license Apache-2.0 --dependencies rclpy std_msgs\n```\n\nCopy hello.py into `my_camera_lab/my_camera_lab/hello.py`. Add `'hello = my_camera_lab.hello:main'` to the console_scripts list in setup.py. package.xml declares dependencies and setup.cfg places scripts where ros2 run expects them.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/ros2_ws\ncolcon build --symlink-install --packages-select my_camera_lab\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 run my_camera_lab hello\n```\n\nExpected: the same /hello node and /chatter stream. If the executable is absent, check the entry point, rebuild and source the overlay in a fresh terminal. Stop the scratch publisher before starting the reference talker.\n\n## Receive messages\n\nRun the supplied reference pair in separate sourced terminals:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py talker\n```\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py listener\n```\n\nThe listener should log the talker's strings. Resources includes both complete source files. `create_subscription(String, 'chatter', callback, 10)` connects incoming messages to a function accepting a message argument. Store the subscription on the node object so its ownership is clear.\n\nChange the listener to log `len(msg.data)`. Then remap both nodes using `--ros-args -r chatter:=camera_events`. Remapping only one node disconnects them. Stop duplicate publishers when measuring rates.\n\nLarge image processing needs deliberate design. Copying every frame through many callbacks can consume CPU and bandwidth. Measure your pipeline before choosing an optimization or a language.\n\n## Write the matching minimal subscriber\n\nSave as ~/ros2_ws/hear.py and run with Jazzy sourced while hello.py is publishing. This complete scaffold uses the same type, relative name and QoS depth as the publisher:\n\n```python\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\nclass Hear(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('hear')\n        self.sub = self.create_subscription(String, 'chatter', self.receive, 10)\n\n    def receive(self, msg):\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Received {len(msg.data)} characters: {msg.data}')\n\ndef main():\n    rclpy.init()\n    node = Hear()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```\n\nExpected: a line for each received frame string. The callback handles one message; spin lets the executor invoke it. Stop the scratch pair before starting another talker. In the package version, install Hear through a console_scripts entry point just as you did for Hello.\n\n## Give data a meaningful type\n\nString is convenient for a first lesson. Real interfaces should describe their data: Image for pixels, CameraInfo for calibration and Imu for inertial measurements. Our custom RobotStatus demonstrates generation:\n\n```text\nstring robot_name\nfloat32 battery_percent\nbool is_moving\nuint8 fault_code\n```\n\nros2_course_interfaces lists files in rosidl_generate_interfaces in CMakeLists.txt and declares generator/runtime dependencies in package.xml. Interfaces live in an ament_cmake package even when consumers are Python. Build before importing generated types:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/ros2_ws\ncolcon build --symlink-install --packages-up-to ros2_course_py\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 interface show ros2_course_interfaces/msg/RobotStatus\nros2 run ros2_course_py robot_status_publisher\n```\n\nIn another terminal, `ros2 topic echo /robot_status --once` should show the fields. This example simulates battery state; it does not measure a real battery. Document units, valid ranges and unavailable values. Use a Header when timestamp and coordinate frame matter.\n\n\n\n![Simulated installed talker and listener terminals. Compare their resolved topic, type and increasing message values.](../assets/pubsub-terminal.svg)\n\n## Practice and prove it\n\n**In class, 25 minutes:** write a subscriber that counts received messages and reports once per second. Increment a counter in its subscription callback and report it in a timer callback. At a 2 Hz input the count usually grows by about two per report after startup. Asynchronous interval boundaries mean it need not be exactly two every time.\n\n**Homework, 45 minutes:** add float32 temperature_c to RobotStatus, regenerate the interface, rebuild consumers and publish a simulated value. Submit the interface, changed publisher and one echoed message.\n\n**Checkpoint:** editing a .msg file does not generate an importable type. Build the interface package and dependent consumers, then source the overlay.\n\nSources: [Python publisher/subscriber](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-Client-Libraries/Writing-A-Simple-Py-Publisher-And-Subscriber.html), [custom interfaces](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-Client-Libraries/Custom-ROS2-Interfaces.html). Next: connect Python and C++ and diagnose QoS.\n"},{"id":"cpp","file":"course/lessons/03-lecture3-cpp-nodes.md","nav":"C++ & reliable data","title":"The same graph, in C++","tag":"LECTURE 03","minutes":90,"demo":"qos","outcome":"Connect C++ to Python and reason about QoS.","agenda":["Concepts · 15 min","C++ & CMake · 25 min","QoS · 20 min","Lab · 20 min","Checkpoint · 10 min"],"notes":["Prioritize communication contracts over a full C++ primer.","Ask learners to predict offered/requested reliability in both directions before changing a policy."],"quiz":[["A camera publisher offers best-effort reliability while a subscription requests reliable delivery. Other names and types match. What should you predict?",["A discovered topic may still deliver no samples to that incompatible subscription","The subscriber forces the camera publisher to become reliable","C++ subscribers bypass reliability compatibility"],0,"The offered reliability cannot meet the stronger request. Inspect verbose endpoint policies."],["A publisher offers reliable delivery and the subscriber requests best effort. With all other policies compatible, what is expected?",["The pair is incompatible because the labels differ","The reliability pair is compatible","Only Python can connect these policies"],1,"A reliable offer meets the weaker best-effort request. The compatibility relation is directional."],["A Python listener receives a C++ talker’s String messages. What actually makes this cross-language connection possible?",["The two source files have the same filename","They share a resolved topic, compatible type/QoS and transport configuration","The C++ program imports the Python class"],1,"ROS interfaces and middleware define the connection independently of source-language syntax."],["You change the C++ timer period from 500 ms to 200 ms but still observe the old rate. Which first action addresses the likely cause?",["Rebuild the changed package and run the updated installed executable","Change the image frame_id","Increase the subscriber’s queue depth until it changes the publisher timer"],0,"C++ edits do not alter an existing installed binary. Build and verify the running executable."],["A late-joining subscriber needs retained static transform information. Which policy should you inspect in addition to reliability?",["Editor indentation width","Durability and retained-history compatibility","The source language of the static publisher"],1,"Transient-local durability supports retained data for suitable late-joining subscriptions. Reliability alone does not describe retained history."]],"reflections":[["Explain why a larger queue does not solve an incompatible reliable-request/best-effort-offer pair.","Queue depth concerns retained samples; reliability compatibility concerns whether the publisher can meet the requested policy. Fix the policy contract, then consider buffering separately.","Use offered and requested terminology in your answer."],["Compare the same Python and C++ publisher without claiming that the simple result proves one language is faster.","Explain the common graph contract and identify confounders such as logging, compilation/build state, scheduler load and measurement overhead.","Report observed rate and at least two limitations."]],"body":"# The same graph, in C++\n\nChange the language. Keep the communication contract.\n\n## Translate the concepts\n\nrclpy and rclcpp are client libraries for the same ROS graph. A Python subscriber can receive a C++ publisher's messages when name, type and QoS agree. C++ is useful for native libraries and demanding computation; Python is convenient for orchestration and experiments. Language choice alone does not make a system fast or real time.\n\nReview classes, templates and smart pointers before class. If C++ is new, focus on mapping the familiar publisher, timer and callback concepts and use the complete reference scaffold. **By the end:** build a C++ node, connect it to Python and diagnose a QoS mismatch.\n\n## Read a complete publisher\n\nThe supplied talker.cpp is the annotated version of this example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <chrono>\n#include <functional>\n#include <memory>\n#include <string>\n#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\n#include \"std_msgs/msg/string.hpp\"\nusing namespace std::chrono_literals;\n\nclass Talker : public rclcpp::Node {\npublic:\n  Talker() : Node(\"cpp_talker\") {\n    pub_ = create_publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>(\"chatter\", 10);\n    timer_ = create_wall_timer(500ms, [this]() {\n      std_msgs::msg::String msg;\n      msg.data = \"frame \" + std::to_string(count_++);\n      pub_->publish(msg);\n    });\n  }\nprivate:\n  int count_ = 0;\n  rclcpp::Publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>::SharedPtr pub_;\n  rclcpp::TimerBase::SharedPtr timer_;\n};\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n  rclcpp::init(argc, argv);\n  rclcpp::spin(std::make_shared<Talker>());\n  rclcpp::shutdown();\n  return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThe template argument sets the message type at compile time. SharedPtr members retain the publisher and timer. The lambda captures this to access members. `create_wall_timer` runs on steady time, independently of the ROS replay clock. During bag replay, use the node’s ROS clock when comparing sensor timestamps; this keeps measurements and time queries on the same timeline.\n\n## Build and connect\n\nFor a talker, the essential CMake operations are:\n\n```cmake\nfind_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)\nfind_package(rclcpp REQUIRED)\nfind_package(std_msgs REQUIRED)\nadd_executable(talker src/talker.cpp)\nament_target_dependencies(talker rclcpp std_msgs)\ninstall(TARGETS talker DESTINATION lib/${PROJECT_NAME})\nament_package()\n```\n\nThis is an excerpt: the complete supplied CMakeLists.txt also defines the project, CMake version and other executables. package.xml declares dependencies for package discovery and dependency installation; CMake configures and links targets.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/ros2_ws\ncolcon build --packages-up-to ros2_course_cpp\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 run ros2_course_cpp talker\n```\n\nIn another terminal run `ros2 run ros2_course_py listener`. Expected: Python receives C++ strings. Reverse the pairing with the Python talker and C++ listener. Rebuild after a C++ edit; the old installed binary does not change automatically.\n\n## Make QoS compatible\n\nQuality of Service describes delivery and storage policies. **Reliable** requests retransmission when needed; it does not promise zero latency or delivery through every failure. **Best effort** permits loss, often favoring fresh sensor data over retries.\n\n| Publisher offers | Subscriber requests | Compatible? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Reliable | Reliable | Yes |\n| Reliable | Best effort | Yes |\n| Best effort | Reliable | No |\n| Best effort | Best effort | Yes |\n\nCompatibility is directional. Other policies matter too. Keep-last **depth** sets how many samples can be retained. When a consumer falls behind that finite history, older samples can be lost. **Durability** distinguishes volatile data from transient-local retained history. Late subscribers to retained map data or static transforms need suitable durability.\n\nFor sensors, inspect the publisher and often start with qos_profile_sensor_data in Python or rclcpp::SensorDataQoS() in C++. Inspect each driver’s offered policies before choosing the subscription profile.\n\n## Break delivery, then repair it\n\nTerminal A:\n\n```bash\nros2 topic pub /qos_demo std_msgs/msg/String \"{data: hello}\" \\\n  --rate 2 --qos-reliability best_effort\n```\n\nTerminal B:\n\n```bash\nros2 topic echo /qos_demo --qos-reliability reliable\n```\n\nExpected: no samples arrive; an incompatibility warning may appear. Stop echo, inspect the endpoints, then request best effort:\n\n```bash\nros2 topic info /qos_demo --verbose\nros2 topic echo /qos_demo --qos-reliability best_effort\n```\n\nExpected: samples arrive. A subscriber policy fixed this pair; renaming the topic would not help. Predict all four reliability combinations in the interactive matrix.\n\n\n\n![Simulated QoS checkpoint: inspect the best-effort offer, then select a compatible echo subscription.](../assets/qos-check.svg)\n\n## Practice and prove it\n\n**In class, 20 minutes:** connect C++ to Python, remap both endpoints and demonstrate the QoS failure. Submit verbose endpoint output and a one-sentence diagnosis.\n\n**Homework, 45 minutes:** change the C++ talker to 5 Hz, rebuild and compare ros2 topic hz for Python and C++ talkers one at a time. This tiny example is not a valid language benchmark. Explain confounders such as logging, scheduling and subscriber overhead.\n\n**Checkpoint:** reliable publisher to best-effort subscriber works. The reverse pair is incompatible. A queue depth is not a reliability policy.\n\nSources: [C++ tutorial](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-Client-Libraries/Writing-A-Simple-Cpp-Publisher-And-Subscriber.html), [QoS compatibility](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Concepts/Intermediate/About-Quality-of-Service-Settings.html). Next: services, actions, launch and TF.\n"},{"id":"systems","file":"course/lessons/04-lecture4-services-actions-tf.md","nav":"Services, launch & frames","title":"Connect a complete system","tag":"LECTURE 04","minutes":90,"demo":"tf","outcome":"Choose interactions and transform a measurement between frames.","agenda":["Services · 15 min","Actions · 15 min","Parameters & launch · 20 min","TF & time · 25 min","Checkpoint · 15 min"],"notes":["Demonstrate action cancellation with the supplied client and confirm the terminal result.","Use measured versus illustrative transform values explicitly. The phone Android device frame is not assumed to be the camera optical frame."],"quiz":[["A robot is asked to navigate for several minutes while reporting progress and accepting cancellation. Which ROS interaction best represents that job?",["An action with goal, feedback, cancellation handling and terminal result","A short blocking service call with no progress protocol","A parameter containing only the destination name"],0,"Actions explicitly represent long-running goal lifecycles. The server must still implement responsive cancellation."],["The service request completes quickly and returns an area. What additional assumption would be incorrect when reasoning about failures and retries?",["The response has an application-defined validity field","A service provides an application-level exactly-once transaction guarantee","The callback should avoid blocking unrelated work"],1,"A service request/response is not a database transaction guarantee; application failure/retry semantics still matter."],["A sensor is 0.1 m forward and 0.2 m above demo_base, yawed +90°. Its point (1,0,0) m is expressed in sensor coordinates. Where is it in demo_base?",["(1.1, 0, 0.2) m","(0.1, 1.0, 0.2) m","(0.1, -1.0, 0.2) m"],1,"Rotate the sensor’s positive x into base positive y, then add the mounting translation."],["A ROS optical-frame point uses x right, y down and z forward. What must happen before interpreting it in a standard body frame?",["Rename its header.frame_id and keep all coordinates unchanged","Apply the appropriate known transform, including its rotation","Negate all quaternion and translation fields without checking direction"],1,"A name change does not transform coordinates. Use the measured frame relation and the intended target/source direction."],["A local odometry estimate is continuous but drifts, while loop closure can correct global position abruptly. Which division of TF responsibilities fits that behavior?",["Global mapper owns map → odom; local estimator owns odom → base_link","A rigid camera mount owns both moving transforms","Publish odom → base_link as a static identity while the robot moves"],0,"REP 105 separates globally corrected positioning from continuous local odometry."],["A dynamic transform tree exists, but a lookup fails for a replayed image’s timestamp. Which evidence should you inspect next?",["Only whether the frame names use underscores","The requested time, buffer coverage and use_sim_time / replay clock configuration","The app theme used during capture"],1,"Geometry must be available at the requested time. Wall-clock versus bag-clock mismatch can break an otherwise valid tree."]],"reflections":[["Compute the demo point transform in words, then state which transform a lookup_transform(target, source, time) request returns.","Rotate (1,0,0) by +90° about z to (0,1,0), then add (0.1,0,0.2). The lookup maps source coordinates into target coordinates at the requested time.","Include units and frame names, not just three numbers."],["Explain why the phone bridge does not publish an identity TF from phone_android_device to phone_camera_optical_frame.","The Android device axes and transmitted-image optical axes need a measured geometric relationship. A guessed transform would falsely assert calibration; proper VIO needs a measured relation and timing model.","Describe one physical axis-check experiment and its limitation."]],"body":"# Connect a complete system\n\nChoose the right interaction. Give every measurement a place and a time.\n\n## Choose topics, services or actions\n\nUse a **topic** for a continuing stream, such as camera frames. Use a **service** for a short request and response, such as reading a setting or computing an area. Use an **action** for a longer goal that needs feedback and cancellation, such as navigating to a pose.\n\nA service client sends a request and waits for a response from a server. If a connection fails, retrying may repeat work, so operations such as saving a file need an application-level retry policy. Keep service callbacks short so other work can be processed. An action adds goal acceptance, feedback and a final status; its server also needs to check and respond to cancellation requests.\n\n**By the end:** call a custom service, send an action goal, configure nodes through parameters and launch, and explain a TF chain. This lecture is dense: the core path is CLI use plus the supplied files; implementing a new action server is an extension.\n\n## Call a service\n\nThe supplied ComputeRectangleArea.srv has request fields above the separator and response fields below:\n\n```text\nfloat64 width\nfloat64 height\n---\nfloat64 area\nbool valid\nstring message\n```\n\nStart the Python server:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py area_server\n```\n\nIn a second terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 service list -t\nros2 service call /compute_area \\\n  ros2_course_interfaces/srv/ComputeRectangleArea \\\n  \"{width: 3.0, height: 4.0}\"\nros2 run ros2_course_py area_client --ros-args -p width:=5.0 -p height:=2.5\n```\n\nExpected: valid true with area 12.0 for the CLI call and 12.5 for the client. Negative or non-finite dimensions should be rejected by the application. Run one server at a time: two servers advertising the same service make it unclear which implementation handled a request.\n\nRead area_client.py: call_async returns a Future, and an executor must keep processing work for the response to arrive. The standalone client can spin while waiting. Inside an already-spinning node callback, use an asynchronous continuation and suitable callback groups instead: blocking that callback can prevent its own response from being processed.\n\n## Follow a goal over time\n\nCountUntil.action separates goal, result and feedback with two separators:\n\n```text\nint32 target\nfloat64 period\n---\nint32 reached_number\n---\nint32 current_number\n```\n\nRun the server, then send a goal in another terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py count_until_server\n```\n\n```bash\nros2 action list -t\nros2 action send_goal /count_until \\\n  ros2_course_interfaces/action/CountUntil \\\n  \"{target: 8, period: 0.5}\" --feedback\n```\n\nExpected: feedback counts upward and the result reports reached_number 8. The server rejects nonpositive targets and invalid periods. Its teaching implementation uses a multithreaded executor and a reentrant callback group, so cancellation can be serviced while execution waits. It is not a real-time job scheduler.\n\nTo demonstrate cancellation, run `ros2 run ros2_course_py count_until_client --ros-args -p target:=20 -p period:=0.5 -p cancel_after:=2.0`. The client requests cancellation after two seconds and logs the terminal status. Cancellation is a protocol: Ctrl+C on an arbitrary CLI client does not by itself guarantee that the server cancels the goal.\n\n\n\n![Simulated service response and action feedback. An action exposes progress and a terminal goal state.](../assets/service-action.svg)\n\nFor a deeper source walkthrough, try [Annex D’s service and action experiments](../annexes/ros-workflows.md).\n\n## Configure and launch\n\nParameters configure a node. Declare them with defaults, validate values and make the runtime behavior match accepted updates. A parameter changing in the graph does not automatically rebuild a timer or reconfigure a sensor.\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py params_demo --ros-args -p rate_hz:=2.0\n```\n\nIn another terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 param list /params_demo\nros2 param set /params_demo message \"changed live\"\nros2 param set /params_demo rate_hz 5.0\nros2 topic hz /chatter\n```\n\nExpected: new messages use the new text and the output rate changes to about 5 Hz. The reference implementation validates positive finite rates and reads committed parameters and replaces its timer at the next existing timer tick. Stop this node before the next demonstration.\n\nLaunch files are repeatable system recipes. They configure nodes, remappings and parameters, but textual order alone does not guarantee a dependency is ready. For more complex systems use lifecycle management or explicit readiness checks.\n\n```bash\nros2 launch ros2_course_py pubsub_demo.launch.py \\\n  message:=\"camera pipeline ready\" rate:=5.0\n```\n\nExpected: my_talker and my_listener exchange messages on /lecture_chatter. A launch argument is an input to the launch description; a parameter belongs to a node. The launch file explicitly passes one into the other.\n\n## Give data a coordinate frame\n\nAn image has a timestamp and frame_id in its Header. TF2 answers how coordinate frames relate at a requested time. The standard mobile-robot chain is `map → odom → base_link → camera_link → camera_optical_frame`.\n\n**map → odom:** a mapping/localization estimate corrects global drift and can jump after loop closure. **odom → base_link:** local odometry is continuous but can drift. **base_link → sensor:** a calibrated rigid mount is static. Exactly one authority should publish each child frame's parent transform.\n\nREP 103 uses x forward, y left and z up for a body frame. Optical frames use x right, y down and z forward. A frame name alone does not rotate the data. Use measured geometry for physical sensors. A static transform describes a rigid mount; a moving sensor’s pose needs a changing estimate.\n\nTry a deliberately simple fixed-frame exercise. These values describe a teaching mount, not your hardware calibration:\n\n```bash\nros2 run tf2_ros static_transform_publisher \\\n  --x 0.1 --y 0.0 --z 0.2 --yaw 0 --pitch 0 --roll 0 \\\n  --frame-id base_link --child-frame-id camera_link\n```\n\nIn another terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo base_link camera_link\nros2 run tf2_tools view_frames\n```\n\nExpected: the translation is 0.1, 0, 0.2 metres. view_frames writes a graph file in the working directory. A lookup outside the buffer's time range can fail even when every frame name exists. Inspect timestamps, clock and transform availability together.\n\n\n\n![Frame ownership and a worked transform. This metric mobile-robot example is separate from the arbitrary-scale phone world.](../assets/tf-tree.svg)\n\n## Work through one point transform\n\nSuppose demo_sensor is translated by (0.1, 0, 0.2) m in demo_base and yawed +90° around z. Its point (1, 0, 0) m becomes (0, 1, 0) after rotation, then (0.1, 1.0, 0.2) after translation. This is different from changing frame_id: the numbers must change too.\n\nUse the interactive transform lab, then read the Frames appendix. A lookup_transform(target, source, time) returns the transform mapping source coordinates into target coordinates at that time. Optical axes, body axes and phone-device axes use separate conventions. The phone camera–IMU mounting becomes an edge in this tree only after its geometry and axis conventions have been measured.\n\n## Practice and prove it\n\n**In class, 25 minutes:** launch the pub/sub pair, update a validated parameter and produce the fixed TF above. Submit the launch command, measured rate and frame graph. Stop the teaching static transform before starting a real sensor driver that publishes the same child frame.\n\n**Homework, 60 minutes:** sketch the future camera system. Label topic types, clock source and the node responsible for each TF edge. Explain why a static odom → base_link would claim a moving rig is stationary.\n\n**Checkpoint:** a map correction may jump, while local odometry should remain continuous. That is why the global and local transforms are split.\n\nSources: [services](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-CLI-Tools/Understanding-ROS2-Services/Understanding-ROS2-Services.html), [actions](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Intermediate/Writing-an-Action-Server-Client/Py.html), [REP 103](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-0103.html), [REP 105](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-0105.html). Read the Frames, transforms & time appendix for worked point transforms, composition, quaternions and lookup direction. Next: connect phone sensors and qualified monocular SLAM.\n"},{"id":"slam","file":"course/lessons/05-lecture5-sensors-slam.md","nav":"Phone SLAM capstone","title":"A phone that helps build a map","tag":"LECTURE 05","minutes":180,"demo":"phone","outcome":"Pair a phone, inspect real ROS sensors and run qualified sparse monocular SLAM.","agenda":["Pipeline & time · 25 min","Pairing & topics · 30 min","Frames/calibration · 20 min","Break · 10 min","Mapping/recording · 50 min","Diagnosis · 25 min","Discussion · 20 min"],"notes":["TLS, hotspot reachability, per-mode calibration and backend compilation are pre-work. Supply a qualified reference recording and rehearse WSL USB/Wi-Fi routes where used.","Use a short textured scene and sideways translation for initialization. The monocular result is sparse and arbitrary-scale.","The native component topics are inspected; this adapter uses images. An unsupported phone should not block analysis of the reference run."],"quiz":[["A student installs the Android sensor APK and starts the TLS receiver on a laptop. Which pairing configuration identifies and authorizes that receiver?",["The reachable /ws/android WSS endpoint, exact server certificate SHA-256 and current session token","An insecure ws:// endpoint containing only the app filename","The APK download URL used as if it were the laptop receiver"],0,"The exact certificate pin identifies the TLS server, the token authorizes the session and the endpoint must be reachable over the chosen USB or Wi-Fi route."],["An Android camera reports REALTIME and motion samples arrive, but camera–IMU extrinsics and noise have not been measured. What can you conclude about fusion readiness?",["The data is automatically ready for tightly coupled metric VIO","The transport works, while estimator suitability and camera–IMU calibration remain unproven","The camera’s metric depth is now available from each individual image"],1,"A shared phone clock and arriving samples are useful evidence. Inertial estimation still requires measured geometry, timing validation, uncertainty and a compatible integration stage."],["The monocular ORB-SLAM3 viewer shows a sparse set of 3D landmarks after initialization. Which description is accurate for this course’s baseline?",["A dense, metrically scaled room mesh","A sparse reconstruction with arbitrary scale and initially unqualified world orientation","A saved navigation occupancy grid with guaranteed obstacle completeness"],1,"A calibrated monocular system estimates relative 3D structure and motion, while absolute scale remains unknown. Dense metric mapping needs additional information and processing."],["The mapper does not initialize while the phone only rotates in place. Which change best supplies missing geometric information?",["Translate gently sideways through a textured, overlapping scene","Raise the pairing token length to change map scale","Rename image_raw to image_rect without processing pixels"],0,"Translation supplies parallax for monocular triangulation. Pure rotation does not establish feature depth."],["After calibrating a fixed phone stream, the learner changes lens and image size. Which action is needed before trusting the mapping result?",["Keep using the old calibration because both lenses belong to one phone","Restore the calibrated mode or recalibrate and regenerate matching settings","Change only ROS_DOMAIN_ID to rescale the camera matrix"],1,"Intrinsics and image geometry depend on the actual lens/crop/resolution and processing mode."],["The ROS adapter publishes tracked_points and a bounded path while Pangolin shows the engine’s map. Which evidence should a report distinguish?",["The ROS cloud/path are diagnostics, while the engine viewer shows its optimized sparse map","Every received point is a permanently retained dense map vertex","The path is automatically reoptimized whenever any loop closes"],0,"The supplied adapter exposes current tracked landmarks and historical poses. It does not claim a complete optimized ROS map export."],["You replay a recorded phone run after stopping the live bridge. Which timing setup is appropriate for consumers of recorded sensor timestamps?",["Replay publishes /clock and relevant consumers use use_sim_time true","Live bridge and replay publish the same topics concurrently to improve sample rate","Use the laptop’s current wall time as if it were the original capture time"],0,"A single replay clock aligns consumers with recorded timestamps; competing live sources make interpretation ambiguous."]],"reflections":[["Explain the Android camera-to-viewer path and distinguish REALTIME camera timestamps, UNKNOWN callback fallback and the estimated phone-to-laptop clock offset.","Describe Camera2 image capture, JPEG/WSS, validation/clock conversion, ROS Image, measured settings and the monocular engine viewer. REALTIME shares the SensorEvent phone clock; UNKNOWN uses a labelled image callback estimate. The network clock offset remains an estimate.","Name the image and native accelerometer/gyroscope topics, identify the camera clock source and state which stream the mapper consumes."],["Report a short phone experiment without overstating its result. Include one failure, its evidence, your repair and the remaining limits.","Document phone/OS/APK checksum, camera ID/size/clock source, calibration, RTT/rates, commands and bag. Separate transport evidence from sparse monocular mapping; explain the remaining inertial-calibration work.","Attach a bag manifest, real screenshot and tracking-state evidence."]],"body":"# A phone that helps build a map\n\nTurn a phone into a sensor, inspect its measurements in ROS and watch a sparse reconstruction grow.\n\n## Understand the project and its result\n\nInstall the small native **Android sensor app** supplied in Resources. It reads a rear camera through Camera2 and motion sensors through SensorManager, sends packets over a secure WebSocket, and shows its capture mode. The laptop’s phone_bridge turns those packets into standard ROS messages. A separately prepared adapter passes calibrated images into **ORB-SLAM3**, whose viewer displays sparse 3D landmarks and camera keyframes.\n\n**SLAM** means simultaneous localization and mapping: estimate camera motion while building a representation of the scene. **Visual odometry** estimates motion locally. A SLAM system also maintains a map and may correct drift after recognizing a place it has visited before; this is called **loop closure**. A feature is a recognizable image location. Parallax is the change in its viewing direction as the camera moves sideways or forward.\n\nThe core mapper uses one camera. Correspondences between images and parallax support triangulation of scene points. The result has **arbitrary scale**: its relative structure can be useful even though its size in metres is unknown. The output is a sparse set of landmarks and keyframes. A dense mesh would describe surfaces much more completely; a navigation occupancy map would classify free and occupied space. Those are further processing goals.\n\nAn **IMU** measures acceleration and angular rate. The Android app preserves the individual accelerometer and gyroscope timestamps for inspection. The supplied mapper uses images; a camera–IMU fusion extension additionally needs measured intrinsics, extrinsics, timing, noise and a compatible integration pipeline. Annex C explains that path.\n\nBy the end, you should be able to pair a phone, display its image, inspect native motion topics, record a bag and explain a calibrated monocular mapping run. TLS setup, per-mode camera calibration and backend compilation are pre-class preparation. Use the native Android app for both connection options: wired USB or wireless over the phone hotspot. [Annex E](../annexes/android-connections.md) gives the complete setup for each.\n\n![Conceptual AI-generated equipment illustration: a phone supplies measurements, a checkerboard supports calibration and a laptop runs ROS.](../assets/phone-lab-illustration.png)\n\n## Follow a measurement through the system\n\n![Android sensor pipeline: camera and independent motion packets reach a laptop receiver over WSS using a hotspot or a USB route.](../assets/native-pipeline.svg)\n\nThe Android app uses Camera2 image timestamps when its camera reports **REALTIME**. Android motion events use SensorEvent timestamps. With that camera capability, both use the phone’s elapsed-realtime clock domain. Cameras reporting UNKNOWN use an explicitly labelled image callback estimate in this app. Check the source displayed on your actual phone; device support varies.\n\nThe app converts native unrotated YUV pixels into JPEG without rotating, mirroring or resizing them. The receiver decodes the JPEG to a bgr8 Image and publishes matching CameraInfo. The image Header gives time and frame; CameraInfo describes pixel projection and calibration. Zero camera intrinsics indicate that a measured calibration has not yet been loaded.\n\nThe phone is a sensor client. The **bridge** is the ROS node. Keep ROS discovery local to Ubuntu while one secure WebSocket carries the sensor data. Choose one connection option:\n\n| Connection | How it reaches the receiver | App endpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Option 1: wired USB** | ADB reverse forwards the phone port over a data cable | wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android |\n| **Option 2: wireless** | Laptop joins this phone's Wi-Fi hotspot | wss://LAPTOP_HOTSPOT_IP:8443/ws/android |\n\nFollow [Annex E](../annexes/android-connections.md) to set up your choice on native Ubuntu. [Annex F](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md) adds Windows/WSL forwarding. Both options use the same Android app, receiver, ROS topics and calibration. USB movement is limited by the cable. Wireless allows freer movement; nearby hotspots still share radio airtime.\n\n![Two Android connection options. Wired USB uses ADB reverse; wireless uses the laptop's phone-hotspot address. Both publish through the same ROS receiver.](../assets/android-connections.svg)\n\n## Pair the Android app and see real images\n\nIn a sourced Ubuntu terminal, run the updated receiver with the certificate and key prepared in Phone lab setup:\n\n```bash\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\nsource ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash\nunset ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY\nexport ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST\nexport ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42\nros2 run ros2_course_py phone_bridge \\\n  --cert ~/phone-lab/laptop.pem --key ~/phone-lab/laptop-key.pem\n```\n\nThe terminal prints a fresh token and **ANDROID SERVER CERTIFICATE SHA-256**. In the app, enter wss://LAPTOP_IP:8443/ws/android, that token and the certificate fingerprint. Use your laptop’s actual hotspot address. With the USB reverse route established, use wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android instead. Start capture, grant camera access if requested and tap Start again. Keep the app visible.\n\n![Simulated Android app: copy the endpoint, token and server fingerprint from the receiver, then inspect the camera clock source and counters.](../assets/android-screen.svg)\n\nThe certificate fingerprint identifies the intended TLS server. The token authorizes the session. The native app checks the exact certificate pin in its own connection, so it needs no phone-wide development CA installation. A renewed certificate requires its new fingerprint. Both USB and wireless use this same certificate pin and session token.\n\nIn a second sourced Ubuntu terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 node list\nros2 topic list -t\nros2 topic info /phone/camera/image_raw --verbose\nros2 topic hz /phone/camera/image_raw\nros2 topic echo /phone/diagnostics\nros2 run image_view image_view --ros-args -r image:=/phone/camera/image_raw\n```\n\nRun the long-lived inspection commands one at a time or in separate terminals. Expect /phone_bridge, your camera scene in image_view and a rate near the 10 fps send target under suitable conditions. The app preview uses the same native pixel orientation, so a portrait phone may show a landscape-oriented image. Record the actual dimensions and clock source. Missing motion sensors are reported; the camera exercise can still proceed.\n\n![Simulated image viewer and topic rate. Your camera scene and measured rate supply evidence of actual transport and decoding.](../assets/image-view.svg)\n\n## Inspect inertial samples and time\n\nThe app publishes accelerometer and gyroscope events independently. The receiver retains their separate timestamps in two topics:\n\n```bash\nros2 topic echo /phone/imu/accelerometer --once --qos-reliability best_effort\nros2 topic echo /phone/imu/gyroscope --once --qos-reliability best_effort\nros2 topic hz /phone/imu/accelerometer\n```\n\nAccelerometer values use m/s² and include the gravity-related response. Gyroscope values use rad/s. The frame phone_android_device uses Android device axes. Each ROS Imu message marks orientation and the other sensor component unavailable using covariance[0] = -1. Each available component retains its own event time. A future estimator that expects combined IMU samples needs an explicit alignment/interpolation stage.\n\nHold the phone still, then turn it about one axis. Observe the angular component that responds and document the sign. A stationary accelerometer magnitude is often near 9.8 m/s². These checks establish units and basic behavior. Camera–IMU extrinsics measure the physical relationship between camera and device axes; obtain them before adding that edge to TF.\n\n![IMU inspection checkpoint: compare rest values and one-axis turns with the documented device axes.](../assets/imu-check.svg)\n\nSeven request/reply exchanges estimate an offset from phone elapsed time to laptop ROS wall time. The app keeps the lowest-RTT estimate. Network asymmetry, drift and camera exposure/readout remain sources of error. REALTIME camera metadata establishes a shared phone timebase; validate the actual data before claiming suitability for inertial estimation. For live capture, use_sim_time stays false.\n\n![Clock estimate diagram. The same conversion maps Android sensor times to ROS; camera timestamp quality is recorded separately.](../assets/clock-timeline.svg)\n\n## Calibrate the transmitted camera mode\n\nCamera intrinsics describe focal lengths and principal point in pixels. Distortion coefficients describe the lens model. Calibrate the **Android app’s actual transmitted pixels** using the checkerboard workflow in Annex C. Record camera ID, image dimensions, orientation, focus mode and app version. Changing the acquisition app, camera ID, crop or geometry requires fresh calibration or an independently justified conversion.\n\nThe app requests video and optical stabilization off and uses continuous-video focus when available. Check that your chosen device behaves consistently; focus and vendor processing can still change the effective model. Hold one phone orientation throughout the calibrated run.\n\nSave the measured ROS YAML, then restart the bridge with it:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py phone_bridge \\\n  --cert ~/phone-lab/laptop.pem --key ~/phone-lab/laptop-key.pem \\\n  --calibration ~/phone-lab/camera.yaml\n```\n\nThe bridge checks dimensions and publishes matching CameraInfo. The supplied calibration_to_orb.py converts supported measured pinhole plumb_bob values into the optional mapper’s settings file. It changes file format; the calibration tool supplies the measurements.\n\n![Simulated checkerboard tool: vary target position, size and tilt, then fit, validate and save.](../assets/calibration-screen.svg)\n\n## Watch a sparse reconstruction grow\n\nUse the instructor-prepared optional phone_orb_slam3 workspace and the measured settings from Phone lab setup. Its engine, vocabulary and tested dependency versions are prepared separately from the beginner packages. With that workspace sourced, run:\n\n```bash\nros2 run phone_orb_slam3 phone_mono --ros-args \\\n  -p vocabulary:=$HOME/ORB_SLAM3/Vocabulary/ORBvoc.txt \\\n  -p settings:=$HOME/phone-lab/phone-orb.yaml \\\n  -p keyframe_output:=$HOME/phone-lab/keyframes.txt\n```\n\nChoose a well-lit, textured, mostly static scene. Translate gently sideways to provide parallax for initialization. Once tracking starts, move slowly with overlapping views and attempt a small loop. The Pangolin viewer shows landmarks and keyframes. Record tracking state and the motion that helped initialization; motion blur, a blank wall or moving people can make tracking fail.\n\nThe adapter publishes camera_pose, tracked_points, path and status under /phone/slam. In RViz set Fixed Frame to phone_slam_world and add PointCloud2 and Path displays for the corresponding topics. The ROS cloud contains currently tracked landmarks. The bounded ROS path is a diagnostic history whose old poses are not revised after loop closure. Inspect the engine viewer for its optimized sparse map.\n\n![Simulated RViz diagnostic display: currently tracked landmarks and a camera path in an arbitrary-scale world.](../assets/sparse-map.svg)\n\nThe reconstruction’s scale and gravity direction need an additional alignment step before its coordinates can be attached to a metric robot’s TF tree. The supplied adapter publishes visualization poses in its own world frame. Lecture 4’s map/odom/base_link chain explains how a metric robot organizes its geometry.\n\n## Record, replay and diagnose\n\nRecord a short, consented run with native component topics:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/phone-lab\nros2 bag record -o ~/phone-lab/walk01 \\\n  /phone/camera/image_raw /phone/camera/camera_info \\\n  /phone/imu/accelerometer /phone/imu/gyroscope /phone/diagnostics\n```\n\nStop the recorder cleanly with Ctrl+C. Stop live capture and the mapper. Start a fresh mapper with the same measured settings and append -p use_sim_time:=true to its --ros-args parameters. In another terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 bag info ~/phone-lab/walk01\nros2 bag play ~/phone-lab/walk01 --clock --rate 0.5\n```\n\nThe replay clock aligns timestamp consumers with the recording. Half-speed playback reduces processing pressure while preserving recorded sample times. Save a real viewer screenshot and inspect the keyframe trajectory written on normal shutdown. A trajectory is a list of camera poses; it is a different artifact from a complete serialized SLAM map.\n\n| Symptom | First check | Useful evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| APK cannot pair | Endpoint, exact certificate fingerprint and token | App error and receiver session state |\n| WSL localhost test fails | Running receiver, port ownership and localhost forwarding | Windows port test and Ubuntu server terminal |\n| App reports UNKNOWN camera clock | This device’s Camera2 timestamp capability | App display and diagnostics; keep camera-only scope |\n| Native motion topic is silent | Sensor availability and app state | Per-sensor counter and received rate |\n| Images arrive but initialization fails | Calibration, texture, translation and blur | Feature view and tracking state |\n| Lag grows during movement | Transport/processing throughput | Received rate, queue behavior and RTT |\n| TF query has no camera–IMU edge | Extrinsics have not been measured/published | Frame graph and sensor frame IDs |\n\n## Explain your result and choose an extension\n\nSuggested 180-minute session: pipeline/time 25 min; pairing/topics 30 min; frame/calibration review 20 min; break 10 min; mapping/recording 50 min; diagnosis 25 min; discussion 20 min. For five 90-minute meetings, assign the full mapping run as a follow-up lab. Use a qualified reference phone bag when a student device lacks the required capabilities.\n\nSubmit phone/OS/app checksum, camera ID/size/clock source, measured calibration, exact commands/backend versions, received-rate evidence, a bag checksum and one diagnosed fault. Include a real sparse-map screenshot and keyframe trajectory where mapping succeeds. Rubric: transport 25%; timing/frames/calibration reasoning 30%; reproducible recording/mapping evidence 25%; diagnosis and remaining limits 20%.\n\nFor a visual-inertial extension, qualify sensor timing, measure camera–IMU extrinsics and inertial noise/bias, define how independent sensor events feed the estimator and follow its initialization procedure. ORB-SLAM3 offers inertial modes, while this course adapter selects MONOCULAR. Read [Annex A](../annexes/android-development.md), [Annex C](../annexes/calibration.md) and the [WSL guide](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md) for the development and preparation details.\n\nOfficial references: [Camera2](https://developer.android.com/media/camera/camera2), [SensorEvent](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/hardware/SensorEvent), [ORB-SLAM3](https://github.com/UZ-SLAMLab/ORB_SLAM3), [OpenVINS calibration](https://docs.openvins.com/gs-calibration.html), and [WSL networking](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/networking).\n"}],"resources":[{"id":"cheatsheet","file":"course/annexes/cheatsheet-cli.md","title":"Command field guide","description":"Jazzy commands for the graph, frames and phone sensors.","body":"# Jazzy command field guide\n\nRun these commands in Ubuntu 24.04 Bash. UPPERCASE names are placeholders. Source ROS in every terminal; source the overlay after it has been built.\n\n## Environment and builds\n\n```bash\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\nsource ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash\nunset ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY\nexport ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST\nexport ROS_DOMAIN_ID=42\ncd ~/ros2_ws\nrosdep install --from-paths src --ignore-src -r -y\ncolcon build --symlink-install\ncolcon build --packages-up-to ros2_course_py\ncolcon test\ncolcon test-result --verbose\n```\n\nUse --packages-up-to when dependencies may need rebuilding too. Keep environment settings consistent across terminals. Localhost-only ROS does not prevent the separate HTTPS server from listening on the laptop’s hotspot interface.\n\n## Inspect the graph and its interfaces\n\n```bash\nros2 node list\nros2 node info /phone_bridge\nros2 topic list -t\nros2 topic info /phone/camera/image_raw --verbose\nros2 topic hz /phone/camera/image_raw\nros2 topic echo /phone/camera/camera_info --once\nros2 topic echo /phone/imu/accelerometer --once --qos-reliability best_effort\nros2 topic echo /phone/imu/gyroscope --once --qos-reliability best_effort\nros2 interface show sensor_msgs/msg/Image\nros2 interface show sensor_msgs/msg/Imu\nros2 run image_view image_view --ros-args -r image:=/phone/camera/image_raw\n```\n\nUse verbose endpoint information for QoS. Topic hz measures what the tool receives. Avoid dumping full pixel arrays; inspect headers or CameraInfo when debugging time and calibration.\n\n## Requests, goals and configuration\n\nStart the appropriate server in another terminal first:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py area_server\n```\n\n```bash\nros2 service call /compute_area ros2_course_interfaces/srv/ComputeRectangleArea \\\n  \"{width: 3.0, height: 4.0}\"\nros2 action list -t\nros2 action send_goal /count_until ros2_course_interfaces/action/CountUntil \\\n  \"{target: 8, period: 0.5}\" --feedback\nros2 param get /params_demo rate_hz\nros2 param set /params_demo rate_hz 5.0\nros2 launch ros2_course_py pubsub_demo.launch.py rate:=5.0\n```\n\nThe service, action and parameter examples are separate demonstrations with their own running nodes. The action client’s --ros-args -p cancel_after:=2.0 option requests cancellation; closing a client is not a general cancellation guarantee.\n\n## Phone capture and replay\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py phone_bridge \\\n  --cert ~/phone-lab/laptop.pem --key ~/phone-lab/laptop-key.pem \\\n  --calibration ~/phone-lab/camera.yaml\nros2 bag record -o ~/phone-lab/walk01 \\\n  /phone/camera/image_raw /phone/camera/camera_info \\\n  /phone/imu/accelerometer /phone/imu/gyroscope /phone/diagnostics\nros2 bag info ~/phone-lab/walk01\nros2 bag play ~/phone-lab/walk01 --clock --rate 0.5\n```\n\nThese are commands for different terminals/stages. Stop the live bridge before replay. Relevant timestamp consumers use use_sim_time true for playback; the live bridge uses wall time. Use a fresh mapper process when replaying a new experiment.\n\n## Frames and recovery\n\n```bash\nros2 run tf2_tools view_frames\nros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo demo_base demo_sensor\nros2 doctor --report\necho $ROS_DISTRO\nros2 daemon stop\nros2 daemon start\n```\n\nThe demo TF exists only after the lecture’s static publisher has started. The phone bridge leaves camera–IMU TF unspecified until that relationship is measured. Restarting the daemon may repair stale CLI discovery; it cannot repair a missing calibration or a genuinely incompatible QoS pair.\n"},{"id":"frames","file":"course/annexes/frames.md","title":"Frames, transforms & time","description":"Worked point transforms, optical axes and TF ownership.","body":"# Frames: where a measurement lives\n\nA coordinate frame is a named origin and three directed axes. A point's coordinates are meaningful only relative to that frame. A timestamp says when the measurement or transform applies. Names and timestamps are part of the data contract, not decorative labels.\n\n## Start with a physical picture\n\nImagine the phone upright and its camera looking at a mug. In a camera optical frame, x points right in the transmitted image, y down and z forward through the lens. A visible mug is usually at positive z. Its pixel location is not itself a 3D point: one image pixel identifies a viewing ray, and depth is still unknown.\n\nROS body frames conventionally use x forward, y left, z up. Android sensor axes are defined relative to the device’s natural orientation. The camera optical frame describes the transmitted pixels; its mounting and axis relationship to either motion frame must be measured. A screen rotating does not physically rotate the camera mounting, but it may change the image presentation. That is why the project keeps a fixed orientation and calibrates the exact image stream.\n\n\n\n![Transform ownership and a point calculation: a positive 90-degree yaw rotates sensor x toward base y.](../assets/tf-tree.svg)\n\n## Transform a point, step by step\n\nA rigid transform contains a rotation R and translation t. The notation **T_A_B** here maps coordinates expressed in B into A: p_A = R_A_B p_B + t_A_B. The translation is B's origin expressed in A, and the rotation maps B's axes into A. This convention must be stated; different libraries use different symbols.\n\nSuppose a range sensor is mounted 0.1 m forward and 0.2 m above a base, with parallel axes. A measured point p_sensor = (1, 0, 0) m becomes p_base = (1.1, 0, 0.2) m. Merely changing its frame_id to base_link would leave the numbers unchanged and create a false measurement.\n\nIf the sensor is also yawed +90° about the base's z axis, its positive x points along the base's positive y. The same point becomes (0.1, 1.0, 0.2) m. Rotation and translation do different jobs. Use the numeric transform lab in lecture 4 to try this example before calculating it by hand.\n\n## Compose and invert carefully\n\nFor a chain A → B → C, **T_A_C = T_A_B T_B_C**. Apply the rightmost transform first. Matrix order matters: rotating and then translating generally differs from translating and then rotating. With homogeneous coordinates, each rigid transform is a 4×4 matrix with R in the upper-left, t in the final column and [0,0,0,1] as the last row.\n\nFor the inverse, transpose R and calculate the translation as -R transpose times t. This follows from reversing both the rotation and translation. Quaternions encode rotation as x, y, z, w in ROS messages. A valid rotation quaternion must have unit norm. Identity is (0,0,0,1), not four zeros. Euler angles are convenient for small examples but require a stated axis/order convention and can have singularities.\n\n## Know what TF publishes\n\nIn TF notation, a parent frame and child frame identify a relative pose. The translation gives the child origin in parent coordinates. In a Python lookup, `lookup_transform(target, source, time)` returns the transform that maps source coordinates into target coordinates. This target/source order is a common beginner mistake.\n\nEach child should have one parent and one authoritative publisher at a time. TF is a tree, not a collection of contradictory estimates. A static transform describes a fixed calibrated mounting and is retained on /tf_static. A dynamic transform changes with time and is sent on /tf. CameraInfo describes projection and calibration; it does not create either TF topic.\n\n```bash\nros2 run tf2_ros static_transform_publisher \\\n  --x 0.1 --y 0.0 --z 0.2 --roll 0 --pitch 0 --yaw 1.57079632679 \\\n  --frame-id demo_base --child-frame-id demo_sensor\n```\n\nIn another sourced terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo demo_base demo_sensor\nros2 run tf2_tools view_frames\n```\n\nExpected: translation (0.1,0,0.2) and a rotation of about +90° around z. These are teaching values for demo frames, not your phone's camera–IMU calibration. Stop the demonstration publisher after the exercise.\n\n## Separate global and local motion\n\nFor a metric mobile robot, a common chain is map → odom → base_link → camera_link → camera_optical_frame. Local odometry supplies odom → base_link continuously but may drift. Global mapping/localization supplies map → odom to correct drift; that correction can jump after a loop closure. Calibration or robot description supplies the rigid sensor chain.\n\nThe phone project's monocular world is deliberately named phone_slam_world. Its scale and gravity direction are not known from the camera alone. Publishing its arbitrary coordinates into a metre-valued robot TF tree would imply a physical calibration that has not been established. The optional adapter therefore publishes stamped poses/clouds for visualization in its own visualization frame, preserving the distinction between estimated reconstruction and measured physical geometry.\n\n## Match time as well as geometry\n\nA correct frame tree can still fail at the measurement's time. TF keeps a finite history. Asking for data older than the buffer, later than available transforms, or using a wall-time stamp against replay time can cause extrapolation errors. A latest-transform lookup may be acceptable for inspection, but it is not a general substitute for transforming a measurement at its actual timestamp.\n\nFor Android, bridge stamps convert SensorEvent times and REALTIME camera times into the laptop clock; UNKNOWN camera sources use a labelled callback estimate. The phone-to-laptop offset is approximate. For bag replay, /clock and use_sim_time make consumers use recorded ROS time. Slower replay changes wall-time pacing, not the recorded geometry or timestamps. If your phone IMU/camera relation is unknown, a time fix alone does not establish extrinsic calibration.\n\n## Check your explanation\n\nExplain where the point (1,0,0) goes under the +90° demo transform; which node should own odom → base_link on a moving robot; why renaming frame_id is not a transform; and why a received IMU message does not prove that its axes match the phone camera. A good answer specifies frames, units, transform direction and time.\n\nSources: [REP 103](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-0103.html), [REP 105](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-0105.html), [Jazzy TF2 tutorials](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Intermediate/Tf2/Tf2-Main.html), [OpenVINS sensor calibration](https://docs.openvins.com/gs-calibration.html).\n"},{"id":"dataset","file":"course/annexes/dataset-guide.md","title":"Phone lab setup","description":"Hotspot, trusted TLS, calibration, backend and fallback preparation.","body":"# Phone lab setup: before the live class\n\nPrepare the Android APK, a secure connection, measured camera calibration and a qualified mapping backend before lecture 5. The phone is a sensor client. ROS and the mapper run on Ubuntu, either natively or inside WSL 2.\n\n## Choose a connection route\n\nChoose [Option 1: wired USB or Option 2: wireless](../annexes/android-connections.md). On native Ubuntu, USB uses adb reverse tcp:8443 tcp:8443 with wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android. Run ADB on the computer hosting the receiver. For wireless, connect the laptop to the phone’s hotspot and inspect its actual IPv4 address with ip -4 addr. Give each classroom hotspot a distinct name/password. A separate hotspot keeps each pair’s addressing separate; nearby hotspots still share radio airtime. Keep ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST and consistent ROS_DOMAIN_ID values in all Ubuntu terminals.\n\nFor WSL, start with [Annex F’s USB route](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md): Windows ADB forwards the phone’s socket to Windows localhost, and WSL’s normal localhost forwarding reaches the Ubuntu receiver. The same annex gives mirrored-network and scoped NAT-proxy routes for Wi-Fi. Rehearse the selected route before class and retain the firewall’s protections.\n\nInstall the classroom test APK from Resources on Android 8.0 or newer. Open it and inspect the form without starting sensors yet. The native app needs an endpoint, a fresh token and the receiver certificate SHA-256. Annex E gives the shared pairing checks and route-specific setup.\n\n## Create a receiver certificate and pair Android\n\nThe receiver uses TLS for every phone connection. For a short private lab, create a self-signed development certificate inside Ubuntu. The native app authenticates it by its exact fingerprint, so the phone’s global CA trust store stays unchanged.\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/phone-lab\ncd ~/phone-lab\nopenssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes \\\n  -keyout laptop-key.pem -out laptop.pem -days 30 \\\n  -subj '/CN=phone-lab' \\\n  -addext 'subjectAltName=DNS:localhost,IP:127.0.0.1'\nchmod 600 laptop-key.pem\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\nsource ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py phone_bridge --cert laptop.pem --key laptop-key.pem\n```\n\nKeep the private key on the laptop and outside published files. The terminal prints an Android endpoint template, a token and the certificate SHA-256. Enter the actual reachable address ending /ws/android, that token and the fingerprint in the APK. With USB reverse configured, the address is wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android. With Wi-Fi, use the laptop’s hotspot IPv4 address. The app checks the copied leaf pin and validity dates, identifying the server through that pin.\n\nTap Start, grant camera permission if requested and tap Start again. Pass condition: the app reports streaming, its camera mode/clock source is visible, and image_view on Ubuntu shows the phone’s actual scene. Inspect ROS rates and native motion counters. Stop and background the app to verify that capture ends. Repeat with a wrong pin or token to verify rejection, then restore the correct values.\n\n![Simulated native pairing screen. Compare the camera ID, size, timestamp source and counters with your own run.](../assets/android-screen.svg)\n\n## Calibrate the actual transmitted mode\n\nRecord the Android app's camera ID, native unrotated image dimensions, clock source and focus mode before collecting a target dataset. A calibration belongs to that exact pixel geometry. Switching USB and wireless keeps the same calibration when this camera mode is unchanged.\n\nFollow [Annex C](../annexes/calibration.md): measure the target’s square size and inner-corner count, collect varied sharp views, fit and inspect the result, save the ROS YAML and validate it on new views. The receiver supports the Save workflow; it exposes no set_camera_info service for Commit.\n\nRestart phone_bridge with --calibration ~/phone-lab/camera.yaml. Confirm CameraInfo dimensions and intrinsics, then convert supported measured pinhole plumb_bob values for the optional mapper from the unpacked backend/source folder:\n\n```bash\npython3 maintenance/tools/calibration_to_orb.py \\\n  ~/phone-lab/camera.yaml ~/phone-lab/phone-orb.yaml --fps 10\n```\n\nKeep the camera mode, orientation and processing stable for the mapping run. Validate focus/stabilization behavior on the selected phone. Native timestamp metadata is useful evidence, while camera–IMU calibration and estimator qualification remain additional work for an inertial extension.\n\n## Prepare the optional ORB-SLAM3 backend\n\n\nThis is instructor pre-work. The beginner workspace builds without ORB-SLAM3. A complete optional adapter is included under ros/slam/phone_orb_slam3, while ORB-SLAM3, its vocabulary and Pangolin are external dependencies. Read the [upstream build requirements](https://github.com/UZ-SLAMLab/ORB_SLAM3). Its published examples/build assumptions are not a promise that every Ubuntu 24.04 dependency combination compiles without adjustment.\n\nBuild a compatible Pangolin version and the upstream ORB-SLAM3 library on the reference teaching machine. Prefer a tested instructor image/container or documented pinned source revisions for class. Record the exact ORB-SLAM3, Pangolin, OpenCV, Eigen and compiler versions and any build patches. Prepare this stack on the instructor machine before class so lecture time can focus on ROS integration. The upstream engine and this optional adapter use GPL-3.0-or-later; they are separate from the Apache-2.0 beginner code. No third-party engine source/binary or vocabulary is bundled here.\n\nOnce `$HOME/ORB_SLAM3/lib/libORB_SLAM3.so` and the extracted Vocabulary/ORBvoc.txt exist, copy the supplied adapter into a separate workspace:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/phone_slam_ws/src\ncp -r ros/slam/phone_orb_slam3 ~/phone_slam_ws/src/\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\ncd ~/phone_slam_ws\nrosdep install --from-paths src --ignore-src -r -y\ncolcon build --cmake-args -DORB_SLAM3_ROOT=$HOME/ORB_SLAM3\nsource install/setup.bash\nros2 pkg executables phone_orb_slam3\n```\n\nExpected: phone_mono is installed. Start it with the vocabulary and measured ORB settings shown in lecture 5. It consumes camera images; native inertial topics remain inspection data. The adapter uses the inspected upstream Sophus-based API, but must be compiled and exercised against the instructor's exact engine revision before being called classroom-ready.\n\n## Prepare a reference run and fallback\n\nOn a qualified phone/app, pair, inspect rates, calibrate, initialize by translating through a textured scene, make a short loop and observe tracking. Confirm both the engine viewer and the ROS diagnostic outputs. Stop normally and inspect keyframes.txt. Save a real viewer screenshot, a consented ROS 2 bag and its measured calibration before class.\n\nDistribute a manifest containing phone model, OS/app version and APK checksum, camera ID/clock source, transmitted dimensions, client revision, FPS target, calibration/target details, bridge and backend revisions, input topic names, frame meanings, timestamp limitations, capture/replay commands and file SHA-256 checksums. The fallback bag must contain actual phone images and the matching calibration; a random stereo or ROS 1 bag is not interchangeable.\n\n```bash\nros2 bag info ~/phone-lab/walk01\nfind ~/phone-lab/walk01 -type f -exec sha256sum {} \\;\n```\n\nA recorded bag is not bundled. If the optional backend cannot be qualified before class, explicitly teach the working phone transport/ROS inspection exercise and show the recorded reference mapping demonstration. Record which stages were actually demonstrated, then schedule the remaining mapping qualification separately.\n\n## Acceptance checklist\n\n1. All learner machines use Jazzy / Ubuntu 24.04 and the four beginner packages build.\n2. The actual phone pairs over pinned TLS on the chosen Wi-Fi or USB route; its camera timestamp source is recorded.\n3. Pairing rejects a wrong certificate pin or token and the Android app releases sensors on Stop/backgrounding.\n4. Camera frames arrive; native accelerometer/gyroscope availability, individual timestamps, axes and units are documented.\n5. The Android camera ID/image mode matches measured calibration and its ORB settings.\n6. The optional backend is compiled at recorded versions, initializes and produces a real sparse-map viewer result on a qualified phone.\n7. The reference bag replays with matching calibration into a fresh backend process and a shared replay clock.\n8. The instructor has a screenshot and recording fallback and explains sparse monocular reconstruction and the additional work needed for visual-inertial estimation.\n"},{"id":"phone-app","file":"course/annexes/phone-app.md","title":"Android app & the receiver","description":"Camera and motion acquisition, native protocol and ROS output contracts.","body":"# How the Android app and ROS receiver work\n\nThe native Android APK sends camera and motion data to the laptop receiver using either wired USB or wireless. Both options publish the same ROS topics for inspection and monocular mapping. [Annex E](../annexes/android-connections.md) explains the connection setup.\n\n## Assign a job to each component\n\nThe phone client reads sensors, records their time/frame meaning, encodes small images and manages a bounded send queue. The laptop’s phone_bridge validates packets, maps phone times to ROS time, decodes JPEGs and publishes messages. The optional phone_mono adapter feeds images into the external ORB-SLAM3 engine. Website hosting distributes instructions and software; the receiver and mapper run on each laptop.\n\n![Native phone and laptop pipeline. Android is the sensor source; ROS runs on the laptop.](../assets/native-pipeline.svg)\n\n## Qualify the Android capture mode\n\nAndroid uses Camera2 and SensorManager. The app displays the camera ID, dimensions, focus mode and timestamp source. A REALTIME source shares its clock domain with SensorEvent; UNKNOWN uses a labelled image callback estimate. Android accelerometer and gyroscope events retain their independent timestamps. The app stops capture when it leaves the foreground.\n\nThe app sends directly to the configured laptop endpoint, keeps the token in memory and releases capture on Stop. USB carries the connection through ADB reverse; wireless reaches the laptop over the phone hotspot. Test your selected route on the actual device before class. See [Annex A](../annexes/android-development.md) for the native implementation.\n\n## Pair and establish time\n\nThe native endpoint is /ws/android with protocol version 2 and client android-camera2. The server accepts a native connection without a browser Origin header and checks its version and session token. The APK checks the exact TLS leaf certificate fingerprint copied from the receiver terminal, including certificate validity dates. This scopes trust to that one server connection.\n\nThe receiver reserves one paired-phone slot. Stop capture before changing from USB to wireless or pairing a different phone. Both routes use the same version-2 protocol.\n\nSeven sync request/reply exchanges estimate offset to laptop ROS wall time. A clock packet establishes the selected conversion before sensors are accepted. Each stream has its own increasing sequence and timestamp. The receiver rejects invalid sizes/numbers, malformed JPEGs, old/future timestamps and duplicate sequences. Its rate limit is 150 messages per second.\n\n![Clock conversion schematic. Capture-time quality depends on the client and camera metadata.](../assets/clock-timeline.svg)\n\n## Read the published sensor topics\n\n| Topic | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| /phone/camera/image_raw | Decoded bgr8 Image, with capture/callback time converted to ROS time |\n| /phone/camera/image_raw/compressed | The transmitted JPEG as CompressedImage |\n| /phone/camera/camera_info | CameraInfo from measured calibration, or zero intrinsics before calibration |\n| /phone/imu/accelerometer | Android acceleration component, m/s², individual SensorEvent timestamp |\n| /phone/imu/gyroscope | Android angular-rate component, rad/s, individual SensorEvent timestamp |\n| /phone/diagnostics | Session, camera metadata and clock-estimate information |\n\nAndroid component messages use phone_android_device. Each marks the missing component and orientation unavailable with covariance[0] = -1. Unknown covariance remains unknown. The image frame phone_camera_optical_frame describes the transmitted pixels: x right, y down, z forward. CameraInfo describes projection; measured relative geometry belongs in TF.\n\n## Interpret the mapper and its outputs\n\nThe supplied adapter uses only images in ORB-SLAM3 MONOCULAR mode. Pangolin shows the engine’s optimized sparse landmarks and keyframes. The ROS camera_pose, currently tracked_points and bounded path are diagnostic views in phone_slam_world, whose scale and gravity direction are not established. Normal shutdown exports a keyframe trajectory.\n\nA native source makes timing and capture choices more inspectable. An inertial estimator additionally needs stable intrinsics, camera–IMU extrinsics, time validation, noise/bias characterization and an adapter that handles the separate motion streams. Annex C describes that further project. The provided monocular exercise remains a practical way to learn the full sensor-to-ROS-to-mapping path.\n"},{"id":"android-development","file":"course/annexes/android-development.md","title":"Annex A · Build the Android app","description":"Install the APK, inspect Camera2 timing, preserve sensor timestamps and build your own version.","body":"# Annex A: develop the Android sensor app\n\nThe native Android app is the sensor client for this course, with wired USB and wireless connection options. Camera2 provides camera stream configuration and timestamp metadata; SensorManager supplies individually timestamped motion events. This gives us a clearer measurement contract for teaching and future research. Each phone still needs a practical qualification run.\n\n## One app, two connection options\n\n| Capability | Native Android app |\n| --- | --- |\n| Installation | Supplied classroom APK on Android 8.0+ |\n| Image acquisition | Camera2, fixed camera ID and native YUV size |\n| Camera time | Camera timestamp when REALTIME; labelled callback estimate for UNKNOWN |\n| Motion time | Individual SensorEvent timestamps for accelerometer and gyroscope |\n| Connection | Wired USB with ADB reverse, or wireless over the phone hotspot |\n| Receiver trust | Exact server certificate SHA-256 and a fresh session token |\n| Core mapping result | Sparse monocular reconstruction from camera images |\n\nThe native APIs improve access and observability. They do not by themselves solve lens calibration, camera–IMU mounting, timing validation, rolling shutter, noise or bias. The supplied mapper uses camera images. The IMU exercises prepare students to understand a later fusion project.\n\nOfficial background: [Camera2 overview](https://developer.android.com/media/camera/camera2), [camera timestamp source](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/hardware/camera2/CameraCharacteristics#SENSOR_INFO_TIMESTAMP_SOURCE), and [SensorEvent timestamp and axes](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/hardware/SensorEvent).\n\n## Install and pair the classroom APK\n\nDownload the [Android classroom test APK](../../downloads/nodes-to-maps-sensor-debug.apk) and [SHA-256 checksum](../../downloads/nodes-to-maps-sensor-debug.apk.sha256). On Android, open the APK and follow the device’s installation prompt. If Android requests permission to install from your browser or file manager, allow that source for the installation and turn the allowance off afterward. The supplied APK uses a classroom development signature. An instructor should rehearse it on the intended phones before distribution.\n\nStart the updated phone_bridge on Ubuntu. Its terminal prints a session token and **ANDROID SERVER CERTIFICATE SHA-256**. Enter three values in the app: the wss:// endpoint ending in /ws/android, the token and that 64-digit fingerprint. Tap **Start camera + sensors**. Grant camera permission if requested, then tap Start again. Sensor support is reported after connection. Keep this screen visible during the run.\n\n![Simulated Android app checkpoint: endpoint, pairing token, certificate fingerprint, camera clock source and independent sensor counters.](../assets/android-screen.svg)\n\nFor wireless, use the laptop’s IPv4 address on the phone hotspot. For wired USB, establish adb reverse and enter wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android. [Annex E](../annexes/android-connections.md) gives both options on native Ubuntu, with [Annex F](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md) covering WSL. Stop releases capture; switching away from the app also stops the session.\n\n## Read the small project\n\nThe [Android source ZIP](../../downloads/android-sensor-source.zip) contains an Android Studio project with three Java classes:\n\n| File | What to trace |\n| --- | --- |\n| MainActivity.java | Permission, connection state, seven clock exchanges, motion callbacks, counters and cleanup |\n| CameraFeed.java | Camera selection, YUV dimensions, timestamp source, capture request and JPEG conversion |\n| SensorSocket.java | Pinned TLS, WebSocket upgrade, bounded outgoing queue, masked client frames and closure |\n| AndroidManifest.xml | CAMERA/INTERNET permissions, minimum SDK and foreground activity |\n\nOpen the project in Android Studio with JDK 17 and Android SDK 35. The supplied Gradle configuration uses Android Gradle plugin 8.7.3 with Gradle 8.9. Use the included Gradle wrapper (`./gradlew :app:assembleDebug`, or gradlew.bat on Windows) to build the debug APK and inspect app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk. A direct Windows compiler script is also supplied in the source ZIP; its README lists the required tool directories. The [Android build guide](https://developer.android.com/build/building-cmdline) and [app signing guide](https://developer.android.com/studio/publish/app-signing) explain reproducible builds and release signing.\n\nFor your public course release, retain your own protected signing key so later APK versions can update the same application. Distribute the APK and its checksum. If your rebuilt APK uses a different signer, uninstall the classroom build before installing yours. Signing keys stay outside the downloadable source. A successful build and signature check establish package integrity; device tests establish camera and sensor behavior.\n\n## Follow a camera image\n\nCameraFeed selects a rear Camera2 ID, preferring a REALTIME timestamp source, then the largest supported even YUV dimensions with each edge at most 640 pixels. It displays those choices. ImageReader acquires the latest available frame. The app samples at a 10 fps send target and converts the plane buffers into NV21, respecting row stride and pixel stride, before JPEG encoding.\n\n```java\nlong sampleNs = realtime ? image.getTimestamp()\n                         : SystemClock.elapsedRealtimeNanos();\n```\n\nFor REALTIME cameras, the image timestamp is in the elapsed-realtime clock domain shared by Android sensor events. For UNKNOWN cameras, the app labels camera timing as a callback estimate and keeps the exercise camera-only. The timestamp-source field is visible in the app and receiver diagnostics. Image timestamps describe capture timing; exposure duration and rolling shutter still affect the scene represented by each frame.\n\nThe transmitted pixels retain their native sensor orientation: no rotation, mirroring or resize is applied. The preview shows those same pixels. A portrait phone can therefore show a landscape-oriented image. Calibrate the dimensions and axes visible in ROS. Stabilization is requested off; focus is continuous-video when supported. Validate the selected phone’s processing and focus behavior on fresh target views.\n\n## Preserve each inertial sample’s timestamp\n\nAccelerometer and gyroscope events arrive independently. The app sends each event as its own packet, using SensorEvent.timestamp. Accelerometer values use m/s²; gyroscope values already use rad/s. Android device axes are x toward the device’s right, y toward its top and z out of its screen in the device’s natural orientation. They stay tied to the device when the UI rotates; use the official sensor-axis diagrams when checking a particular device.\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"accel\",\"seq\":0,\"t_ms\":12345.0,\"values\":[0.0,0.0,9.8]}\n```\n\nThe receiver publishes /phone/imu/accelerometer and /phone/imu/gyroscope under phone_android_device. Each message marks the other measurement and orientation unavailable. There is no artificial combined sample with two different acquisition times hidden behind one header. A later fusion adapter must define its interpolation/alignment policy for an estimator expecting a combined IMU message.\n\nThe app requests events at 50 Hz per sensor and limits sends to that rate; actual rates depend on hardware and scheduling. A shared send budget limits bursts, and events delivered over 250 ms late are skipped. The bounded socket queue also drops work under pressure. These controls help preserve a fresh inspection stream within the receiver’s traffic limit. Compare observed topic rates and timestamps with the requested rates.\n\n## Authenticate and stop cleanly\n\nSensorSocket validates the TLS leaf certificate’s validity dates and its exact SHA-256 fingerprint. Copying that fingerprint from the intended laptop binds the app to that receiver. The app uses its own scoped pin check, so no phone-wide development CA installation is needed. A different or renewed certificate needs a new fingerprint. The session token then authorizes one phone connection.\n\nThe app uses protocol version 2 on /ws/android for both USB and wireless. The receiver permits one paired phone at a time. Stop capture before changing the connection route. The Android app stops capture in onPause, closes the socket and releases the screen-awake flag. Pairing fields remain only in process memory and are excluded from Android saved state and backup.\n\n## Test a change and prepare a classroom release\n\nFirst run the supplied transport and protocol tests. Then check a real phone: install, deny/grant camera permission, wrong/right certificate pin, wrong/right token, Start/Stop, backgrounding, reconnect, image dimensions, timestamp-source display, sensor rates and camera calibration. Record the phone/OS, app checksum, camera ID and backend versions with a short bag. Finish with the live/reference monocular mapping exercise.\n"},{"id":"bridge-development","file":"course/annexes/bridge-development.md","title":"Annex B · Build the ROS receiver","description":"Follow a WebSocket packet through validation, decoding, timestamps and ROS publication.","body":"# Annex B: build and understand the ROS receiver\n\nAllow 60–90 minutes after lectures 2 and 3. This tour follows the actual Python node in the workspace ZIP. A **ROS node** is a program participant in the ROS graph; it is separate from Node.js. This receiver uses Python, aiohttp, rclpy and OpenCV.\n\n## Locate the complete implementation\n\nIn Resources, download the workspace or use the source-file selector. Read ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/phone_protocol.py first, then phone_bridge.py. setup.py installs phone_bridge as a console executable. package.xml declares its ROS and Python dependencies. The receiver needs no phone webpage or static web assets.\n\n| Component | Job | Why it is separate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Session in phone_protocol.py | Validate packet fields, sequences and clock conversion | Can be tested without ROS or a phone |\n| create_app in phone_bridge.py | Handle native /ws/android WSS sessions over USB or wireless | Can use a fake publication sink in tests |\n| RosSink | Decode JPEG and publish ROS messages | Owns ROS-specific dependencies and publishers |\n| main | Parse options, create TLS context and spin ROS | Connects the server and ROS executor lifetimes |\n\n![Phone-to-laptop flow diagram. The WebSocket receiver is the boundary where web packets become ROS messages.](../assets/native-pipeline.svg)\n\n## Follow one packet across the boundary\n\nThe Android app checks the receiver certificate pin and sends a version-2 hello to /ws/android. The server checks its client type and token. Only one phone can be paired at a time. USB and wireless use the same endpoint path and version-2 protocol. The receiver replies ready, answers sync requests and accepts a clock conversion. Each sensor stream carries its own increasing sequence number and phone-relative timestamp. Session converts it into laptop time and rejects nonfinite values, stale times and invalid sizes before RosSink is called.\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"accel\",\"seq\":0,\"t_ms\":12345.0,\"values\":[0.0,0.0,9.8]}\n```\n\nThese are illustrative values, not a calibration or a packet to replay into an arbitrary live session. The timestamp must fit that session’s measured offset. The image, accelerometer and gyroscope sequences advance independently. Observe their validators in phone_protocol.py, then predict which check rejects a repeated sequence number.\n\nThe aiohttp event loop receives web traffic while a separate ROS executor thread processes ROS work. This keeps the transport logic out of a blocking subscription callback. A production system would also need a deployment-specific load and security review; the supplied server is scoped to paired classroom devices.\n\n## Decode pixels and publish a standard message\n\nFor an image, the sink base64-decodes the payload, uses cv2.imdecode, checks the real dimensions, then constructs a bgr8 Image. The following excerpt shows the central conversion; the complete code includes validation, timestamps, CameraInfo and compressed publication.\n\n```python\n# Concept excerpt inside a ROS node with a CvBridge and Image publisher.\npixels = cv2.imdecode(np.frombuffer(jpeg_bytes, dtype=np.uint8), cv2.IMREAD_COLOR)\nmsg = bridge.cv2_to_imgmsg(pixels, encoding='bgr8')\nmsg.header.stamp = stamp\nmsg.header.frame_id = 'phone_camera_optical_frame'\nimage_publisher.publish(msg)\n```\n\nThe image header records time and coordinate meaning. The matching CameraInfo header is copied from it. A measured ROS camera YAML supplies K, D, R and P; before calibration, zero intrinsics explicitly indicate an uncalibrated camera. These matrices describe projection, while TF describes relative frame geometry. Read the official [Image definition](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/p/sensor_msgs/msg/Image.html), [CameraInfo definition](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/p/sensor_msgs/msg/CameraInfo.html) and [cv_bridge source](https://github.com/ros-perception/vision_opencv) to understand the fields.\n\n## Publish IMU data with an honest contract\n\nRosSink publishes Android component events under phone_android_device. An accel packet creates an Imu on /phone/imu/accelerometer; a gyro packet creates one on /phone/imu/gyroscope. The other component is marked unavailable by setting its covariance[0] to -1. Orientation is also unavailable, so orientation_covariance[0] is -1 as specified by the [Imu message definition](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/p/sensor_msgs/msg/Imu.html). An all-zero covariance for an available component means unknown uncertainty. Estimate it from qualified data before configuring fusion. A future estimator adapter must define how independent native events are aligned into its expected input format.\n\nThe sink uses sensor-data QoS, which favors timely sensor delivery. Inspect offered policies with ros2 topic info --verbose and choose compatible consumers. The phone-to-laptop WebSocket is ordered and reliable; the ROS publication has a separate QoS contract. Changing the ROS queue cannot remove delay already accumulated in image encoding or network buffers.\n\n## Run and verify one stage at a time\n\nFollow Phone lab setup for the certificate and phone trust steps. In a sourced Ubuntu terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py phone_bridge \\\n  --cert ~/phone-lab/laptop.pem --key ~/phone-lab/laptop-key.pem\n```\n\nPair from the phone, then open another sourced terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 node info /phone_bridge\nros2 topic info /phone/camera/image_raw --verbose\nros2 topic hz /phone/camera/image_raw\nros2 topic echo /phone/camera/camera_info --once --qos-reliability best_effort\nros2 run image_view image_view --ros-args -r image:=/phone/camera/image_raw\n```\n\nExpect your scene in image_view, plausible dimensions, and increasing received counts. With no calibration, K starts with zero. After restarting with --calibration, compare the dimensions and matrix with the measured YAML. A small received rate compared with the send target suggests you should inspect image size, network load and processing before adding SLAM.\n\n![Simulated sensor pairing and inspection checkpoint. Use a real phone scene in image_view and confirm the bridge node in ROS.](../assets/android-screen.svg)\n\n\n\n![Simulated image_view and received topic rate. Compare your own camera scene, dimensions and observed rate.](../assets/image-view.svg)\n\n## Make one controlled change\n\nAs an exercise, add a diagnostic counter for accepted images and print a once-per-second summary so diagnostics stay readable at higher image rates. Explain where malformed packets are rejected and where decoded dimensions are checked. Keep sensor timestamps attached to the original samples when adding processing stages.\n\nUse maintenance/tests/test_phone.py for protocol and fake-sink integration checks, then rebuild and repeat the actual phone test. Passing protocol tests establishes packet behavior; the device run establishes camera decoding, native certificate pinning and ROS publication. For later extensions, consider [image_transport](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/p/image_transport/) for ROS image transport plugins and [ROS executors](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Concepts/Intermediate/About-Executors.html) for callback scheduling.\n"},{"id":"calibration","file":"course/annexes/calibration.md","title":"Annex C · Camera & IMU calibration","description":"Checkerboard workflow, sensor-axis checks and a path toward qualified visual-inertial data.","body":"# Annex C: camera and IMU calibration\n\nCalibration measures how sensor numbers relate to geometry, units and time. For the core capstone, complete the camera workflow and inspect the native motion components. Camera–IMU fusion is a further project requiring a sensor source with suitable timing and calibration support. Allow 45–90 minutes for a first camera session; qualify the phone before the live mapping lecture.\n\n## Separate four questions\n\n| Question | Quantity | Required for the supplied monocular mapper? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Where does an image pixel point? | Camera intrinsics and lens distortion | Yes |\n| How are the camera and IMU mounted? | Relative rotation and translation, called extrinsics | Needed for future inertial fusion |\n| When was each measurement taken? | Clock relation, exposure/sample time and temporal offset | Consistent image times now; qualified camera–IMU timing for fusion |\n| How uncertain are inertial values? | Bias, noise density and bias random walk | Needed for future inertial fusion |\n\nA visible checkerboard solves the first question. It cannot by itself supply inertial noise or guarantee simultaneous camera/IMU samples. The [OpenVINS calibration guide](https://docs.openvins.com/gs-calibration.html) explains how these quantities fit an estimator.\n\n## Prepare the exact transmitted camera mode\n\nFor Android, record the displayed rear camera ID, native YUV dimensions, sensor orientation, focus mode and camera clock source. The app sends unrotated JPEGs of those pixels and requests stabilization off. Hold one orientation and use the same mode for calibration and mapping. A calibration measures the transmitted stream’s pixel geometry, so changing camera or image processing requires a fresh measurement or validated conversion. USB and wireless transport preserve the same pixels and use the same calibration when the camera mode stays fixed.\n\nPrint a checkerboard on a flat rigid backing. Count **inner corners**, where four squares meet: a board with 9×7 squares has 8×6 inner corners. Measure a square edge on the final print, since printer scaling can change it. In this example each square is 24 mm, so the tool receives 0.024 metres. Use your own measured values.\n\n![Simulated calibration view: a 9-by-7 square target has 8-by-6 inner corners. Coverage bars represent varied position, size and tilt.](../assets/calibration-screen.svg)\n\n## Collect coverage, calibrate and save\n\nStart the uncalibrated bridge and stream the chosen camera mode. In another sourced terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 run camera_calibration cameracalibrator \\\n  --size 8x6 --square 0.024 --no-service-check \\\n  --ros-args -r image:=/phone/camera/image_raw -r camera:=/phone/camera\n```\n\nMove the target slowly through the image: center and edges, near and far, then tilted about different axes. Keep the whole board visible for useful samples. The X/Y/Size/Skew indicators help you collect varied views. Sharp, well-lit images help corners be detected accurately. If detection fails, inspect inner-corner counts, glare, blur and target flatness first.\n\nStart the bridge and confirm its image publisher before starting the calibrator. If the image stays blank, inspect `ros2 topic info /phone/camera/image_raw --verbose` and compare the publisher and subscriber QoS. The bridge offers BEST_EFFORT; a reliable subscription cannot receive its images. Restart the calibrator after the publisher is available so it can select compatible QoS. Warnings for unused /left and /right topics can occur in this monocular exercise.\n\nWhen coverage is sufficient, choose **Calibrate**, inspect the result and then **Save**. The standard tool normally writes /tmp/calibrationdata.tar.gz. List its contents, locate the monocular YAML and copy that file to ~/phone-lab/camera.yaml. Save works with this receiver; Commit would require a set_camera_info service that the supplied bridge does not expose.\n\n```bash\ntar -tzf /tmp/calibrationdata.tar.gz\nmkdir -p ~/phone-lab/calibration-export\ntar -xzf /tmp/calibrationdata.tar.gz -C ~/phone-lab/calibration-export\nfind ~/phone-lab/calibration-export -name '*.yaml'\n```\n\nUse the path printed by find for the following copy; replace EXPORTED_YAML with that path:\n\n```bash\ncp EXPORTED_YAML ~/phone-lab/camera.yaml\n```\n\nFollow the official [monocular calibration tutorial](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/p/camera_calibration/doc/tutorial_mono.html) for tool controls and [its Jazzy implementation](https://github.com/ros-perception/image_pipeline/tree/jazzy/camera_calibration) for version-specific options. Coverage is necessary, while a low reprojection error is only one diagnostic: evaluate the fit on fresh views too.\n\n## Interpret and validate the saved model\n\nThe pinhole matrix K is [fx, 0, cx; 0, fy, cy; 0, 0, 1]. Focal lengths fx/fy and principal point cx/cy are in pixels. D contains distortion coefficients; their number and meaning depend on distortion_model. R and P support rectification/projection conventions. Read the YAML’s image_width and image_height and compare them with the actual topic. CameraInfo documents each field.\n\nRestart the bridge with --calibration ~/phone-lab/camera.yaml. Inspect one CameraInfo message and a fresh live image. Check that dimensions match, K has positive focal lengths, and the optical axes refer to the transmitted image. Validate rectification on separate target views using the image_pipeline tools: straight target edges should become more consistent with the selected lens model. Repeat calibration if the fit or camera mode is unstable.\n\nFor the optional mapper, run the converter from the unpacked backend/source folder:\n\n```bash\npython3 maintenance/tools/calibration_to_orb.py \\\n  ~/phone-lab/camera.yaml ~/phone-lab/phone-orb.yaml --fps 10\n```\n\nThe converter supports measured pinhole plumb_bob models with four or five distortion coefficients. It translates file formats; it does not estimate new values. A different distortion model needs an appropriate converter and estimator model. Retain the original YAML, target measurements, actual frame rate, image dimensions and source revisions with the recording. See [OpenCV’s camera model](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/d0c/group__calib3d.html) for the projection equations.\n\n## Inspect the IMU before planning fusion\n\nHold the phone still, then rotate it slowly about one device axis at a time. Echo /phone/imu/gyroscope and record which angular component responds and with which sign. Echo /phone/imu/accelerometer separately to inspect acceleration. At rest the gyroscope should be close to zero, while the magnitude of acceleration including gravity is often near 9.8 m/s². Record the phone orientation and compare signs with Android’s device-axis definition. The camera optical axes describe the unrotated transmitted image and need a measured extrinsic relationship to the device axes.\n\n![Illustrative IMU inspection checklist: stationary values and one-axis rotations test units and signs, while physical camera–IMU calibration remains a separate measurement.](../assets/imu-check.svg)\n\nCollect a short stationary recording and plot all six components. Look for missing values, irregular intervals, saturation and obvious bias. A stationary mean is an observation under those conditions; the API’s sensor calibration/filtering and actual timing quality limit what can be inferred from it. Native events retain separate accelerometer/gyroscope times. REALTIME camera timestamps share their phone clock domain, while UNKNOWN camera devices use this app’s labelled image callback fallback. The bridge marks orientation unavailable and leaves unknown covariance explicit. Use the [Imu message contract](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/p/sensor_msgs/msg/Imu.html) when describing this data.\n\n## Plan a camera–IMU calibration project\n\nFor qualified inertial fusion, first choose a native/hardware sensor source with known image and IMU timestamps, a stable camera model and a supported estimator. Preserve the raw streams and metadata. Then follow a documented calibration pipeline such as the [Kalibr project’s camera–IMU procedure](https://github.com/ethz-asl/kalibr/wiki/camera-imu-calibration) and [IMU noise model](https://github.com/ethz-asl/kalibr/wiki/IMU-Noise-Model).\n\n1. Calibrate camera intrinsics in the acquisition mode and prepare the calibration target definition.\n2. Characterize inertial noise and bias using the pipeline’s specified stationary acquisition and analysis procedure. This commonly needs much longer data than a classroom motion demo.\n3. Capture joint camera/IMU data with the target visible and varied, smooth rotation and translation that excite all axes.\n4. Estimate the rigid camera–IMU transform and temporal offset using the chosen tool. Read its transform direction and time-offset sign convention before conversion.\n5. Inspect residuals, coverage and repeatability; validate the model on a different sequence before running visual-inertial estimation.\n\nKalibr has its own supported input/environment requirements. Plan ROS 2 bag conversion and the alignment of independently timestamped accelerometer/gyroscope streams explicitly, preserving original samples. Check REALTIME camera metadata, exposure/readout and residual timing on the actual phone. A constant-offset estimate cannot repair an unknown time-varying delay. The provided mapper keeps the core course focused on monocular images, while native component topics support this further calibration project.\n\n## Submit a reproducible calibration record\n\nInclude the target dimensions and measured square size, phone/app/camera ID/mode, transmitted image dimensions, original YAML, converted settings, coverage and validation evidence, and the changes that would invalidate this calibration. Explain what you checked about the IMU and which quantities remain unknown. A strong report separates measured quantities from assumptions and proposed future work.\n"},{"id":"workflows","file":"course/annexes/ros-workflows.md","title":"Annex D · More ROS workflows","description":"Guided service/action experiments, bag replay, TF, robot descriptions and next projects.","body":"# Annex D: explore more ROS workflows\n\nUse these short experiments after the corresponding lecture. Each starts with the supplied packages and asks for evidence. Open one sourced terminal per long-running node. The capstone combines these skills; they are equally useful for a mobile robot, manipulator or sensor station.\n\n## Services: inspect the contract, then implement a response\n\nStart area_server, then inspect and call its interface in a second terminal:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py area_server\n```\n\n```bash\nros2 interface show ros2_course_interfaces/srv/ComputeRectangleArea\nros2 service type /compute_area\nros2 service call /compute_area ros2_course_interfaces/srv/ComputeRectangleArea \\\n  \"{width: 3.0, height: 4.0}\"\n```\n\nExpect area 12.0, valid true and an explanatory message. Repeat with a negative width and explain the application’s rejection. Read area_server.py beside the .srv file: request fields arrive in the callback, response fields are assigned, and the callback returns that response. In area_client.py, trace call_async and the Future through which the result becomes available.\n\n**Extend:** add a separate perimeter service using a new .srv interface, build its generated types and consumers, then test a valid rectangle and an invalid dimension. Give it its own service name. Follow the official [Python service/client tutorial](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-Client-Libraries/Writing-A-Simple-Py-Service-And-Client.html) for a full implementation pattern.\n\n![Simulated service response and action feedback. The two interaction patterns expose different information to a client.](../assets/service-action.svg)\n\n## Actions: trace goal, feedback, cancellation and result\n\nStart count_until_server. In another terminal, inspect the action and send a goal:\n\n```bash\nros2 interface show ros2_course_interfaces/action/CountUntil\nros2 action info /count_until\nros2 action send_goal /count_until ros2_course_interfaces/action/CountUntil \\\n  \"{target: 8, period: 0.5}\" --feedback\n```\n\nObserve goal acceptance, increasing current_number feedback and reached_number 8 in the result. Next use the supplied cancellation client:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py count_until_client --ros-args \\\n  -p target:=20 -p period:=0.5 -p cancel_after:=2.0\n```\n\nRecord the final status and number reached; timing can vary. Read count_until_server.py: goal validation, cancel handling and execute are distinct responsibilities. Its reentrant callback group and multithreaded executor let cancellation be processed while the teaching execute callback waits. For a sensor project, a “capture N frames” action could report progress and respond to a cancel request. Build that as an extension after tracing the supplied server. The [official action tutorial](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Intermediate/Writing-an-Action-Server-Client/Py.html) explains the server/client lifecycle.\n\n## Bags: turn a live experiment into a repeatable input\n\nWith the phone streaming, record a short, consented scene. A ROS bag stores messages and their metadata, making later inspection and replay possible. MCAP is the usual default storage format in Jazzy; inspect the Storage id in your own output.\n\n```bash\nros2 bag record -o ~/phone-lab/walk01 \\\n  /phone/camera/image_raw /phone/camera/camera_info \\\n  /phone/imu/accelerometer /phone/imu/gyroscope /phone/diagnostics\n```\n\nStop the recorder with Ctrl+C so metadata is finalized. Run ros2 bag info and compare topic types, message counts and duration. Images may use substantial space, so start with a short run. Keep calibration files and a manifest beside the bag. Record physical TF topics too when your system has measured transforms that downstream consumers need.\n\n![Simulated bag inspection and replay checklist. Actual counts, duration and storage format come from your recording.](../assets/bag-terminal.svg)\n\nFor replay, stop live publishers and restart relevant consumers with use_sim_time:=true. For example, append -p use_sim_time:=true to the optional mapper’s --ros-args parameters in lecture 5, keeping the same vocabulary and camera settings. Start a fresh engine so its internal map belongs to this run, then play:\n\n```bash\nros2 bag play ~/phone-lab/walk01 --clock --rate 0.5\nros2 topic echo /clock --once\n```\n\nRun the echo in another terminal if play occupies yours. --clock publishes recorded ROS time; the rate option changes wall-time pacing. A slower run can reduce processing pressure while preserving recorded sensor timestamps. **Evidence:** compare tracking behavior on the same bag at normal and half speed and explain what changed. Follow the [official rosbag2 guide](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Beginner-CLI-Tools/Recording-And-Playing-Back-Data/Recording-And-Playing-Back-Data.html) and [rosbag2 project](https://github.com/ros2/rosbag2/tree/jazzy) for storage, filtering and QoS overrides.\n\n## TF: ask where a point was at its measurement time\n\nComplete the Frames appendix’s demo_base/demo_sensor experiment. Use tf2_echo to inspect the transform and view_frames to inspect the tree. Calculate the transformed point before comparing with the interactive lesson. Explain who owns each edge and whether it should change over time.\n\n![A metric mobile-robot frame chain and worked point transform. Phone monocular coordinates require a separate scale and alignment step.](../assets/tf-tree.svg)\n\nNext read the [TF2 Python listener tutorial](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Intermediate/Tf2/Writing-A-Tf2-Listener-Py.html). A buffer stores transform history; a listener fills it. lookup_transform(target, source, time) retrieves the mapping from source into target. For sensor processing, request the measurement timestamp and handle missing data or extrapolation. A latest lookup is useful for inspection, but a moving system’s current pose can differ from its pose when the sample was acquired.\n\n**Extend:** transform a PointStamped from demo_sensor into demo_base with tf2_geometry_msgs. Inspect its numeric coordinates and frame_id. This makes the distinction between relabeling a message and actually transforming it concrete.\n\n## Parameters and launch: make runs repeatable\n\nChange params_demo’s rate and text through ros2 param set, measure /chatter, then save the configuration:\n\n```bash\nros2 param dump /params_demo > ~/phone-lab/params-demo.yaml\n```\n\nStop the original node and restart with the saved configuration:\n\n```bash\nros2 run ros2_course_py params_demo --ros-args \\\n  --params-file ~/phone-lab/params-demo.yaml\n```\n\nCompare accepted parameter values with observed output behavior. Then inspect pubsub_demo.launch.py: its launch arguments are explicitly passed to node parameters and remappings. Add a launch argument for your own experiment so runs can be configured from one repeatable command. Use the [launch tutorials](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Intermediate/Launch/Launch-Main.html) and [parameter concepts](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Concepts/Basic/About-Parameters.html) to go further.\n\n## Choose the next project by its missing contract\n\n| Direction | What it adds | Official starting point |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Describe a robot | URDF links/joints and robot_state_publisher create a consistent frame model | [URDF tutorials](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials/Intermediate/URDF/URDF-Main.html) |\n| Start sensors in a controlled order | Lifecycle states make configure/activate/shutdown explicit | [Managed nodes design](https://design.ros2.org/articles/node_lifecycle.html) |\n| Fuse metric sensors | Estimated uncertainty, frame semantics and timestamp consistency | [robot_localization](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/p/robot_localization/) |\n| Navigate a mobile robot | Metric localization, costmaps, planning and control | [Nav2 first-time setup](https://docs.nav2.org/setup_guides/index.html) |\n| Simulate before hardware | A robot model and compatible ROS/Gazebo integration | [ROS/Gazebo compatibility](https://gazebosim.org/docs/latest/ros_installation/) |\n\nJazzy’s recommended modern Gazebo pairing is Harmonic. For each extension, write down the input topics, types, units, timestamps and transforms it expects. That habit turns a collection of working nodes into an understandable system.\n"},{"id":"android-connections","file":"course/annexes/android-connections.md","title":"Annex E · Android USB or wireless","description":"Two connection options for the same APK: wired USB with ADB reverse or wireless over the phone hotspot.","body":"# Annex E: connect Android by USB or wireless\n\nUse the same Android APK, ROS receiver and calibration for both options. The connection changes how packets reach the laptop; it does not change the camera mode, ROS topics or SLAM workflow. Start with USB for a desk exercise. Wireless gives the phone more freedom to move during mapping.\n\n![Two connection options: USB forwards the phone's localhost port to the laptop; wireless uses the laptop's address on the phone hotspot. Both reach the same ROS receiver.](../assets/android-connections.svg)\n\n## Choose one route\n\n| Connection detail | Option 1: wired USB | Option 2: wireless |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Physical connection | Data-capable USB cable | Laptop joins this phone's Wi-Fi hotspot |\n| App endpoint | wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android, after adb reverse | wss://LAPTOP_HOTSPOT_IP:8443/ws/android |\n| Extra setup | Platform Tools, USB debugging and computer authorization | Hotspot credentials, reachable laptop interface and receiver-port firewall rule if needed |\n| Movement | Limited by cable length; keep the laptop secure | Walk slowly within reliable Wi-Fi coverage |\n| WSL host | Run ADB in Windows; Windows localhost forwards into Ubuntu | Follow the mirrored/NAT details in Annex F |\n\nBoth routes use certificate pinning and a fresh session token. Wireless here means the app sends data over Wi-Fi; wireless ADB and USB tethering are unnecessary for these instructions.\n\n## Prepare the receiver once\n\nInstall the [Android APK](../../downloads/nodes-to-maps-sensor-debug.apk). Follow [Phone lab setup](../annexes/dataset-guide.md) to create the receiver certificate and start phone_bridge inside Ubuntu. Keep the certificate SHA-256 and session token visible in the terminal. A fresh receiver process creates a fresh token; a replacement certificate needs its new fingerprint.\n\nThe receiver listens on port 8443. On native Ubuntu, its default 0.0.0.0 binding supports either route. With WSL, use [Annex F](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md) for the Windows-to-Ubuntu boundary. ROS commands and mapping continue to run inside Ubuntu.\n\n## Option 1: wired USB on native Ubuntu\n\nInstall ADB and the Ubuntu device rules, then connect an unlocked Android phone with a data-capable cable:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install adb android-sdk-platform-tools-common\nadb devices\n```\n\nOn the phone, enable Developer options and USB debugging, then authorize your own laptop when prompted. Expect one serial followed by device. An unauthorized entry needs phone approval; an empty list suggests a cable, USB mode or Linux device-access issue. Follow Android's [hardware-device setup](https://developer.android.com/studio/run/device) for Linux group/rule requirements, then reconnect as needed.\n\nWith the receiver running on this Ubuntu laptop:\n\n```bash\nadb reverse tcp:8443 tcp:8443\nadb reverse --list\n```\n\nExpect a reverse rule containing tcp:8443 twice. If several devices are attached, select the intended one with adb -s SERIAL reverse tcp:8443 tcp:8443, replacing SERIAL with the value from adb devices.\n\nIn the app, enter wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android, the receiver fingerprint and token. ADB carries this phone-local connection over USB to the laptop's port 8443. Tap Start camera + sensors, grant camera permission if requested, and tap Start again. Keep the app visible.\n\nFor Windows with WSL, run these ADB commands in **Windows PowerShell** using the official [Platform Tools](https://developer.android.com/tools/releases/platform-tools). First verify Test-NetConnection -ComputerName 127.0.0.1 -Port 8443 succeeds while the receiver is running inside Ubuntu. The full sequence and troubleshooting are in [Annex F](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md). This route uses Windows ADB and requires no USB passthrough into WSL.\n\n## Option 2: wireless on a phone hotspot\n\nEnable the Android phone's Wi-Fi hotspot with a distinct classroom name and password. Connect only its paired laptop. Separate hotspots give each pair its own addressing, while all nearby devices still share radio airtime. Some phones restrict traffic back to connected clients; verify the actual phone-to-laptop route before the lesson.\n\nOn native Ubuntu, inspect the laptop's address on its connected Wi-Fi interface:\n\n```bash\nip -4 addr\n```\n\nUse that interface's IPv4 address, excluding the /prefix suffix. Enter wss://LAPTOP_HOTSPOT_IP:8443/ws/android in the app, replacing LAPTOP_HOTSPOT_IP with the actual address. Enter the same receiver fingerprint and its current token. The phone's own hotspot/gateway address and the laptop's 127.0.0.1 are different destinations.\n\nKeep the receiver listening on the reachable interface. If a firewall blocks the connection, allow TCP 8443 only from the phone's address on the intended lab interface. Keep the firewall enabled. On WSL, the Windows Wi-Fi address and Ubuntu's private NAT address may differ; follow Annex F's wireless forwarding and scoped firewall instructions.\n\nADB is used only for the USB option. A wireless mapping run needs the APK's Wi-Fi connection, not an ADB connection. Mobile internet is unnecessary after all course software and dependencies are available locally.\n\n## Verify the same result on either route\n\nIn a sourced Ubuntu terminal, run these inspections one at a time:\n\n```bash\nros2 topic hz /phone/camera/image_raw\nros2 topic echo /phone/diagnostics\nros2 run image_view image_view --ros-args -r image:=/phone/camera/image_raw\n```\n\nExpect increasing app counters, arriving ROS images and your actual camera scene. Inspect /phone/imu/accelerometer and /phone/imu/gyroscope separately. Record which sensors exist, image dimensions, camera clock source and observed rate. A port test proves reachability only; the image and topic checks establish that sensor data passes through the receiver.\n\nTo switch routes, stop capture, set up the new route, change the endpoint and restart. Keep the camera mode and calibration fixed. The certificate fingerprint stays the same while the receiver certificate stays the same; the token stays the same while that receiver process runs. Repeat the image/rate checks before mapping.\n\n## Finish the lab\n\nStop capture and confirm the camera is released. For USB, run adb reverse --remove tcp:8443 on the computer where the rule was created, then disconnect the cable and disable USB debugging when finished. For wireless, disconnect the hotspot and remove temporary receiver-port/proxy rules using Annex F where applicable. Keep private keys and non-consented recordings outside published files.\n\nOfficial references: [Android Debug Bridge](https://developer.android.com/tools/adb), [ADB reverse port forwarding](https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/layout/webapps/access-local-server), and [WSL networking](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/networking).\n"},{"id":"wsl","file":"course/annexes/wsl-capstone.md","title":"Annex F · Capstone on WSL 2","description":"Run Jazzy inside Ubuntu on Windows: USB, mirrored Wi-Fi, scoped NAT forwarding and graphics checks.","body":"# Annex F: run the phone capstone on WSL 2\n\n[Annex E](../annexes/android-connections.md) introduces the two capstone options: wired USB and wireless. This annex adds the Windows/Ubuntu forwarding steps for those same options.\n\nWindows hosts the laptop desktop and USB tools; Ubuntu 24.04 inside WSL 2 runs ROS 2 Jazzy, phone_bridge and the optional mapper. The Android phone sends to the same receiver used on native Ubuntu. Start with the USB route below, then choose a tested Wi-Fi route if you need more freedom to walk.\n\n## Prepare Ubuntu and graphics\n\nUse an up-to-date WSL 2 installation. In Windows PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\nwsl --update\nwsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04\nwsl -l -v\n```\n\nConfirm that Ubuntu-24.04 reports version 2. Complete the course setup and package build **inside Ubuntu**. Keep ~/ros2_ws and ~/phone_slam_ws in the Linux home directory. Test rviz2 before adding phone data. With current Windows graphics drivers, WSLg can display Linux GUI applications; follow Microsoft’s [WSLg instructions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/tutorials/gui-apps).\n\nThe optional ORB-SLAM3 engine and Pangolin viewer need the same build and calibration rehearsal described in Phone lab setup. Test their OpenGL support separately. If RViz has a driver issue, `LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 rviz2` can help diagnose it; software rendering may be too slow for the live mapping workload. A recorded bag is useful for comparing transport and processing problems.\n\n## USB route: Windows ADB to the WSL receiver\n\nThis route uses Windows’ normal localhost forwarding into WSL. The phone remains attached to Windows ADB. ROS stays in Ubuntu, so USB passthrough into WSL is unnecessary for this setup. Use a data-capable cable and the official [Android Platform Tools](https://developer.android.com/tools/releases/platform-tools).\n\n1. Install the classroom APK on Android. Enable Developer options and USB debugging for this lab, connect the cable and authorize your own computer on the phone.\n2. In Ubuntu, source Jazzy and the course overlay, then start phone_bridge with the certificate and key prepared in Phone lab setup. Its default host is 0.0.0.0, allowing WSL’s forwarding path to reach it.\n3. In Windows PowerShell, verify that the receiver port is reachable locally:\n\n```powershell\nTest-NetConnection -ComputerName 127.0.0.1 -Port 8443\nadb devices\nadb reverse tcp:8443 tcp:8443\nadb reverse --list\n```\n\nExpect TcpTestSucceeded True, one authorized device and a reverse rule for tcp:8443. Run these adb commands from Windows, using the Platform Tools executable. If there are several devices, choose the intended serial with `adb -s SERIAL reverse tcp:8443 tcp:8443`. A phone listed as unauthorized needs its on-device approval.\n\nIf PowerShell reports that adb is not recognized, download and extract the official Platform Tools linked above. Open PowerShell in the extracted platform-tools directory and use `./adb.exe devices`, `./adb.exe reverse tcp:8443 tcp:8443` and `./adb.exe reverse --list`, or add that directory to your user PATH and open a fresh terminal. Installing the Android APK or Ubuntu does not install Windows ADB. Without a connected, authorized phone, stop at the receiver/localhost check and record the device steps as untested.\n\n4. In the Android app, enter **wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android**, the receiver token and certificate SHA-256 printed inside Ubuntu. Tap Start. The reverse rule carries the phone’s localhost socket through USB to Windows localhost, which forwards to the running WSL server.\n5. In another sourced Ubuntu terminal, inspect the topics and open image_view. Confirm real camera pixels and native sensor messages before starting SLAM.\n\n![WSL USB route diagram: Android localhost passes through adb reverse to Windows localhost and then to the ROS receiver in Ubuntu.](../assets/wsl-route.svg)\n\nIf Windows cannot reach localhost:8443, check that the Ubuntu receiver is running, no Windows process already owns that port, and WSL’s localhost forwarding is enabled. An explicit `localhostForwarding=true` belongs under [wsl2] in %UserProfile%\\.wslconfig for NAT networking. Preserve any other settings. Configuration changes require a WSL restart; save and stop other WSL work before `wsl --shutdown`, then start Ubuntu and the receiver again.\n\nUSB is a good first connection test and a compact desk experiment. Plan walking motion around the cable’s length and trip risk. For a larger room loop, use a rehearsed Wi-Fi route. Stop capture at the end, remove the reverse rule with `adb reverse --remove tcp:8443`, and revoke USB debugging authorization if this was a temporary lab computer. See the official [ADB reverse example](https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/layout/webapps/access-local-server) for the port-forwarding mechanism.\n\n## Wi-Fi route 1: mirrored networking on Windows 11\n\nMicrosoft supports mirrored networking on Windows 11 22H2 and newer. It can make a WSL server directly reachable from the LAN. Follow the current [WSL networking guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/networking), especially the Hyper-V firewall section. In %UserProfile%\\.wslconfig, add or update this setting while preserving the file’s other values:\n\n```ini\n[wsl2]\nnetworkingMode=mirrored\n```\n\nSave and stop WSL work before restarting WSL. Connect Windows to the phone hotspot and identify Windows’ Wi-Fi IPv4 address with Get-NetIPConfiguration. Start phone_bridge inside Ubuntu, then allow TCP 8443 through the applicable Windows/Hyper-V firewall policy. A local administrator can create a Hyper-V rule limited to this port:\n\n```powershell\nNew-NetFirewallHyperVRule -Name \"RosPhone8443\" -DisplayName \"ROS phone lab 8443\" `\n  -Direction Inbound -VMCreatorId '{40E0AC32-46A5-438A-A0B2-2B479E8F2E90}' `\n  -Protocol TCP -LocalPorts 8443\n```\n\nScope applicable Windows host rules to the phone address and lab interface as shown in the NAT example below. Managed computers may require IT to approve firewall rules. Keep the firewall enabled. In Android, use wss://WINDOWS_HOTSPOT_IP:8443/ws/android and the printed certificate pin/token. A successful phone pairing establishes the actual route; Windows localhost alone checks only the host side. Remove the temporary Hyper-V rule afterward with `Remove-NetFirewallHyperVRule -Name \"RosPhone8443\"`.\n\n## Wi-Fi route 2: NAT with an explicit TCP proxy\n\nUse this route when WSL uses NAT and the phone cannot reach it directly. First identify three actual IPv4 addresses:\n\n| Placeholder | Where to find it |\n| --- | --- |\n| WINDOWS_HOTSPOT_IP | Get-NetIPConfiguration: Windows Wi-Fi interface connected to the phone hotspot |\n| WSL_IP | `wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 hostname -I`: select Ubuntu’s current IPv4 address |\n| PHONE_IP | The hotspot gateway shown by Windows, checked against the phone’s hotspot settings |\n\nThe phone connects to WINDOWS_HOTSPOT_IP. Windows forwards that TCP connection to WSL_IP. The proxy preserves TLS end to end. Replace the placeholders before running these **administrator PowerShell** commands:\n\n```powershell\n$courseWindowsIP = 'WINDOWS_HOTSPOT_IP'\n$courseWslIP = 'WSL_IP'\n$coursePhoneIP = 'PHONE_IP'\nnetsh interface portproxy add v4tov4 listenaddress=$courseWindowsIP listenport=8443 connectaddress=$courseWslIP connectport=8443\nNew-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'ROS phone lab NAT 8443' -Direction Inbound `\n  -Action Allow -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 8443 -LocalAddress $courseWindowsIP `\n  -RemoteAddress $coursePhoneIP\nnetsh interface portproxy show v4tov4\n```\n\nKeep phone_bridge running on 0.0.0.0 inside Ubuntu. In Android use wss://WINDOWS_HOTSPOT_IP:8443/ws/android with the current pin and token. WSL’s private IP can change after restart; inspect it and update the proxy before reconnecting. A firewall/proxy setup is specific to this machine and lab network.\n\nAfter the lab, use the same Windows IP value to remove the temporary route and rule:\n\n```powershell\nnetsh interface portproxy delete v4tov4 listenaddress=$courseWindowsIP listenport=8443\nRemove-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'ROS phone lab NAT 8443'\n```\n\n## Complete the same ROS and mapping exercise\n\nOnce connected, the ROS commands, camera calibration, monocular backend and bag workflow are identical to the native-Ubuntu lesson. Keep ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST and ROS_DOMAIN_ID consistent across Ubuntu terminals. The phone communicates with a TCP receiver; it does not need ROS discovery across the Windows/WSL boundary.\n\nCheck /phone/camera/image_raw, /phone/camera/camera_info, /phone/imu/accelerometer and /phone/imu/gyroscope. Calibrate the Android app’s actual image mode. Run the separately prepared mapper and set RViz Fixed Frame to phone_slam_world. For replay, stop live sources and use /clock with use_sim_time on consumers.\n\nRecord which route worked, all relevant software versions, the app's camera ID/clock source and topic rates. Test the complete route on classroom machines, including any VPN or institutional firewall, before relying on it during a lecture. Windows localhost reachability checks only the host-to-receiver segment; confirm actual phone images and sensor messages before starting mapping.\n"},{"id":"visual-guide","file":"course/annexes/visual-guide.md","title":"Visual checkpoints","description":"A gallery of labelled examples: compare your terminal, graph, calibration and map views.","body":"# Visual checkpoints: what should I see?\n\nUse this gallery when you are unsure what a command or tool is meant to reveal. Each software view is a **simulated teaching example**, with illustrative values and simplified layout. Compare the named fields and relationships with your own output. Open a picture for full resolution. The figures also appear next to the corresponding course steps.\n\n## Setup and the graph\n\nCheck ROS_DISTRO and Ubuntu codename first. After the workspace builds, verify that executables are installed and visible in the current sourced terminal.\n\n![Simulated setup terminal. Jazzy, noble, four packages and a visible executable list are the checkpoints.](../assets/setup-terminal.svg)\n\nDrive the turtle with the teleop terminal focused. A moving turtle shows behavior; the graph explains the publisher, topic and subscriber behind it.\n\n![Simulated turtlesim and command graph. The typed cmd_vel stream links the keyboard controller to the simulator.](../assets/turtle-graph.svg)\n\n## Programs and interaction patterns\n\nKeep the publisher and listener running in separate terminals. Increasing message values and a shared topic show data flow. The logger prefix identifies the node that printed the line.\n\n![Simulated pub/sub terminals. One installed talker publishes strings while the listener receives them.](../assets/pubsub-terminal.svg)\n\n![Simulated QoS diagnosis. A best-effort publisher needs a subscriber whose reliability request it can meet.](../assets/qos-check.svg)\n\n![Simulated service and action outputs. Compare a single response with feedback and a final goal result.](../assets/service-action.svg)\n\n## Frames and sensor transport\n\nThe metric mobile-robot chain teaches transform ownership. The phone’s monocular world is a separate arbitrary-scale visualization frame.\n\n![Frame ownership diagram and worked transform. Rotation followed by translation changes the point coordinates.](../assets/tf-tree.svg)\n\n![Native phone pipeline. Camera2 and independently timestamped motion events reach the laptop receiver.](../assets/native-pipeline.svg)\n\n![Simulated Android pairing checkpoint. Inspect the camera mode, clock source and separate component counters.](../assets/android-screen.svg)\n\n\n\n![Simulated image_view and received topic rate. Compare your own camera scene, dimensions and observed rate.](../assets/image-view.svg)\n\n## Calibration, timing and mapping\n\nUse measured target dimensions and varied camera views. Timing exchanges estimate the phone-to-laptop clock relation. Inspect the native camera’s timestamp source and measure camera–IMU alignment separately.\n\n![Simulated checkerboard tool. Track horizontal, vertical, size and skew coverage before fitting and saving.](../assets/calibration-screen.svg)\n\n![IMU inspection guide. Test rest values and single-axis turns before planning sensor fusion.](../assets/imu-check.svg)\n\n![Clock conversion timeline. Inspect capture timing separately from the estimated phone-to-laptop clock relation.](../assets/clock-timeline.svg)\n\n![Simulated sparse-map display. Sparse landmarks and a diagnostic path provide structure with arbitrary scale.](../assets/sparse-map.svg)\n\n## USB and wireless connection routes\n\n![Android connection options. ADB reverse carries USB packets; the phone hotspot carries wireless packets to the same receiver.](../assets/android-connections.svg)\n\n### WSL connection route\n\n![WSL USB route. Android localhost reaches the receiver inside Ubuntu through Windows ADB and localhost forwarding.](../assets/wsl-route.svg)\n\n## Recording and reproducibility\n\nInspect the recording’s actual topic counts, storage format and duration. Stop live publishers, then use the replay clock consistently in consumers.\n\n![Simulated bag information and playback. Use matching camera settings and a fresh mapper process for the recorded run.](../assets/bag-terminal.svg)\n\nFor a lab report, replace these examples with your own screenshots and the commands that produced them. Record what a viewer establishes: transport, image quality, tracking state and reconstruction are distinct observations.\n"},{"id":"troubleshooting","file":"course/annexes/troubleshooting.md","title":"When something goes quiet","description":"Find the first broken contract in the network or ROS graph.","body":"# Find the first broken contract\n\nChange one thing, collect evidence and retest. Separate Android permissions, USB/wireless transport, ROS messages and estimator assumptions.\n\n## Installation and terminal setup issues\n\nOn a fresh Ubuntu WSL account, sudo normally asks for that account's Linux password. Type it locally when prompted; no characters are displayed. Build the learner workspace as your normal Linux user.\n\nThe ROS apt repository must be configured before apt can find ros-jazzy packages. Follow the official Jazzy repository instructions linked from setup, including Universe and ros2-apt-source, then run apt update.\n\nFor persistent ROS sourcing and DDS variables, use the labelled .bashrc block in Before you begin. Verify it from a fresh interactive Ubuntu Bash terminal. A noninteractive `bash -c` may skip the interactive portion of .bashrc; automation should source ROS explicitly. Existing terminals still need the overlay sourced after the first build.\n\n## ROS or an executable is missing\n\nConfirm Ubuntu 24.04 and source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash. Build from ~/ros2_ws and source install/setup.bash in the terminal that runs the command. Check the first build/rosdep error. A Python script running directly does not prove its console_scripts entry point was installed.\n\nIf phone_bridge cannot import aiohttp, yaml, cv_bridge or OpenCV, install the declared Ubuntu dependencies and use the system Python matching Jazzy. A random pip virtual environment may hide ROS Python packages. The optional SLAM package lives in a separate workspace and requires the externally built engine.\n\nIf colcon warns that ros2_course_interfaces is already built in an underlay while rebuilding the same workspace, the course overlay is already sourced in the build shell. Sourcing /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash again does not clear the inherited overlay. For a clean build shell, run this from Ubuntu, then source only the underlay and build:\n\n```bash\nenv -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$USER\" PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin bash --noprofile --norc\nsource /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash\ncd ~/ros2_ws\ncolcon build --symlink-install\nsource install/setup.bash\n```\n\nThe first command opens a nested clean shell. Type exit when finished, then open a normal terminal for node exercises. This shell is for compilation; it intentionally lacks GUI and lab discovery variables. Do not add --allow-overriding indiscriminately: overriding packages from a different workspace can have real API/ABI consequences.\n\n## The native Android app cannot pair\n\nCheck the endpoint ends in /ws/android, the 64-digit certificate SHA-256 matches the current receiver certificate, and the token belongs to this receiver session. A certificate replacement needs a new pin. Check the app’s error before changing a setting. Use the updated receiver and APK together.\n\nFor USB on native Ubuntu, verify the authorized adb device and reverse rule. On WSL, also verify Windows localhost:8443 before using the phone’s 127.0.0.1 endpoint. For Wi-Fi, use the actual laptop hotspot address. Annex F separates WSL mirrored and NAT routes and shows scoped firewall/proxy checks.\n\nIf camera setup fails, verify camera permission and that another app has released the camera. Record camera ID, size and timestamp source. UNKNOWN timing permits the labelled camera-only exercise; inertial suitability requires further qualification.\n\n## The selected connection cannot reach the receiver\n\nFor native Ubuntu USB, confirm adb devices lists an authorized phone and adb reverse --list contains tcp:8443. For WSL USB, also test Windows localhost:8443 before pairing. Recreate the reverse rule after a cable/device reconnect when needed. [Annex E](../annexes/android-connections.md) walks through both hosts.\n\nFor wireless, confirm the laptop joined this phone's hotspot and the endpoint uses the laptop's actual Wi-Fi IPv4 address. Keep the firewall enabled and scope any TCP 8443 allowance to the phone and lab interface. WSL may need the mirrored/NAT forwarding described in Annex F. Some hotspot implementations restrict phone-to-client traffic; test that route or use USB.\n\nStop the app, correct the address, fingerprint or token, then restart. The receiver permits one paired phone. Private keys remain on the laptop; pairing tokens belong only in the current lab session.\n\n## Camera or motion is unavailable\n\nIn Android, the app reports accelerometer and gyroscope availability separately. The two ROS topics preserve event times; a missing component stays unavailable. Start and Stop require the app to remain in the foreground. Inspect /phone/diagnostics for camera metadata and clock quality.\n\nThe Android app stops capture on backgrounding. Keep it visible and explicitly restart after switching apps. A phone with a missing motion sensor can still perform the camera-only mapping exercise; document which component is unavailable.\n\n## ROS topics exist but data is silent\n\nJazzy prints a deprecation warning when ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY is set. Remove the old export from your shell setup, run `unset ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY`, set `export ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST`, and keep ROS_DOMAIN_ID and RMW_IMPLEMENTATION consistent. The updated .bashrc block also clears ROS_STATIC_PEERS for the local course lab. Stop existing nodes and the ROS CLI daemon before starting a fresh set of terminals; changing one shell does not change running processes.\n\nCompare resolved names/types and `ros2 topic info TOPIC --verbose`. A best-effort offer cannot meet a reliable request; requesting best effort can connect to that publisher. Keep Jazzy/domain/localhost settings consistent. A listed topic is not proof that samples are arriving.\n\nIf the Android app’s counters increase but ROS does not receive, inspect the receiver error. It rejects malformed dimensions/JPEGs, stale or future stamps and non-increasing sequences. Pairing and clock handshake must complete before samples are accepted.\n\n## Mapping will not initialize or loses tracking\n\nConfirm measured calibration matches the transmitted lens, crop, dimensions and orientation. Translate through textured, well-lit, overlapping views. Pure rotation does not provide monocular depth. Motion blur, autofocus, stabilization, rolling shutter and moving people can degrade results. Slow replay reduces compute load; it cannot repair the captured pixels.\n\nWatch tracking status and the engine viewer. The baseline map is sparse and arbitrary-scale. Receiving motion topics alone does not establish metric scale; an estimator must fuse qualified measurements. If ORB-SLAM3 cannot be built or qualified on the reference machine, mark that backend unverified and use the documented sensor transport exercise plus a real recorded mapping fallback.\n\n## Frames or time do not agree\n\nA coordinate transform changes the point values as well as their frame label. Camera–IMU alignment requires measured extrinsics before a TF edge can describe it. Inspect actual frame names, transform direction and measurement time. The bridge publishes optical images and device-frame motion measurements. Camera–IMU extrinsics remain a separate measured relationship.\n\nThe monocular phone_slam_world is not a gravity-aligned metric map frame. The optional adapter publishes visualization poses in its own arbitrary-scale world. During replay, publish /clock and use_sim_time on relevant consumers; stop competing live publishers first.\n"},{"id":"references","file":"course/annexes/references.md","title":"Keep learning","description":"Primary documentation and next steps.","body":"# Primary references and next steps\n\nThe course target is Jazzy / Ubuntu 24.04. Use distribution-specific documentation and record third-party versions for the phone backend.\n\n## ROS foundations\n\n- [Jazzy tutorials](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Tutorials.html): CLI, client libraries, launch, interfaces and TF2.\n- [Jazzy Ubuntu installation](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Installation/Ubuntu-Install-Debs.html): supported binaries and repository setup.\n- [Jazzy rclpy source](https://github.com/ros2/rclpy/tree/jazzy): inspect the implementation behind client-library behavior.\n- [QoS policies](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Concepts/Intermediate/About-Quality-of-Service-Settings.html): directional compatibility and durability.\n- [REP 103](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-0103.html) and [REP 105](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-0105.html): units, axes and mobile frame semantics.\n\n## A small vocabulary to keep nearby\n\n| Term | Meaning in this course |\n| --- | --- |\n| Callback / executor | A function invoked for ready work / the component that schedules and runs it |\n| Header | Message metadata carrying a timestamp and coordinate-frame name |\n| QoS | Delivery and storage policies that must be compatible between endpoints |\n| Intrinsics / extrinsics | A camera’s pixel-to-ray model / the geometric relationship between sensors or frames |\n| Latency / rate | Delay between events / number of samples per second; a high rate can still have large delay |\n| Radian | Angular unit: a full turn is 2π radians, so 180 degrees is π radians |\n| Sparse / dense reconstruction | Selected scene landmarks / much more complete surface sampling or geometry |\n| Scale ambiguity | Monocular reconstruction can preserve relative shape without knowing its size in metres |\n| Sensor fusion | Combining measurements in an estimator using their models, timing and uncertainty |\n| Ground truth | An independent reference used to evaluate an estimate |\n\n## Native Android acquisition and WSL\n\n- [Camera2 overview](https://developer.android.com/media/camera/camera2): camera configuration and capture APIs.\n- [Camera timestamp source](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/hardware/camera2/CameraCharacteristics#SENSOR_INFO_TIMESTAMP_SOURCE): REALTIME and UNKNOWN clock meanings.\n- [SensorEvent](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/hardware/SensorEvent): per-event time, device axes and motion units.\n- [Android build and signing](https://developer.android.com/studio/publish/app-signing): package identity and APK distribution.\n- [ADB reverse](https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/layout/webapps/access-local-server): USB port forwarding.\n- [WSL networking](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/networking): localhost, mirrored networking and NAT forwarding.\n\n## Calibration and visual mapping\n\n- [image_pipeline Jazzy](https://github.com/ros-perception/image_pipeline/tree/jazzy): monocular calibration tools.\n- [OpenCV calibration](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/d0c/group__calib3d.html): pinhole models and reprojection geometry.\n- [ORB-SLAM3 upstream](https://github.com/UZ-SLAMLab/ORB_SLAM3): the existing sparse SLAM engine used by the optional adapter. Its build dependencies and license are separate from ROS course packages.\n- [OpenVINS calibration](https://docs.openvins.com/gs-calibration.html): camera–IMU timing/extrinsics requirements for advanced inertial estimation.\n\n## Guided annexes\n\n- [Annex A: Build the Android app](../annexes/android-development.md): install/build the APK and inspect Camera2, SensorManager, timestamp metadata and cleanup.\n- [Annex B: Build the ROS receiver](../annexes/bridge-development.md): trace protocol validation, OpenCV decoding, headers and publication.\n- [Annex C: Camera & IMU calibration](../annexes/calibration.md): measure camera intrinsics, validate a model and plan a qualified inertial workflow.\n- [Annex D: More ROS workflows](../annexes/ros-workflows.md): practice services, actions, recording/replay, TF, launch and parameters.\n- [Annex E: Android USB or wireless](../annexes/android-connections.md): connect the same APK and receiver over a cable or a phone hotspot.\n- [Annex F: Capstone on WSL 2](../annexes/wsl-capstone.md): choose a USB or Wi-Fi route and verify the Windows/Ubuntu boundaries.\n- [Visual checkpoints](../annexes/visual-guide.md): compare your own output with labelled illustrative examples.\n\n## After the crash course\n\nLearn URDF/robot_state_publisher, lifecycle management, integration testing and deployment. Study [robot_localization](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/p/robot_localization/) and [Nav2](https://docs.nav2.org/) after your metric sensor/TF contracts are established. Consult the [ROS/Gazebo compatibility table](https://gazebosim.org/docs/latest/ros_installation/) before adding simulation. Jazzy’s recommended modern pairing is Gazebo Harmonic. The simulator is an optional extension after the core course.\n"},{"id":"verification","file":"course/annexes/verification.md","title":"Course scope & learning outcomes","description":"What you will learn, practice and prepare for the capstone.","body":"# Course scope and learning outcomes\n\nThis course teaches **ROS 2 Jazzy on Ubuntu 24.04** through five lectures and a phone-sensor project.\n\n## What you can learn here\n\nThe five lectures introduce graph inspection, Python/C++ nodes, interfaces, QoS, services, actions, parameters, launch, TF and sensor integration. Setup is separate from contact time. The full plan is four 90-minute lectures and one 180-minute project session; a five-by-90-minute delivery moves the mapping lab into follow-up practice.\n\nThe capstone connects a native Android sensor app to a ROS receiver. The same APK connects by wired USB or wireless. Its optional ORB-SLAM3 adapter creates a sparse monocular reconstruction with arbitrary scale. Native accelerometer/gyroscope events retain separate timestamps and are published for inspection. Annex C explains the acquisition and calibration requirements for a future visual-inertial project.\n\n## Find the explanation you need\n\n| Your question | Start here |\n| --- | --- |\n| What do I install and what is a sourced terminal? | Before you begin |\n| What should my terminal or viewer show? | Visual checkpoints and the figures beside each exercise |\n| How do I create, install and connect nodes? | Lectures 1–3 and the source browser |\n| How do I request work and track progress? | Lecture 4 and Annex D’s service/action experiments |\n| How do coordinate frames and timestamps work together? | Frames, transforms & time |\n| How is the app developed? | Annex A: Build the Android app |\n| How does web data become a ROS message? | Annex B: Build the ROS receiver |\n| How do I measure camera calibration and inspect inertial data? | Annex C: Camera & IMU calibration |\n| How do I repeat an experiment or go further? | Annex D and Keep learning |\n\n## Practice and assessment\n\nEach lesson includes predictions, practical exercises and written explanations. Attempt the exercises before consulting the worked responses. Use your own terminal output, frame graph, camera measurements and recordings to explain your results. Quiz progress and notes are stored in your browser and can be exported from Resources.\n\n## How to interpret the illustrations\n\nSoftware examples are labelled **simulated**. They illustrate topic names, fields and places to inspect; their rates, counts, timing and map points are examples. The equipment picture is an AI-generated conceptual illustration. A learner’s own terminal output, calibration record and real viewer screenshot provide evidence for their run. Open any figure to inspect it at full size.\n\n## Prepare for the capstone\n\nFollow Phone lab setup before the project session. Prepare an Android phone, a working USB or wireless connection, camera calibration for the exact transmitted image mode and a consented reference recording. The optional mapping adapter additionally requires a separately built ORB-SLAM3 engine, vocabulary and matching camera settings.\n\nThe mapping result is a sparse monocular reconstruction with arbitrary scale. Receiving accelerometer and gyroscope messages does not establish metric scale or a calibrated visual-inertial estimator. Device compatibility, camera timing and measured calibration must be checked on the equipment you use.\n\n## Official sources behind the guide\n\n- [Jazzy release](https://discourse.ros.org/t/ros-2-jazzy-jalisco-released/37862), [installation source](https://github.com/ros2/ros2_documentation/blob/jazzy/source/Installation/Ubuntu-Install-Debs.rst) and [REP 2000](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-2000.html): distribution support and platforms.\n- [Jazzy rclpy](https://github.com/ros2/rclpy/tree/jazzy), [QoS documentation](https://docs.ros.org/en/jazzy/Concepts/Intermediate/About-Quality-of-Service-Settings.html) and [cv_bridge changelog](https://github.com/ros-perception/vision_opencv/blob/rolling/cv_bridge/CHANGELOG.rst): client APIs, communication compatibility and header migration.\n- [Jazzy camera calibration](https://github.com/ros-perception/image_pipeline/tree/jazzy/camera_calibration), [REP 103](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-0103.html) and [REP 105](https://www.ros.org/reps/rep-0105.html): image calibration, units and frame meanings.\n- [Android Camera2](https://developer.android.com/media/camera/camera2), [camera timestamp source](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/hardware/camera2/CameraCharacteristics#SENSOR_INFO_TIMESTAMP_SOURCE), [SensorEvent](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/hardware/SensorEvent) and [WSL networking](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/networking): native acquisition, clock domains, device axes and connection routes.\n- [ORB-SLAM3 public interface](https://github.com/UZ-SLAMLab/ORB_SLAM3/blob/master/include/System.h), [OpenVINS calibration](https://docs.openvins.com/gs-calibration.html) and [Kalibr](https://github.com/ethz-asl/kalibr/wiki): mapper input contracts and calibration requirements.\n"},{"id":"solutions","file":"course/assessment/exercise-solutions.md","title":"Worked explanations & rubric","description":"Compare your reasoning after attempting the exercises.","body":"# Worked explanations and assessment guide\n\nAttempt the task before comparing. Evidence and reasoning matter more than recalling an isolated command. The generated descriptive-questions.md contains a model explanation and requested evidence for every written checkpoint.\n\n## Lecture 1: graph and naming\n\nTeleop publishes Twist on /turtle1/cmd_vel; turtlesim receives commands there and publishes Pose on /turtle1/pose. Under /robot2, relative names gain that namespace. Remapping only one endpoint disconnects a previously shared stream. The phone itself is a web client; phone_bridge is the node in the ROS graph.\n\nAccept a diagram supported by node list, topic list -t and verbose endpoint output. A moving-turtle screenshot illustrates behavior; node/topic evidence explains the connection that produced it.\n\n## Lecture 2: callbacks and interfaces\n\nUse the complete publisher and subscriber in the lesson. In a subscription callback increment a node counter; use a timer to report the count. At approximately 2 Hz, a one-second report usually observes about two new messages, but startup/discovery and interval boundaries can vary.\n\nFor temperature_c homework, modify the .msg, regenerate interfaces, rebuild consumers and source the overlay. A .msg file is source for generated code, not an immediately available Python class.\n\n## Lecture 3: QoS reasoning\n\nA best-effort publisher cannot meet a reliable subscriber’s requested reliability. The reverse pair is compatible when other policies agree. Depth and durability are separate policies; a larger queue cannot fix incompatible reliability. Python/C++ interoperability depends on ROS contracts, not matching source syntax.\n\nFor the 5 Hz C++ exercise, a 200 ms timer is the nominal period. Rebuild the installed executable. Discuss logging and scheduler/measurement overhead before making performance claims.\n\n## Lecture 4: services, parameters and transforms\n\nThe area request 3 by 4 returns 12 with valid true. The service does not provide an application-level exactly-once transaction guarantee. The action example has a goal, streamed feedback and a terminal result; use the supplied client’s cancel_after parameter to request cancellation and confirm canceled status.\n\nThe node validates updates without side effects, then reads committed values and adjusts its timer at the next existing timer tick. Explain the difference between accepting a parameter value and applying it to a running resource. Jazzy also offers post-set callbacks for designs that need an explicit application hook.\n\nFor the +90° sensor demo, rotate (1,0,0) to (0,1,0), then add (0.1,0,0.2): the result is (0.1,1.0,0.2) m in demo_base. lookup_transform(target, source, time) maps source coordinates into target. An identity quaternion is (0,0,0,1), and changing frame_id alone does not transform a point.\n\n## Lecture 5: phone integration\n\nThe Android app checks the receiver certificate pin and session token over WSS. Option 1 uses wired USB with ADB reverse; Option 2 uses wireless over the phone hotspot. Both run the same app and version-2 WSS protocol, with the same pin, token and ROS topics. Hotspots separate local addressing while sharing radio airtime. A low RTT helps estimate phone-to-laptop offset; the camera’s timestamp source and sensor calibration need their own evidence.\n\nAndroid accelerometer and gyroscope components retain separate event timestamps and m/s² or rad/s units under phone_android_device. Each ROS message marks its missing component unavailable. Camera–IMU geometry must be measured. The supplied backend consumes images and creates a sparse arbitrary-scale monocular reconstruction. The Pangolin map, current tracked-point cloud and bounded ROS pose history are different outputs.\n\n## Capstone evidence and rubric\n\nSubmit the mode/version/calibration manifest, trusted connection and topic-rate evidence, a short consented bag with checksums, exact commands, mapping evidence from a qualified live/reference run, and one diagnosed failure. A useful deliberate fault is a wrong topic remap or invalid pairing token, repaired using concrete observations and explain which observation identified the fault.\n\nRubric: transport 25%; time/frame/calibration reasoning 30%; reproducibility/mapping evidence 25%; diagnosis and limits 20%. Learners with unsupported phones should analyze the instructor-prepared qualified reference run. Award credit for evidence that distinguishes sensor transport, sparse monocular reconstruction and the additional requirements of inertial estimation.\n"}],"code":[{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_bringup/CMakeLists.txt","body":"cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.8)\nproject(ros2_course_bringup)\n\nfind_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)\n\n# A \"bringup\" package usually contains NO source code -- just launch files,\n# parameter YAMLs and RViz configs that wire other packages together.\ninstall(DIRECTORY\n  launch\n  DESTINATION share/${PROJECT_NAME}\n)\n\nament_package()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_bringup/launch/course_pubsub.launch.py","body":"\"\"\"Include the beginner Python launch recipe from a system bringup package.\"\"\"\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom ament_index_python.packages import get_package_share_directory\nfrom launch import LaunchDescription\nfrom launch.actions import IncludeLaunchDescription\nfrom launch.launch_description_sources import PythonLaunchDescriptionSource\n\n\ndef generate_launch_description():\n    launch_file = Path(get_package_share_directory('ros2_course_py')) / 'launch' / 'pubsub_demo.launch.py'\n    return LaunchDescription([IncludeLaunchDescription(PythonLaunchDescriptionSource(str(launch_file)))])\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_bringup/package.xml","body":"<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\n<?xml-model href=\"http://download.ros.org/schema/package_format3.xsd\" schematypens=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\"?>\n<package format=\"3\">\n  <name>ros2_course_bringup</name>\n  <version>1.0.0</version>\n  <description>Repeatable beginner launch recipe for the Jazzy phone-sensor course.</description>\n  <maintainer email=\"instructor@example.com\">Instructor</maintainer>\n  <license>Apache-2.0</license>\n\n  <buildtool_depend>ament_cmake</buildtool_depend>\n\n  <exec_depend>launch</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>launch_ros</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>rviz2</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>tf2_ros</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>ament_index_python</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>ros2_course_py</exec_depend>\n\n  <export>\n    <build_type>ament_cmake</build_type>\n  </export>\n</package>\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_cpp/CMakeLists.txt","body":"cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.8)\nproject(ros2_course_cpp)\n\n# This course targets Jazzy and C++17.\nif(NOT CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD)\n  set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)\nendif()\nif(CMAKE_COMPILER_IS_GNUCXX OR CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES \"Clang\")\n  add_compile_options(-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic)\nendif()\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 1. FIND every dependency you use\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nfind_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)\nfind_package(rclcpp REQUIRED)\nfind_package(std_msgs REQUIRED)\nfind_package(geometry_msgs REQUIRED)\nfind_package(sensor_msgs REQUIRED)\nfind_package(ros2_course_interfaces REQUIRED)\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 2. For each executable: add_executable + ament_target_dependencies\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nadd_executable(talker src/talker.cpp)\nament_target_dependencies(talker rclcpp std_msgs)\n\nadd_executable(listener src/listener.cpp)\nament_target_dependencies(listener rclcpp std_msgs)\n\nadd_executable(area_server src/area_server.cpp)\nament_target_dependencies(area_server rclcpp ros2_course_interfaces)\n\nadd_executable(scan_min src/scan_min.cpp)\nament_target_dependencies(scan_min rclcpp sensor_msgs)\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 3. INSTALL them into lib/<package> so `ros2 run` can find them.\n#    Forgetting this is the #1 reason for \"No executable found\".\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ninstall(TARGETS\n  talker\n  listener\n  area_server\n  scan_min\n  DESTINATION lib/${PROJECT_NAME}\n)\n\n# If you later add launch/ or config/ folders to this package, install them too:\n#   install(DIRECTORY launch config DESTINATION share/${PROJECT_NAME})\n\nament_package()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_cpp/package.xml","body":"<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\n<?xml-model href=\"http://download.ros.org/schema/package_format3.xsd\" schematypens=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\"?>\n<package format=\"3\">\n  <name>ros2_course_cpp</name>\n  <version>1.0.0</version>\n  <description>C++ (rclcpp) example nodes for the 5-session ROS 2 course.</description>\n  <maintainer email=\"instructor@example.com\">Instructor</maintainer>\n  <license>Apache-2.0</license>\n\n  <buildtool_depend>ament_cmake</buildtool_depend>\n\n  <depend>rclcpp</depend>\n  <depend>std_msgs</depend>\n  <depend>geometry_msgs</depend>\n  <depend>sensor_msgs</depend>\n  <depend>ros2_course_interfaces</depend>\n\n  <test_depend>ament_lint_auto</test_depend>\n  <test_depend>ament_lint_common</test_depend>\n\n  <export>\n    <build_type>ament_cmake</build_type>\n  </export>\n</package>\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_cpp/src/area_server.cpp","body":"// Lecture 3/4 - a C++ service server using our custom interface.\n//\n// Run:  ros2 run ros2_course_cpp area_server\n// Call (one line): ros2 service call /compute_area\n//   ros2_course_interfaces/srv/ComputeRectangleArea \"{width: 3.0, height: 4.0}\"\n\n#include <cmath>\n#include <functional>\n#include <memory>\n\n#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\n#include \"ros2_course_interfaces/srv/compute_rectangle_area.hpp\"\n//                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n// NOTE the naming rule: the interface file ComputeRectangleArea.srv becomes the\n// header compute_rectangle_area.hpp (CamelCase -> snake_case).\n\nusing ComputeRectangleArea = ros2_course_interfaces::srv::ComputeRectangleArea;\nusing std::placeholders::_1;\nusing std::placeholders::_2;\n\nclass AreaServer : public rclcpp::Node\n{\npublic:\n  AreaServer()\n  : Node(\"cpp_area_server\")\n  {\n    server_ = this->create_service<ComputeRectangleArea>(\n      \"compute_area\",\n      std::bind(&AreaServer::callbackComputeArea, this, _1, _2));\n\n    RCLCPP_INFO(this->get_logger(), \"C++ area service is ready.\");\n  }\n\nprivate:\n  void callbackComputeArea(\n    const ComputeRectangleArea::Request::SharedPtr request,\n    const ComputeRectangleArea::Response::SharedPtr response)\n  {\n    if (!std::isfinite(request->width) || !std::isfinite(request->height) ||\n      request->width < 0.0 || request->height < 0.0 ||\n      !std::isfinite(request->width * request->height)) {\n      response->area = 0.0;\n      response->valid = false;\n      response->message = \"dimensions and area must be finite; dimensions must be >= 0\";\n    } else {\n      response->area = request->width * request->height;\n      response->valid = true;\n      response->message = \"ok\";\n    }\n\n    RCLCPP_INFO(\n      this->get_logger(), \"%.2f x %.2f -> %.2f\",\n      request->width, request->height, response->area);\n  }\n\n  rclcpp::Service<ComputeRectangleArea>::SharedPtr server_;\n};\n\nint main(int argc, char ** argv)\n{\n  rclcpp::init(argc, argv);\n  rclcpp::spin(std::make_shared<AreaServer>());\n  rclcpp::shutdown();\n  return 0;\n}\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_cpp/src/listener.cpp","body":"// Lecture 3 - the C++ subscriber.\n//\n// Run:  ros2 run ros2_course_cpp listener\n//\n// Cross-language check: run the PYTHON talker and the C++ listener together.\n// They interoperate, because both speak DDS on the wire, not Python or C++.\n\n#include <functional>\n#include <memory>\n\n#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\n#include \"std_msgs/msg/string.hpp\"\n\nusing std::placeholders::_1;\n\nclass ListenerNode : public rclcpp::Node\n{\npublic:\n  ListenerNode()\n  : Node(\"cpp_listener\")\n  {\n    subscription_ = this->create_subscription<std_msgs::msg::String>(\n      \"chatter\", 10,\n      std::bind(&ListenerNode::callbackChatter, this, _1));\n\n    RCLCPP_INFO(this->get_logger(), \"C++ listener started, waiting...\");\n  }\n\nprivate:\n  void callbackChatter(const std_msgs::msg::String::SharedPtr msg)\n  {\n    RCLCPP_INFO(this->get_logger(), \"I heard: '%s'\", msg->data.c_str());\n  }\n\n  rclcpp::Subscription<std_msgs::msg::String>::SharedPtr subscription_;\n};\n\nint main(int argc, char ** argv)\n{\n  rclcpp::init(argc, argv);\n  rclcpp::spin(std::make_shared<ListenerNode>());\n  rclcpp::shutdown();\n  return 0;\n}\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_cpp/src/scan_min.cpp","body":"// Lecture 5 - the same LaserScan processing as scan_stats.py, in C++.\n//\n// Run: ros2 run ros2_course_cpp scan_min --ros-args -p use_sim_time:=true\n//\n// Optional LiDAR example. Measure performance on your actual data;\n// language choice alone is not a performance guarantee.\n\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <functional>\n#include <cmath>\n#include <limits>\n#include <memory>\n\n#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\n#include \"sensor_msgs/msg/laser_scan.hpp\"\n\nusing std::placeholders::_1;\n\nclass ScanMin : public rclcpp::Node\n{\npublic:\n  ScanMin()\n  : Node(\"cpp_scan_min\")\n  {\n    // rclcpp::SensorDataQoS() is the C++ equivalent of qos_profile_sensor_data.\n    subscription_ = this->create_subscription<sensor_msgs::msg::LaserScan>(\n      \"scan\", rclcpp::SensorDataQoS(),\n      std::bind(&ScanMin::callbackScan, this, _1));\n\n    RCLCPP_INFO(this->get_logger(), \"Waiting for /scan ...\");\n  }\n\nprivate:\n  void callbackScan(const sensor_msgs::msg::LaserScan::SharedPtr scan)\n  {\n    float closest = std::numeric_limits<float>::infinity();\n    double closest_angle = 0.0;\n    size_t valid = 0;\n\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < scan->ranges.size(); ++i) {\n      const float r = scan->ranges[i];\n      if (!std::isfinite(r)) {continue;}\n      if (r < scan->range_min || r > scan->range_max) {continue;}\n      ++valid;\n      if (r < closest) {\n        closest = r;\n        closest_angle = scan->angle_min + i * scan->angle_increment;\n      }\n    }\n\n    if (valid == 0) {\n      RCLCPP_WARN(this->get_logger(), \"No valid returns in this scan.\");\n      return;\n    }\n\n    RCLCPP_INFO(\n      this->get_logger(),\n      \"valid=%zu/%zu  closest=%.2f m at %.1f deg\",\n      valid, scan->ranges.size(), closest, closest_angle * 180.0 / std::acos(-1.0));\n  }\n\n  rclcpp::Subscription<sensor_msgs::msg::LaserScan>::SharedPtr subscription_;\n};\n\nint main(int argc, char ** argv)\n{\n  rclcpp::init(argc, argv);\n  rclcpp::spin(std::make_shared<ScanMin>());\n  rclcpp::shutdown();\n  return 0;\n}\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_cpp/src/talker.cpp","body":"// Lecture 3 - the C++ (rclcpp) twin of the Python talker.\n//\n// Build: colcon build --packages-select ros2_course_cpp\n// Run:   ros2 run ros2_course_cpp talker\n//\n// Compare this file line-by-line with ros2_course_py/talker.py. The CONCEPTS are\n// identical; only the syntax changes.\n\n#include <chrono>\n#include <functional>\n#include <memory>\n#include <string>\n\n#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\n#include \"std_msgs/msg/string.hpp\"\n\nusing namespace std::chrono_literals;   // lets us write 500ms\n\nclass TalkerNode : public rclcpp::Node\n{\npublic:\n  TalkerNode()\n  : Node(\"cpp_talker\"), counter_(0)      // \"cpp_talker\" is the default node name\n  {\n    // Template argument = message type. Arguments = topic name, QoS depth.\n    publisher_ = this->create_publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>(\"chatter\", 10);\n\n    timer_ = this->create_wall_timer(\n      500ms,\n      std::bind(&TalkerNode::publishMessage, this));\n\n    RCLCPP_INFO(this->get_logger(), \"C++ talker has been started.\");\n  }\n\nprivate:\n  void publishMessage()\n  {\n    auto msg = std_msgs::msg::String();\n    msg.data = \"Hello ROS 2 from C++! count = \" + std::to_string(counter_);\n    publisher_->publish(msg);\n    RCLCPP_INFO(this->get_logger(), \"Publishing: '%s'\", msg.data.c_str());\n    counter_++;\n  }\n\n  rclcpp::Publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>::SharedPtr publisher_;\n  rclcpp::TimerBase::SharedPtr timer_;\n  int counter_;\n};\n\nint main(int argc, char ** argv)\n{\n  rclcpp::init(argc, argv);                       // 1. start the client library\n  auto node = std::make_shared<TalkerNode>();     // 2. create the node\n  rclcpp::spin(node);                             // 3. process callbacks forever\n  rclcpp::shutdown();                             // 4. clean up\n  return 0;\n}\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_interfaces/action/CountUntil.action","body":"# GOAL: what the client asks for\nint32   target        # count 1, 2, 3 ... up to this number\nfloat64 period        # seconds to wait between numbers\n---\n# RESULT: what the client gets when the action finishes\nint32   reached_number\n---\n# FEEDBACK: streamed to the client while the action runs\nint32   current_number\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_interfaces/CMakeLists.txt","body":"cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.8)\nproject(ros2_course_interfaces)\n\nif(CMAKE_COMPILER_IS_GNUCXX OR CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES \"Clang\")\n  add_compile_options(-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic)\nendif()\n\nfind_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)\nfind_package(rosidl_default_generators REQUIRED)\n\n# Every .msg / .srv / .action file must be listed here, or it will not be generated.\nrosidl_generate_interfaces(${PROJECT_NAME}\n  \"msg/RobotStatus.msg\"\n  \"srv/ComputeRectangleArea.srv\"\n  \"action/CountUntil.action\"\n)\n\nament_export_dependencies(rosidl_default_runtime)\nament_package()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_interfaces/msg/RobotStatus.msg","body":"# A custom message describing the state of a (pretend) robot.\n# Field types come from the built-in ROS 2 IDL types:\n#   bool, byte, char, float32, float64, int8/16/32/64, uint8/16/32/64, string\n# Arrays: int32[] unbounded, int32[5] fixed, int32[<=5] bounded\n\nstring  robot_name        # human readable name, e.g. \"tb3_01\"\nfloat32 battery_percent   # 0.0 .. 100.0\nbool    is_moving         # true while wheels are turning\nuint8   fault_code        # 0 = OK\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_interfaces/package.xml","body":"<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\n<?xml-model href=\"http://download.ros.org/schema/package_format3.xsd\" schematypens=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\"?>\n<package format=\"3\">\n  <name>ros2_course_interfaces</name>\n  <version>1.0.0</version>\n  <description>Custom messages, services and actions for the 5-session ROS 2 course.</description>\n  <maintainer email=\"instructor@example.com\">Instructor</maintainer>\n  <license>Apache-2.0</license>\n\n  <buildtool_depend>ament_cmake</buildtool_depend>\n\n  <!-- These three lines are what turn a plain package into an interface package -->\n  <buildtool_depend>rosidl_default_generators</buildtool_depend>\n  <exec_depend>rosidl_default_runtime</exec_depend>\n  <member_of_group>rosidl_interface_packages</member_of_group>\n\n  <test_depend>ament_lint_auto</test_depend>\n  <test_depend>ament_lint_common</test_depend>\n\n  <export>\n    <build_type>ament_cmake</build_type>\n  </export>\n</package>\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_interfaces/srv/ComputeRectangleArea.srv","body":"# Request fields go above the three dashes.\nfloat64 width\nfloat64 height\n---\n# Response fields go below the three dashes.\nfloat64 area\nbool    valid       # false if width or height was negative\nstring  message\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/launch/pubsub_demo.launch.py","body":"\"\"\"\nLecture 4 - a launch file starts several nodes with one command.\n\nRun:  ros2 launch ros2_course_py pubsub_demo.launch.py\n      ros2 launch ros2_course_py pubsub_demo.launch.py message:=\"Namaste\" rate:=5.0\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom launch import LaunchDescription\nfrom launch.actions import DeclareLaunchArgument\nfrom launch.substitutions import LaunchConfiguration\nfrom launch_ros.actions import Node\nfrom launch_ros.parameter_descriptions import ParameterValue\n\n\ndef generate_launch_description():\n    # Launch ARGUMENTS let you change behaviour from the command line.\n    message_arg = DeclareLaunchArgument('message', default_value='Hello from launch')\n    rate_arg = DeclareLaunchArgument('rate', default_value='2.0')\n\n    talker = Node(\n        package='ros2_course_py',\n        executable='params_demo',\n        name='my_talker',                     # remap the node name\n        parameters=[{\n            'message': LaunchConfiguration('message'),\n            'rate_hz': ParameterValue(LaunchConfiguration('rate'), value_type=float),\n        }],\n        remappings=[('chatter', 'lecture_chatter')],   # topic renamed for BOTH nodes\n        output='screen',\n    )\n\n    listener = Node(\n        package='ros2_course_py',\n        executable='listener',\n        name='my_listener',\n        remappings=[('chatter', 'lecture_chatter')],\n        output='screen',\n    )\n\n    return LaunchDescription([message_arg, rate_arg, talker, listener])\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/package.xml","body":"<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\n<?xml-model href=\"http://download.ros.org/schema/package_format3.xsd\" schematypens=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\"?>\n<package format=\"3\">\n  <name>ros2_course_py</name>\n  <version>1.0.0</version>\n  <description>Python (rclpy) example nodes for the 5-session ROS 2 course.</description>\n  <maintainer email=\"instructor@example.com\">Instructor</maintainer>\n  <license>Apache-2.0</license>\n\n  <!-- Every message/library your code imports must appear here as a depend. -->\n  <depend>rclpy</depend>\n  <depend>std_msgs</depend>\n  <depend>geometry_msgs</depend>\n  <depend>sensor_msgs</depend>\n  <depend>nav_msgs</depend>\n  <depend>tf2_ros</depend>\n  <depend>rcl_interfaces</depend>\n  <depend>ros2_course_interfaces</depend>\n\n  <exec_depend>launch</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>ament_index_python</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>python3-aiohttp</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>python3-opencv</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>python3-numpy</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>python3-yaml</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>cv_bridge</exec_depend>\n  <exec_depend>launch_ros</exec_depend>\n\n  <test_depend>ament_copyright</test_depend>\n  <test_depend>ament_flake8</test_depend>\n  <test_depend>ament_pep257</test_depend>\n  <test_depend>python3-pytest</test_depend>\n\n  <export>\n    <build_type>ament_python</build_type>\n  </export>\n</package>\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/area_client.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 4 - a SERVICE client.\n\nRun:  ros2 run ros2_course_py area_client --ros-args -p width:=5.0 -p height:=2.5\n\nThe important idea: `call_async()` returns a Future. You must let the executor\nspin until the future is done -- otherwise you will wait forever.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom ros2_course_interfaces.srv import ComputeRectangleArea\n\n\nclass AreaClient(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('area_client')\n\n        self.declare_parameter('width', 3.0)\n        self.declare_parameter('height', 4.0)\n\n        self.client_ = self.create_client(ComputeRectangleArea, 'compute_area')\n\n        # Wait until a server actually exists, otherwise the call is dropped.\n        while not self.client_.wait_for_service(timeout_sec=1.0):\n            self.get_logger().warn('Waiting for /compute_area service...')\n\n    def send_request(self):\n        request = ComputeRectangleArea.Request()\n        request.width = self.get_parameter('width').value\n        request.height = self.get_parameter('height').value\n\n        future = self.client_.call_async(request)\n        # spin_until_future_complete keeps the node alive until the answer arrives.\n        rclpy.spin_until_future_complete(self, future)\n\n        response = future.result()\n        if response.valid:\n            self.get_logger().info(f'Area = {response.area}')\n        else:\n            self.get_logger().error(f'Server said: {response.message}')\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = AreaClient()\n    try:\n        node.send_request()\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/area_server.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 4 - a SERVICE server (short request/response).\n\nRun:  ros2 run ros2_course_py area_server\nCall: ros2 service call /compute_area ros2_course_interfaces/srv/ComputeRectangleArea \"{width: 3.0, height: 4.0}\"\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom ros2_course_interfaces.srv import ComputeRectangleArea\n\n\nclass AreaServer(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('area_server')\n\n        # create_service(srv_type, service_name, callback)\n        self.srv_ = self.create_service(\n            ComputeRectangleArea,\n            'compute_area',\n            self.callback_compute_area,\n        )\n        self.get_logger().info('Area service is ready.')\n\n    def callback_compute_area(self, request, response):\n        # A service callback MUST fill in `response` and return it.\n        # Keep it FAST -- while it runs, the node is busy.\n        if (not math.isfinite(request.width) or not math.isfinite(request.height)\n                or request.width < 0.0 or request.height < 0.0\n                or not math.isfinite(request.width * request.height)):\n            response.area = 0.0\n            response.valid = False\n            response.message = 'dimensions and area must be finite; dimensions must be >= 0'\n        else:\n            response.area = request.width * request.height\n            response.valid = True\n            response.message = 'ok'\n\n        self.get_logger().info(\n            f'{request.width} x {request.height} -> {response.area}'\n        )\n        return response\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = AreaServer()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/camera_audit.py","body":"\"\"\"Inspect the four stereo inputs without copying or processing their pixels.\n\nThis basic audit checks recent messages, not exact synchronization or calibration.\nRun with use_sim_time:=true for a bag publishing /clock.\n\"\"\"\nimport math\nimport time\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom rclpy.qos import qos_profile_sensor_data\nfrom sensor_msgs.msg import CameraInfo, Image\n\n\ndef stamp_ns(message):\n    return message.header.stamp.sec * 1_000_000_000 + message.header.stamp.nanosec\n\n\nclass CameraAudit(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('camera_audit')\n        self.latest = {}\n        self.arrival = {}\n        self.subscriptions_ = []\n        defaults = {\n            'left_image': '/stereo/left/image_rect',\n            'right_image': '/stereo/right/image_rect',\n            'left_info': '/stereo/left/camera_info',\n            'right_info': '/stereo/right/camera_info',\n        }\n        for key, topic in defaults.items():\n            self.declare_parameter(key + '_topic', topic)\n            msg_type = Image if key.endswith('image') else CameraInfo\n            self.subscriptions_.append(self.create_subscription(\n                msg_type, self.get_parameter(key + '_topic').value,\n                lambda msg, name=key: self.receive(name, msg), qos_profile_sensor_data))\n        self.timer_ = self.create_timer(1.0, self.report)\n\n    def receive(self, name, message):\n        self.latest[name] = message\n        self.arrival[name] = time.monotonic()\n\n    def report(self):\n        missing = set(('left_image', 'right_image', 'left_info', 'right_info')) - self.latest.keys()\n        if missing:\n            self.get_logger().warn('Waiting for: ' + ', '.join(sorted(missing)))\n            return\n        left, right = self.latest['left_image'], self.latest['right_image']\n        li, ri = self.latest['left_info'], self.latest['right_info']\n        problems = []\n        for name, msg in self.latest.items():\n            if time.monotonic() - self.arrival[name] > 2.0:\n                problems.append(name + ' not received for >2 wall seconds')\n            if not msg.header.frame_id:\n                problems.append(name + ' has empty frame_id')\n            if stamp_ns(msg) == 0:\n                problems.append(name + ' has a zero stamp')\n        for side, image, info in [('left', left, li), ('right', right, ri)]:\n            if (image.width, image.height) != (info.width, info.height):\n                problems.append(side + ' resolution mismatch')\n            if image.header.frame_id != info.header.frame_id:\n                problems.append(side + ' image/CameraInfo frame mismatch')\n            if image.width == 0 or image.height == 0 or len(image.data) != image.step * image.height:\n                problems.append(side + ' empty or inconsistent image buffer')\n            if not all(math.isfinite(v) for v in list(info.k) + list(info.p)) or info.k[0] <= 0:\n                problems.append(side + ' invalid intrinsics/projection')\n        fx = ri.p[0]\n        baseline = -ri.p[3] / fx if math.isfinite(fx) and fx > 0 else float('nan')\n        if not math.isfinite(baseline) or baseline <= 0:\n            problems.append('invalid conventional right projection baseline')\n        skew_ms = (max(stamp_ns(m) for m in self.latest.values()) -\n                   min(stamp_ns(m) for m in self.latest.values())) / 1_000_000\n        text = (\n            f'{left.width}x{left.height} encoding={left.encoding}/{right.encoding} '\n            f'frames={left.header.frame_id}/{right.header.frame_id} '\n            f'fx={fx:.2f}px baseline={baseline:.4f}m recent stamp spread={skew_ms:.2f}ms; '\n            'latest samples are not a synchronized tuple')\n        if problems:\n            self.get_logger().warn(text + '; ' + '; '.join(problems))\n        else:\n            self.get_logger().info(text)\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = CameraAudit()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            if rclpy.ok():\n                rclpy.shutdown()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/count_until_client.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 4 - the matching ACTION client.\n\nRun:  ros2 run ros2_course_py count_until_client --ros-args -p target:=6\n\"\"\"\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.action import ActionClient\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\n\nfrom ros2_course_interfaces.action import CountUntil\n\n\nclass CountUntilClient(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('count_until_client')\n        self.declare_parameter('target', 5)\n        self.declare_parameter('period', 0.7)\n        self.declare_parameter('cancel_after', 0.0)\n        self.cancel_timer_ = None\n        self.goal_handle_ = None\n\n        self.action_client_ = ActionClient(self, CountUntil, 'count_until')\n\n    def send_goal(self):\n        self.action_client_.wait_for_server()\n\n        goal = CountUntil.Goal()\n        goal.target = self.get_parameter('target').value\n        goal.period = self.get_parameter('period').value\n\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Sending goal: count to {goal.target}')\n        self.action_client_ \\\n            .send_goal_async(goal, feedback_callback=self.feedback_callback) \\\n            .add_done_callback(self.goal_response_callback)\n\n    def goal_response_callback(self, future):\n        goal_handle = future.result()\n        if not goal_handle.accepted:\n            self.get_logger().error('Goal was REJECTED by the server.')\n            if rclpy.ok():\n                rclpy.shutdown()\n            return\n        self.get_logger().info('Goal accepted.')\n        self.goal_handle_ = goal_handle\n        cancel_after = self.get_parameter('cancel_after').value\n        if cancel_after > 0.0:\n            self.cancel_timer_ = self.create_timer(cancel_after, self.cancel_goal)\n        goal_handle.get_result_async().add_done_callback(self.result_callback)\n\n    def cancel_goal(self):\n        self.cancel_timer_.cancel()\n        self.goal_handle_.cancel_goal_async()\n        self.get_logger().info('Cancellation requested; waiting for terminal result.')\n\n    def feedback_callback(self, feedback_msg):\n        self.get_logger().info(f'feedback: {feedback_msg.feedback.current_number}')\n\n    def result_callback(self, future):\n        result = future.result().result\n        status = future.result().status\n        status_name = {4: 'succeeded', 5: 'canceled', 6: 'aborted'}.get(status, str(status))\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Terminal action status: {status_name}')\n        self.get_logger().info(f'FINAL RESULT: reached {result.reached_number}')\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = CountUntilClient()\n    node.send_goal()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/count_until_server.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 4 - an ACTION server: a long job with feedback and the ability to cancel.\n\nRun:  ros2 run ros2_course_py count_until_server\nSend: ros2 action send_goal /count_until ros2_course_interfaces/action/CountUntil \\\n        \"{target: 8, period: 0.5}\" --feedback\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\nimport time\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.action import ActionServer, CancelResponse, GoalResponse\nfrom rclpy.callback_groups import ReentrantCallbackGroup\nfrom rclpy.executors import MultiThreadedExecutor\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\n\nfrom ros2_course_interfaces.action import CountUntil\n\n\nclass CountUntilServer(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('count_until_server')\n\n        self.action_server_ = ActionServer(\n            self,\n            CountUntil,\n            'count_until',\n            goal_callback=self.goal_callback,\n            cancel_callback=self.cancel_callback,\n            execute_callback=self.execute_callback,\n            callback_group=ReentrantCallbackGroup(),\n        )\n        self.get_logger().info('CountUntil action server started.')\n\n    def goal_callback(self, goal_request):\n        \"\"\"Decide whether to accept the goal at all.\"\"\"\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Received goal: target={goal_request.target}')\n        if (goal_request.target <= 0 or not math.isfinite(goal_request.period)\n                or not 0.0 < goal_request.period <= 5.0):\n            self.get_logger().warn('Rejecting: target > 0 and finite period in (0, 5] required.')\n            return GoalResponse.REJECT\n        return GoalResponse.ACCEPT\n\n    def cancel_callback(self, goal_handle):\n        self.get_logger().info('Cancel requested.')\n        return CancelResponse.ACCEPT\n\n    def execute_callback(self, goal_handle):\n        \"\"\"Do the actual work. This runs in its own thread.\"\"\"\n        target = goal_handle.request.target\n        period = goal_handle.request.period\n\n        feedback = CountUntil.Feedback()\n        result = CountUntil.Result()\n\n        counter = 0\n        for i in range(target):\n            if goal_handle.is_cancel_requested:\n                goal_handle.canceled()\n                result.reached_number = counter\n                self.get_logger().info('Goal canceled.')\n                return result\n\n            counter += 1\n            feedback.current_number = counter\n            goal_handle.publish_feedback(feedback)   # <- streamed to the client\n            self.get_logger().info(f'count = {counter}')\n            time.sleep(period)\n\n        goal_handle.succeed()\n        result.reached_number = counter\n        return result\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = CountUntilServer()\n    # An action server that sleeps needs more than one thread, or cancel requests\n    # would never be processed.\n    executor = MultiThreadedExecutor()\n    executor.add_node(node)\n    try:\n        executor.spin()\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        executor.shutdown()\n        node.action_server_.destroy()\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/listener.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 2 - the matching subscriber.\n\nRun it with:  ros2 run ros2_course_py listener\n\"\"\"\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\n\nclass ListenerNode(Node):\n    \"\"\"A node that subscribes to /chatter and prints whatever arrives.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('py_listener')\n\n        # create_subscription(msg_type, topic_name, callback, qos_depth)\n        # The callback runs on the executor thread every time a message arrives.\n        self.subscription_ = self.create_subscription(\n            String,\n            'chatter',\n            self.callback_chatter,\n            10,\n        )\n\n        self.get_logger().info('Listener has been started, waiting for messages...')\n\n    def callback_chatter(self, msg: String):\n        self.get_logger().info(f'I heard: \"{msg.data}\"')\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = ListenerNode()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/obstacle_stopper.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 5 - the classic \"sense -> think -> act\" node.\n\nSubscribes to /scan (sensor_msgs/LaserScan)\nPublishes  to /cmd_vel (geometry_msgs/Twist)\n\nBehaviour: drive forward; if something is closer than `stop_distance` inside the\nfront cone, stop and rotate until the path is clear.\n\nRun in the Gazebo TurtleBot3 world:\n    ros2 run ros2_course_py obstacle_stopper --ros-args -p use_sim_time:=true\n\nSAFETY: this is a teaching example, not a safety system. On real hardware always\nkeep a hand on the e-stop / power switch.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\nimport time\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom rclpy.qos import qos_profile_sensor_data\nfrom geometry_msgs.msg import Twist\nfrom sensor_msgs.msg import LaserScan\n\n\nclass ObstacleStopper(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('obstacle_stopper')\n\n        self.declare_parameter('stop_distance', 0.45)     # metres\n        self.declare_parameter('forward_speed', 0.15)     # m/s\n        self.declare_parameter('turn_speed', 0.5)         # rad/s\n        self.declare_parameter('front_cone_deg', 30.0)    # +/- degrees about 0 rad\n\n        self.cmd_pub_ = self.create_publisher(Twist, 'cmd_vel', 10)\n        self.scan_sub_ = self.create_subscription(\n            LaserScan, 'scan', self.callback_scan, qos_profile_sensor_data\n        )\n\n        self.front_distance_ = float('inf')\n        self.last_valid_scan_ = None\n        self.control_timer_ = self.create_timer(0.1, self.control_loop)  # 10 Hz\n        self.get_logger().info('Obstacle stopper running. Ctrl-C to stop.')\n\n    def callback_scan(self, scan: LaserScan):\n        \"\"\"Reduce the whole scan to ONE number: min distance in the front cone.\"\"\"\n        half_cone = math.radians(self.get_parameter('front_cone_deg').value)\n\n        best = float('inf')\n        for i, r in enumerate(scan.ranges):\n            if math.isinf(r) or math.isnan(r):\n                continue\n            if not (scan.range_min <= r <= scan.range_max):\n                continue\n\n            angle = scan.angle_min + i * scan.angle_increment\n            # Wrap into [-pi, pi] so the cone test works for 0..2pi scanners too.\n            angle = math.atan2(math.sin(angle), math.cos(angle))\n            if abs(angle) <= half_cone:\n                best = min(best, r)\n\n        self.front_distance_ = best\n        self.last_valid_scan_ = time.monotonic() if math.isfinite(best) else None\n\n    def control_loop(self):\n        cmd = Twist()\n        # Optional simulation example: never drive on absent, stale or invalid data.\n        if self.last_valid_scan_ is None or time.monotonic() - self.last_valid_scan_ > 0.5:\n            self.cmd_pub_.publish(cmd)\n            return\n        stop_distance = self.get_parameter('stop_distance').value\n\n        if self.front_distance_ < stop_distance:\n            cmd.linear.x = 0.0\n            cmd.angular.z = self.get_parameter('turn_speed').value\n            self.get_logger().info(\n                f'BLOCKED at {self.front_distance_:.2f} m - turning', throttle_duration_sec=1.0\n            )\n        else:\n            cmd.linear.x = self.get_parameter('forward_speed').value\n            cmd.angular.z = 0.0\n\n        self.cmd_pub_.publish(cmd)\n\n    def stop_robot(self):\n        self.cmd_pub_.publish(Twist())   # all-zero Twist == stop\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = ObstacleStopper()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.stop_robot()\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/params_demo.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 4 - parameters: how you configure a node WITHOUT editing its source.\n\nRun with defaults:\n    ros2 run ros2_course_py params_demo\n\nOverride at launch time:\n    ros2 run ros2_course_py params_demo --ros-args -p message:=\"Namaste\" -p rate_hz:=5.0\n\nInspect and change at RUNTIME:\n    ros2 param list\n    ros2 param get /params_demo message\n    ros2 param set /params_demo message \"changed live!\"\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom rcl_interfaces.msg import SetParametersResult\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String\n\n\nclass ParamsDemo(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('params_demo')\n\n        # declare_parameter(name, default_value) -- ALWAYS declare before you use.\n        self.declare_parameter('message', 'Hello from a parameter')\n        self.declare_parameter('rate_hz', 2.0)\n\n        self.message_ = self.get_parameter('message').value\n        rate_hz = self.get_parameter('rate_hz').value\n        if not isinstance(rate_hz, float) or not math.isfinite(rate_hz) or not 0.1 <= rate_hz <= 100:\n            raise ValueError('rate_hz must be a finite float in [0.1, 100]')\n\n        self.pub_ = self.create_publisher(String, 'chatter', 10)\n        self.timer_ = self.create_timer(1.0 / rate_hz, self.publish)\n        self.rate_hz_ = rate_hz\n\n        # This callback fires whenever someone runs `ros2 param set`.\n        self.add_on_set_parameters_callback(self.on_param_change)\n\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Started with message=\"{self.message_}\", rate={rate_hz} Hz')\n\n    def on_param_change(self, params):\n        # Validate the whole transaction before applying any side effect.\n        for p in params:\n            if p.name == 'rate_hz' and (\n                    not isinstance(p.value, float) or not math.isfinite(p.value)\n                    or not 0.1 <= p.value <= 100.0):\n                return SetParametersResult(\n                    successful=False, reason='rate_hz must be a float in [0.1, 100]')\n            if p.name == 'message' and not isinstance(p.value, str):\n                return SetParametersResult(successful=False, reason='message must be a string')\n        return SetParametersResult(successful=True)\n\n    def apply_param_change(self, params):\n        for p in params:\n            if p.name == 'message' and p.value != self.message_:\n                self.message_ = p.value\n                self.get_logger().info(f'message updated to \"{self.message_}\"')\n            elif p.name == 'rate_hz' and p.value != self.rate_hz_:\n                self.destroy_timer(self.timer_)\n                self.rate_hz_ = p.value\n                self.timer_ = self.create_timer(1.0 / p.value, self.publish)\n\n    def publish(self):\n        # Read committed parameters here, after\n        # validation/acceptance, and apply changes at the next existing timer tick.\n        self.apply_param_change([self.get_parameter('message'), self.get_parameter('rate_hz')])\n        msg = String()\n        msg.data = self.message_\n        self.pub_.publish(msg)\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = ParamsDemo()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/phone_bridge.py","body":"\"\"\"Authenticated WSS receiver for the native Android sensor app.\n\nThe server factory accepts a test sink; ROS is imported only by main/RosSink.\n\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport asyncio\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport secrets\nimport ssl\nimport threading\nimport time\n\nfrom aiohttp import web, WSMsgType\nfrom .phone_protocol import Session\n\n\ndef create_app(sink, token):\n    active = {'ws': None}\n\n    async def websocket(request):\n        origin = request.headers.get('Origin', '')\n        if origin:\n            raise web.HTTPForbidden(text='Origin not allowed')\n        ws = web.WebSocketResponse(max_msg_size=1_000_000, heartbeat=20, receive_timeout=30)\n        await ws.prepare(request)\n        claimed = False\n        session = Session()\n        try:\n            hello = await ws.receive_json(timeout=10)\n            supplied = hello.get('token') if isinstance(hello, dict) else None\n            if (not isinstance(supplied, str) or len(supplied) > 256\n                    or hello.get('type') != 'hello' or hello.get('version') != 2\n                    or hello.get('client') != 'android-camera2'\n                    or not secrets.compare_digest(supplied, token)):\n                await ws.close(code=1008, message=b'Invalid pairing')\n                return ws\n            if active['ws'] is not None:\n                await ws.close(code=1008, message=b'Laptop already paired')\n                return ws\n            active['ws'] = ws\n            claimed = True\n            sink.session_started()\n            metadata = {k: str(hello.get(k, 'unknown'))[:180] for k in\n                        ('camera_id', 'width', 'height', 'sensor_orientation', 'camera_clock', 'focus_mode')}\n            sink.diagnostic({'client': 'android-camera2', 'capture': metadata,\n                             'imu_frame': 'phone_android_device', 'imu_fused': False})\n            await ws.send_json({'type': 'ready', 'version': 2})\n            window_start, count = time.monotonic(), 0\n            async for incoming in ws:\n                if incoming.type != WSMsgType.TEXT:\n                    if incoming.type in (WSMsgType.CLOSE, WSMsgType.ERROR):\n                        break\n                    raise ValueError('Only JSON text messages are accepted')\n                current = time.monotonic()\n                if current - window_start >= 1:\n                    window_start, count = current, 0\n                count += 1\n                if count > 150:\n                    raise ValueError('Rate limit exceeded')\n                data = json.loads(incoming.data)\n                if not isinstance(data, dict):\n                    raise ValueError('Expected a JSON object')\n                if data.get('type') == 'sync':\n                    ident = data.get('id')\n                    if not isinstance(ident, str) or len(ident) > 32:\n                        raise ValueError('Invalid sync id')\n                    await ws.send_json({'type': 'sync', 'id': ident,\n                                        'server_ms': sink.now_ns() / 1_000_000})\n                elif data.get('type') == 'clock':\n                    rtt = session.set_clock(data)\n                    sink.diagnostic({'clock_rtt_ms': rtt, 'timestamp_quality':\n                                     'android_sensor_events_camera_' + str(hello.get('camera_clock', 'unknown'))[:40]})\n                    await ws.send_json({'type': 'clock_ready'})\n                else:\n                    sink.publish(session.sensor(data, sink.now_ns()))\n        except (ValueError, TypeError, KeyError, asyncio.TimeoutError) as exc:\n            if not ws.closed:\n                await ws.send_json({'type': 'error', 'message': str(exc)[:180]})\n                await ws.close(code=1008, message=b'Invalid or timed out sensor session')\n        finally:\n            if claimed:\n                active['ws'] = None\n                sink.diagnostic({'connection': 'disconnected'})\n        return ws\n\n    app = web.Application(client_max_size=1_000_000)\n    app.router.add_get('/ws/android', websocket)\n\n    async def shutdown(_):\n        if active['ws'] is not None:\n            await active['ws'].close(code=1001, message=b'Receiver stopping')\n    app.on_shutdown.append(shutdown)\n    return app\n\n\nclass RosSink:\n    def __init__(self, node, calibration):\n        import cv2\n        import numpy as np\n        import yaml\n        from cv_bridge import CvBridge\n        from sensor_msgs.msg import Image, CompressedImage, CameraInfo, Imu\n        from std_msgs.msg import String\n        from rclpy.qos import qos_profile_sensor_data\n        self.node, self.cv2, self.np, self.bridge = node, cv2, np, CvBridge()\n        self.types = (CompressedImage, CameraInfo, Imu, String)\n        self.image = node.create_publisher(Image, '/phone/camera/image_raw', qos_profile_sensor_data)\n        self.compressed = node.create_publisher(CompressedImage, '/phone/camera/image_raw/compressed', qos_profile_sensor_data)\n        self.info = node.create_publisher(CameraInfo, '/phone/camera/camera_info', qos_profile_sensor_data)\n        self.accel = node.create_publisher(Imu, '/phone/imu/accelerometer', qos_profile_sensor_data)\n        self.gyro = node.create_publisher(Imu, '/phone/imu/gyroscope', qos_profile_sensor_data)\n        self.diag = node.create_publisher(String, '/phone/diagnostics', 10)\n        self.calibration = None\n        if calibration:\n            self.calibration = yaml.safe_load(Path(calibration).read_text())\n            for key, size in [('camera_matrix', 9), ('rectification_matrix', 9), ('projection_matrix', 12)]:\n                values = self.calibration[key]['data']\n                if len(values) != size or not all(np.isfinite(v) for v in values):\n                    raise ValueError('Malformed calibration: ' + key)\n            if self.calibration['camera_matrix']['data'][0] <= 0:\n                raise ValueError('Calibration focal length must be positive')\n\n    def now_ns(self):\n        return self.node.get_clock().now().nanoseconds\n\n    def session_started(self):\n        self.diagnostic({'connection': 'paired',\n                         'imu_is_calibrated': False, 'camera_is_calibrated': bool(self.calibration)})\n\n    def diagnostic(self, values):\n        msg = self.types[3]()\n        msg.data = json.dumps(values)\n        self.diag.publish(msg)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def stamp(header, ns, frame):\n        header.stamp.sec, header.stamp.nanosec = divmod(ns, 1_000_000_000)\n        header.frame_id = frame\n\n    def publish(self, sample):\n        CompressedImage, CameraInfo, Imu, _ = self.types\n        if sample['type'] in ('accel', 'gyro'):\n            msg = Imu()\n            self.stamp(msg.header, sample['stamp_ns'], 'phone_android_device')\n            msg.orientation_covariance[0] = -1.0\n            if sample['type'] == 'accel':\n                msg.angular_velocity_covariance[0] = -1.0\n                msg.linear_acceleration.x, msg.linear_acceleration.y, msg.linear_acceleration.z = sample['values']\n                self.accel.publish(msg)\n            else:\n                msg.linear_acceleration_covariance[0] = -1.0\n                msg.angular_velocity.x, msg.angular_velocity.y, msg.angular_velocity.z = sample['values']\n                self.gyro.publish(msg)\n            return\n        pixels = self.cv2.imdecode(self.np.frombuffer(sample['jpeg'], self.np.uint8), self.cv2.IMREAD_COLOR)\n        if pixels is None or pixels.shape[:2] != (sample['height'], sample['width']):\n            raise ValueError('Decoded JPEG dimensions do not match declared dimensions')\n        msg = self.bridge.cv2_to_imgmsg(pixels, encoding='bgr8')\n        self.stamp(msg.header, sample['stamp_ns'], 'phone_camera_optical_frame')\n        info = CameraInfo()\n        info.header = msg.header\n        info.width, info.height = sample['width'], sample['height']\n        info.distortion_model = 'plumb_bob'\n        if self.calibration:\n            c = self.calibration\n            if (c['image_width'], c['image_height']) != (info.width, info.height):\n                raise ValueError('Calibration resolution mismatch; restore calibrated phone mode')\n            info.distortion_model = c['distortion_model']\n            info.d = [float(v) for v in c['distortion_coefficients']['data']]\n            info.k = [float(v) for v in c['camera_matrix']['data']]\n            info.r = [float(v) for v in c['rectification_matrix']['data']]\n            info.p = [float(v) for v in c['projection_matrix']['data']]\n        compressed = CompressedImage()\n        compressed.header, compressed.format, compressed.data = msg.header, 'jpeg', sample['jpeg']\n        self.image.publish(msg)\n        self.compressed.publish(compressed)\n        self.info.publish(info)\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    import rclpy\n    from rclpy.executors import SingleThreadedExecutor\n    from rclpy.node import Node\n    from rclpy.signals import SignalHandlerOptions\n    from rclpy.utilities import remove_ros_args\n    import sys\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description='Native Android WSS ROS receiver: USB or wireless')\n    parser.add_argument('--cert', required=True)\n    parser.add_argument('--key', required=True)\n    parser.add_argument('--host', default='0.0.0.0')\n    parser.add_argument('--port', type=int, default=8443)\n    parser.add_argument('--calibration', default='')\n    config = parser.parse_args(remove_ros_args(args=sys.argv)[1:])\n    tls = ssl.SSLContext(ssl.PROTOCOL_TLS_SERVER)\n    tls.minimum_version = ssl.TLSVersion.TLSv1_2\n    tls.load_cert_chain(config.cert, config.key)\n    import hashlib\n    pem = Path(config.cert).read_text()\n    certificate = pem[:pem.index('-----END CERTIFICATE-----') + len('-----END CERTIFICATE-----')]\n    pin = hashlib.sha256(ssl.PEM_cert_to_DER_cert(certificate)).hexdigest()\n    rclpy.init(args=args, signal_handler_options=SignalHandlerOptions.NO)\n    node = Node('phone_bridge')\n    if node.get_parameter('use_sim_time').value:\n        raise ValueError('Live phone bridge requires use_sim_time false')\n    sink = RosSink(node, config.calibration)\n    token = secrets.token_urlsafe(24)\n    print('\\nPAIRING TOKEN (this session only): ' + token, flush=True)\n    print('ANDROID SERVER CERTIFICATE SHA-256: ' + pin, flush=True)\n    print(f'Android: wss://YOUR_LAPTOP_ADDRESS:{config.port}/ws/android', flush=True)\n    print(f'USB (after adb reverse): wss://127.0.0.1:{config.port}/ws/android', flush=True)\n    print('Wireless: use the laptop address on the phone hotspot. Keep ROS local.', flush=True)\n    executor = SingleThreadedExecutor()\n    executor.add_node(node)\n    thread = threading.Thread(target=executor.spin, daemon=True)\n    thread.start()\n    try:\n        web.run_app(create_app(sink, token),\n                    host=config.host, port=config.port, ssl_context=tls, access_log=None)\n    finally:\n        executor.shutdown()\n        thread.join(timeout=5)\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/phone_protocol.py","body":"\"\"\"ROS-independent validation for the Android v2 sensor protocol (Apache-2.0).\"\"\"\nimport base64\nimport math\n\nMAX_JPEG = 700_000\n\n\ndef number(value, name, low=-1e15, high=1e15):\n    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, (float, int)):\n        raise ValueError(name + ' must be numeric')\n    if not math.isfinite(value) or not low <= value <= high:\n        raise ValueError(name + ' is outside the finite accepted range')\n    return float(value)\n\n\ndef vector(value, name, limit):\n    if not isinstance(value, list) or len(value) != 3:\n        raise ValueError(name + ' must contain three numbers')\n    return [number(v, name, -limit, limit) for v in value]\n\n\nclass Session:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.offset_ms = None\n        self.last_stamp = {}\n        self.last_sequence = {}\n\n    def set_clock(self, message):\n        self.offset_ms = number(message.get('offset_ms'), 'offset_ms')\n        return number(message.get('rtt_ms'), 'rtt_ms', 0, 2000)\n\n    def sensor(self, message, now_ns):\n        if self.offset_ms is None:\n            raise ValueError('Clock handshake is required before sensors')\n        kind = message.get('type')\n        if kind not in ('image', 'accel', 'gyro'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown sensor type')\n        seq = message.get('seq')\n        if type(seq) is not int or not 0 <= seq < 2**53 or seq <= self.last_sequence.get(kind, -1):\n            raise ValueError('Sequence must increase within each stream')\n        phone_ms = number(message.get('t_ms'), 't_ms', 0, 1e12)\n        stamp = int((phone_ms + self.offset_ms) * 1_000_000)\n        if not now_ns - 5_000_000_000 <= stamp <= now_ns + 250_000_000:\n            raise ValueError('Timestamp is stale or in the future; reconnect and re-sync')\n        if stamp <= self.last_stamp.get(kind, -1):\n            raise ValueError('Timestamps must increase within each stream')\n        result = {'type': kind, 'stamp_ns': stamp, 'seq': seq}\n        if kind in ('accel', 'gyro'):\n            result['values'] = vector(message.get('values'), 'values', 250 if kind == 'accel' else 100)\n        else:\n            width, height = message.get('width'), message.get('height')\n            if type(width) is not int or type(height) is not int or not 1 <= width <= 640 or not 1 <= height <= 640:\n                raise ValueError('Image dimensions must be positive integers <=640')\n            jpeg = message.get('jpeg')\n            if not isinstance(jpeg, str) or len(jpeg) > MAX_JPEG * 4 // 3 + 8:\n                raise ValueError('JPEG message is too large or missing')\n            try:\n                data = base64.b64decode(jpeg, validate=True)\n            except Exception as exc:\n                raise ValueError('Invalid base64 JPEG') from exc\n            if len(data) > MAX_JPEG or not data.startswith(b'\\xff\\xd8') or not data.endswith(b'\\xff\\xd9'):\n                raise ValueError('Invalid JPEG envelope')\n            result.update(width=width, height=height, jpeg=data)\n        self.last_stamp[kind] = stamp\n        self.last_sequence[kind] = seq\n        return result\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/robot_status_publisher.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 2 / 4 - publishing a CUSTOM message type from ros2_course_interfaces.\n\nRun:   ros2 run ros2_course_py robot_status_publisher\nCheck: ros2 topic echo /robot_status\n       ros2 interface show ros2_course_interfaces/msg/RobotStatus\n\"\"\"\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom ros2_course_interfaces.msg import RobotStatus\n\n\nclass RobotStatusPublisher(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('robot_status_publisher')\n\n        self.pub_ = self.create_publisher(RobotStatus, 'robot_status', 10)\n        self.battery_ = 100.0\n\n        self.timer_ = self.create_timer(1.0, self.publish_status)\n        self.get_logger().info('Robot status publisher started.')\n\n    def publish_status(self):\n        msg = RobotStatus()\n        msg.robot_name = 'tb3_01'\n        msg.battery_percent = self.battery_\n        msg.is_moving = self.battery_ > 20.0\n        msg.fault_code = 0 if self.battery_ > 10.0 else 7\n\n        self.pub_.publish(msg)\n        self.get_logger().info(\n            f'battery={msg.battery_percent:.1f}%  moving={msg.is_moving}'\n        )\n\n        self.battery_ = max(0.0, self.battery_ - 2.5)\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = RobotStatusPublisher()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/scan_stats.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 5 - reading a real sensor topic: sensor_msgs/msg/LaserScan.\n\nPrints the closest obstacle and the distance straight ahead, once per second.\n\nRun:  ros2 run ros2_course_py scan_stats\n      ros2 run ros2_course_py scan_stats --ros-args -p use_sim_time:=true   # in Gazebo\n\nKEY IDEA -- a LaserScan is ONE array of distances, plus the geometry needed to\nknow which direction each number points in:\n\n    angle of ranges[i] = angle_min + i * angle_increment      (radians, CCW)\n\n    ranges[0]                 -> angle_min   (often straight behind or right)\n    ranges[len//2]            -> the middle of the sweep\n    index for angle theta     -> int((theta - angle_min) / angle_increment)\n\nValues may be inf or nan -- ALWAYS filter them out before doing maths.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom rclpy.qos import qos_profile_sensor_data\nfrom sensor_msgs.msg import LaserScan\n\n\nclass ScanStats(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('scan_stats')\n\n        # qos_profile_sensor_data == BEST_EFFORT reliability + small queue.\n        # Most real drivers publish sensor data BEST_EFFORT. If you subscribe with\n        # the default RELIABLE profile you will silently receive NOTHING.\n        self.sub_ = self.create_subscription(\n            LaserScan, 'scan', self.callback_scan, qos_profile_sensor_data\n        )\n\n        self.last_scan_ = None\n        self.timer_ = self.create_timer(1.0, self.report)\n        self.get_logger().info('Waiting for /scan ...')\n\n    def callback_scan(self, msg: LaserScan):\n        self.last_scan_ = msg\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def range_at_angle(scan: LaserScan, angle_rad: float):\n        \"\"\"Return the measured distance at `angle_rad`, or None if unusable.\"\"\"\n        if not scan.ranges or scan.angle_increment <= 0.0:\n            return None\n        if angle_rad < scan.angle_min or angle_rad > scan.angle_max:\n            return None\n        index = int(round((angle_rad - scan.angle_min) / scan.angle_increment))\n        index = max(0, min(index, len(scan.ranges) - 1))\n        value = scan.ranges[index]\n        if math.isinf(value) or math.isnan(value):\n            return None\n        if value < scan.range_min or value > scan.range_max:\n            return None\n        return value\n\n    def report(self):\n        scan = self.last_scan_\n        if scan is None:\n            self.get_logger().warn('No scan received yet.')\n            return\n\n        valid = [\n            r for r in scan.ranges\n            if not math.isinf(r) and not math.isnan(r)\n            and scan.range_min <= r <= scan.range_max\n        ]\n\n        if not valid:\n            self.get_logger().warn('Scan received but every reading was invalid.')\n            return\n\n        front = self.range_at_angle(scan, 0.0)\n        front_txt = f'{front:.2f} m' if front is not None else 'no return'\n\n        self.get_logger().info(\n            f'beams={len(scan.ranges)}  valid={len(valid)}  '\n            f'closest={min(valid):.2f} m  farthest={max(valid):.2f} m  '\n            f'front(0 rad)={front_txt}  '\n            f'fov={math.degrees(scan.angle_max - scan.angle_min):.0f} deg'\n        )\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = ScanStats()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/talker.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 2 - the simplest possible publisher.\n\nRun it with:      ros2 run ros2_course_py talker\nWatch the topic:  ros2 topic echo /chatter\n\"\"\"\n\nimport rclpy                                  # the ROS 2 Python client library\nfrom rclpy.node import Node                   # base class for every Python node\nfrom std_msgs.msg import String               # the message type we will publish\n\n\nclass TalkerNode(Node):\n    \"\"\"A node that publishes a String on /chatter twice per second.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        # \"py_talker\" is the DEFAULT node name. It can be changed at runtime with\n        #   ros2 run ros2_course_py talker --ros-args -r __node:=some_other_name\n        super().__init__('py_talker')\n\n        # create_publisher(msg_type, topic_name, qos_depth)\n        # qos_depth = 10 means \"keep the last 10 messages if the subscriber is slow\"\n        self.publisher_ = self.create_publisher(String, 'chatter', 10)\n\n        self.counter_ = 0\n\n        # A timer is how you do \"run this every N seconds\" in ROS 2.\n        # A timer lets the executor dispatch periodic work alongside other callbacks.\n        # Sleeping inside a callback occupies its executor thread.\n        self.timer_ = self.create_timer(0.5, self.publish_message)\n\n        # get_logger() gives you the ROS logging system: it goes to the console AND\n        # to /rosout, so other tools can see it.\n        self.get_logger().info('Talker has been started.')\n\n    def publish_message(self):\n        msg = String()\n        msg.data = f'Hello ROS 2! count = {self.counter_}'\n        self.publisher_.publish(msg)\n        self.get_logger().info(f'Publishing: \"{msg.data}\"')\n        self.counter_ += 1\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)          # 1. start up the ROS 2 client library\n    node = TalkerNode()            # 2. create the node\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)           # 3. block here, letting timers/callbacks fire\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()        # 4. clean up\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/tf_echo_demo.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 4/5 - reading the TF tree from code.\n\nPrints where the robot (base_link) is inside the map, once per second.\n\nRun it while SLAM is running:\n    ros2 run ros2_course_py tf_echo_demo --ros-args -p use_sim_time:=true\n\nEquivalent one-liner from the terminal:\n    ros2 run tf2_ros tf2_echo map base_link\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom rclpy.duration import Duration\n\nfrom tf2_ros import TransformException\nfrom tf2_ros.buffer import Buffer\nfrom tf2_ros.transform_listener import TransformListener\n\n\ndef yaw_from_quaternion(q):\n    \"\"\"Convert a quaternion to a yaw angle (rotation about Z) in radians.\"\"\"\n    siny_cosp = 2.0 * (q.w * q.z + q.x * q.y)\n    cosy_cosp = 1.0 - 2.0 * (q.y * q.y + q.z * q.z)\n    return math.atan2(siny_cosp, cosy_cosp)\n\n\nclass TfEchoDemo(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('tf_echo_demo')\n\n        self.declare_parameter('parent_frame', 'map')\n        self.declare_parameter('child_frame', 'base_link')\n\n        # The Buffer stores the last 10 s of transforms; the Listener fills it\n        # from /tf and /tf_static.\n        self.tf_buffer_ = Buffer()\n        self.tf_listener_ = TransformListener(self.tf_buffer_, self)\n\n        self.timer_ = self.create_timer(1.0, self.lookup)\n\n    def lookup(self):\n        parent = self.get_parameter('parent_frame').value\n        child = self.get_parameter('child_frame').value\n\n        try:\n            # rclpy.time.Time() (all zeros) means \"the latest available transform\".\n            tf = self.tf_buffer_.lookup_transform(\n                parent, child, rclpy.time.Time(), timeout=Duration(seconds=0.0)\n            )\n        except TransformException as ex:\n            self.get_logger().warn(f'Could not transform {parent} -> {child}: {ex}')\n            return\n\n        t = tf.transform.translation\n        yaw = yaw_from_quaternion(tf.transform.rotation)\n        self.get_logger().info(\n            f'{child} in {parent}: x={t.x:+.2f} y={t.y:+.2f} yaw={math.degrees(yaw):+.1f} deg'\n        )\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = TfEchoDemo()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/turtle_square.py","body":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nLecture 1/2 warm-up - make the turtlesim turtle drive a square.\n\nTerminal 1:  ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node\nTerminal 2:  ros2 run ros2_course_py turtle_square\n\nThis node is a pure publisher to /turtle1/cmd_vel. It shows a state machine\ndriven by a timer -- the standard way to write timed behaviour in ROS 2.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport rclpy\nfrom rclpy.node import Node\nfrom geometry_msgs.msg import Twist\n\n\nclass TurtleSquare(Node):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__('turtle_square')\n\n        self.declare_parameter('side_seconds', 2.0)\n        self.declare_parameter('turn_seconds', 1.6)\n\n        self.pub_ = self.create_publisher(Twist, '/turtle1/cmd_vel', 10)\n\n        self.state_ = 'DRIVE'\n        self.elapsed_ = 0.0\n        self.dt_ = 0.1\n        self.timer_ = self.create_timer(self.dt_, self.tick)\n\n    def tick(self):\n        cmd = Twist()\n        side = self.get_parameter('side_seconds').value\n        turn = self.get_parameter('turn_seconds').value\n\n        if self.state_ == 'DRIVE':\n            cmd.linear.x = 1.5\n            if self.elapsed_ >= side:\n                self.state_, self.elapsed_ = 'TURN', 0.0\n        else:\n            cmd.angular.z = 1.0\n            if self.elapsed_ >= turn:\n                self.state_, self.elapsed_ = 'DRIVE', 0.0\n\n        self.pub_.publish(cmd)\n        self.elapsed_ += self.dt_\n\n\ndef main(args=None):\n    rclpy.init(args=args)\n    node = TurtleSquare()\n    try:\n        rclpy.spin(node)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        node.pub_.publish(Twist())\n        node.destroy_node()\n        if rclpy.ok():\n            rclpy.shutdown()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/ros2_course_py/__init__.py","body":""},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/setup.cfg","body":"[develop]\nscript_dir=$base/lib/ros2_course_py\n[install]\ninstall_scripts=$base/lib/ros2_course_py\n"},{"file":"ros/workspace/src/ros2_course_py/setup.py","body":"import os\nfrom glob import glob\n\nfrom setuptools import find_packages, setup\n\npackage_name = 'ros2_course_py'\n\nsetup(\n    name=package_name,\n    version='1.0.0',\n    packages=find_packages(exclude=['test']),\n    data_files=[\n        ('share/ament_index/resource_index/packages',\n            ['resource/' + package_name]),\n        ('share/' + package_name, ['package.xml']),\n        # This line is what makes `ros2 launch ros2_course_py <file>` work.\n        (os.path.join('share', package_name, 'launch'), glob('launch/*launch.py')),\n    ],\n    install_requires=['setuptools'],\n    zip_safe=True,\n    maintainer='Instructor',\n    maintainer_email='instructor@example.com',\n    description='Python (rclpy) example nodes for the 5-session ROS 2 course.',\n    license='Apache-2.0',\n    tests_require=['pytest'],\n    entry_points={\n        # LEFT of '=' is the name you type after `ros2 run ros2_course_py ...`\n        # RIGHT of '=' is <python module path>:<function to call>\n        'console_scripts': [\n            'phone_bridge = ros2_course_py.phone_bridge:main',\n            'camera_audit           = ros2_course_py.camera_audit:main',\n            'talker                 = ros2_course_py.talker:main',\n            'listener               = ros2_course_py.listener:main',\n            'robot_status_publisher = ros2_course_py.robot_status_publisher:main',\n            'params_demo            = ros2_course_py.params_demo:main',\n            'area_server            = ros2_course_py.area_server:main',\n            'area_client            = ros2_course_py.area_client:main',\n            'count_until_server     = ros2_course_py.count_until_server:main',\n            'count_until_client     = ros2_course_py.count_until_client:main',\n            'scan_stats             = ros2_course_py.scan_stats:main',\n            'obstacle_stopper       = ros2_course_py.obstacle_stopper:main',\n            'tf_echo_demo           = ros2_course_py.tf_echo_demo:main',\n            'turtle_square          = ros2_course_py.turtle_square:main',\n        ],\n    },\n)\n"},{"file":"ros/slam/phone_orb_slam3/CMakeLists.txt","body":"cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)\nproject(phone_orb_slam3)\nset(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)\nset(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)\nset(ORB_SLAM3_ROOT \"\" CACHE PATH \"Absolute path to built upstream ORB_SLAM3\")\nif(NOT EXISTS \"${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT}/lib/libORB_SLAM3.so\")\n  message(FATAL_ERROR \"Build upstream ORB-SLAM3 first and pass -DORB_SLAM3_ROOT=/absolute/path\")\nendif()\nfind_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)\nfind_package(rclcpp REQUIRED)\nfind_package(sensor_msgs REQUIRED)\nfind_package(geometry_msgs REQUIRED)\nfind_package(nav_msgs REQUIRED)\nfind_package(std_msgs REQUIRED)\nfind_package(cv_bridge REQUIRED)\nfind_package(OpenCV 4 REQUIRED)\nfind_package(Eigen3 REQUIRED)\nfind_package(Pangolin REQUIRED)\nadd_executable(phone_mono src/phone_mono.cpp)\nament_target_dependencies(phone_mono rclcpp sensor_msgs geometry_msgs nav_msgs std_msgs cv_bridge)\ntarget_include_directories(phone_mono PRIVATE ${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT} ${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT}/include ${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT}/include/CameraModels ${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT}/Thirdparty/Sophus ${OpenCV_INCLUDE_DIRS} ${Pangolin_INCLUDE_DIRS})\ntarget_link_libraries(phone_mono ${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT}/lib/libORB_SLAM3.so ${OpenCV_LIBS} ${Pangolin_LIBRARIES} Eigen3::Eigen)\nset_target_properties(phone_mono PROPERTIES INSTALL_RPATH \"${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT}/lib;${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT}/Thirdparty/DBoW2/lib;${ORB_SLAM3_ROOT}/Thirdparty/g2o/lib\")\ninstall(TARGETS phone_mono DESTINATION lib/${PROJECT_NAME})\nament_package()\n"},{"file":"ros/slam/phone_orb_slam3/package.xml","body":"<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\n<package format=\"3\"><name>phone_orb_slam3</name><version>0.1.0</version><description>Optional monocular phone adapter for upstream ORB-SLAM3, ROS 2 Jazzy.</description><maintainer email=\"instructor@example.com\">Course instructor</maintainer><license>GPL-3.0-or-later</license><buildtool_depend>ament_cmake</buildtool_depend><depend>rclcpp</depend><depend>sensor_msgs</depend><depend>geometry_msgs</depend><depend>nav_msgs</depend><depend>std_msgs</depend><depend>cv_bridge</depend><export><build_type>ament_cmake</build_type></export></package>\n"},{"file":"ros/slam/phone_orb_slam3/src/phone_mono.cpp","body":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later\r\n// Course adapter to ORB-SLAM3's public API. Native IMU components are inspection topics; this adapter uses images.\r\n#include <cmath>\r\n#include <memory>\r\n#include <stdexcept>\r\n#include <string>\r\n#include <vector>\r\n#include <Eigen/Geometry>\r\n#include \"rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp\"\r\n#include \"cv_bridge/cv_bridge.hpp\"\r\n#include \"sensor_msgs/msg/image.hpp\"\r\n#include \"sensor_msgs/msg/point_cloud2.hpp\"\r\n#include \"sensor_msgs/point_cloud2_iterator.hpp\"\r\n#include \"geometry_msgs/msg/pose_stamped.hpp\"\r\n#include \"nav_msgs/msg/path.hpp\"\r\n#include \"std_msgs/msg/string.hpp\"\r\n#include \"System.h\"\r\n#include \"MapPoint.h\"\r\n\r\nclass PhoneMono : public rclcpp::Node {\r\npublic:\r\n  PhoneMono() : Node(\"phone_mono\") {\r\n    const auto vocabulary = declare_parameter<std::string>(\"vocabulary\", \"\");\r\n    const auto settings = declare_parameter<std::string>(\"settings\", \"\");\r\n    output_ = declare_parameter<std::string>(\"keyframe_output\", \"phone-keyframes.txt\");\r\n    if(vocabulary.empty() || settings.empty()) throw std::runtime_error(\"Pass real vocabulary and calibrated settings paths\");\r\n    cv::FileStorage config(settings, cv::FileStorage::READ);\r\n    if(!config.isOpened()) throw std::runtime_error(\"Cannot open calibrated ORB settings\");\r\n    width_ = static_cast<int>(config[\"Camera.width\"]);\r\n    height_ = static_cast<int>(config[\"Camera.height\"]);\r\n    double fx = static_cast<double>(config[\"Camera1.fx\"]);\r\n    if(width_<=0 || height_<=0 || !std::isfinite(fx) || fx<=0) throw std::runtime_error(\"Invalid camera dimensions or focal length\");\r\n    config.release();\r\n    slam_ = std::make_unique<ORB_SLAM3::System>(vocabulary, settings, ORB_SLAM3::System::MONOCULAR, true);\r\n    pose_ = create_publisher<geometry_msgs::msg::PoseStamped>(\"/phone/slam/camera_pose\", 5);\r\n    cloud_ = create_publisher<sensor_msgs::msg::PointCloud2>(\"/phone/slam/tracked_points\", 2);\r\n    path_ = create_publisher<nav_msgs::msg::Path>(\"/phone/slam/path\", 2);\r\n    status_ = create_publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>(\"/phone/slam/status\", 5);\r\n    subscription_ = create_subscription<sensor_msgs::msg::Image>(\"/phone/camera/image_raw\", rclcpp::SensorDataQoS().keep_last(2),\r\n      [this](sensor_msgs::msg::Image::ConstSharedPtr image){ process(image); });\r\n    RCLCPP_INFO(get_logger(), \"Monocular map has arbitrary scale. Pangolin shows the optimized sparse map; ROS cloud contains currently tracked points only.\");\r\n  }\r\n  ~PhoneMono() override {\r\n    if(slam_) {slam_->Shutdown(); if(!output_.empty()) slam_->SaveKeyFrameTrajectoryTUM(output_);}\r\n  }\r\nprivate:\r\n  void process(const sensor_msgs::msg::Image::ConstSharedPtr & image) {\r\n    if(static_cast<int>(image->width)!=width_ || static_cast<int>(image->height)!=height_) {\r\n      RCLCPP_ERROR_THROTTLE(get_logger(), *get_clock(), 2000, \"Phone resolution changed. Restore calibrated mode and restart mapping.\"); return;\r\n    }\r\n    const double time=rclcpp::Time(image->header.stamp).seconds();\r\n    if(time<=last_time_) return;\r\n    try {\r\n      const auto pixels=cv_bridge::toCvShare(image, \"bgr8\");\r\n      const auto Tcw=slam_->TrackMonocular(pixels->image,time);\r\n      last_time_=time;\r\n      const int tracking=slam_->GetTrackingState();\r\n      std_msgs::msg::String report; report.data=\"ORB tracking state=\"+std::to_string(tracking)+\" (2=OK); units=arbitrary monocular scale\";status_->publish(report);\r\n      if(tracking!=2) {history_.poses.clear();return;}\r\n      const auto Twc=Tcw.inverse();\r\n      const Eigen::Vector3f t=Twc.translation();\r\n      const Eigen::Quaternionf q(Twc.rotationMatrix());\r\n      geometry_msgs::msg::PoseStamped pose;\r\n      pose.header=image->header;pose.header.frame_id=\"phone_slam_world\";\r\n      pose.pose.position.x=t.x();pose.pose.position.y=t.y();pose.pose.position.z=t.z();\r\n      pose.pose.orientation.x=q.x();pose.pose.orientation.y=q.y();pose.pose.orientation.z=q.z();pose.pose.orientation.w=q.w();pose_->publish(pose);\r\n      // Diagnostic pose history is bounded and not retroactively loop-optimized.\r\n      history_.header=pose.header;history_.poses.push_back(pose);\r\n      if(history_.poses.size()>2000) history_.poses.erase(history_.poses.begin());\r\n      path_->publish(history_);\r\n      std::vector<Eigen::Vector3f> points;\r\n      for(auto *point:slam_->GetTrackedMapPoints()) if(point && !point->isBad()) {\r\n        const auto p=point->GetWorldPos();if(p.allFinite())points.push_back(p);\r\n      }\r\n      sensor_msgs::msg::PointCloud2 cloud;cloud.header=pose.header;\r\n      sensor_msgs::PointCloud2Modifier modifier(cloud);modifier.setPointCloud2FieldsByString(1,\"xyz\");modifier.resize(points.size());\r\n      if(!points.empty()) {\r\n        sensor_msgs::PointCloud2Iterator<float> x(cloud,\"x\"),y(cloud,\"y\"),z(cloud,\"z\");\r\n        for(const auto &p:points){*x=p.x();*y=p.y();*z=p.z();++x;++y;++z;}\r\n      }\r\n      cloud_->publish(cloud);\r\n    } catch(const cv_bridge::Exception &e){RCLCPP_ERROR(get_logger(),\"Image conversion: %s\",e.what());}\r\n  }\r\n  std::unique_ptr<ORB_SLAM3::System> slam_;\r\n  std::string output_;int width_=0,height_=0;double last_time_=-1;\r\n  nav_msgs::msg::Path history_;\r\n  rclcpp::Subscription<sensor_msgs::msg::Image>::SharedPtr subscription_;\r\n  rclcpp::Publisher<geometry_msgs::msg::PoseStamped>::SharedPtr pose_;\r\n  rclcpp::Publisher<sensor_msgs::msg::PointCloud2>::SharedPtr cloud_;\r\n  rclcpp::Publisher<nav_msgs::msg::Path>::SharedPtr path_;\r\n  rclcpp::Publisher<std_msgs::msg::String>::SharedPtr status_;\r\n};\r\nint main(int argc,char **argv){rclcpp::init(argc,argv);try{rclcpp::spin(std::make_shared<PhoneMono>());}catch(const std::exception &e){RCLCPP_ERROR(rclcpp::get_logger(\"phone_mono\"),\"%s\",e.what());if(rclcpp::ok())rclcpp::shutdown();return 1;}if(rclcpp::ok())rclcpp::shutdown();return 0;}\r\n"},{"file":"apps/android/app/build.gradle","body":"plugins { id 'com.android.application' }\nandroid {\n    namespace 'org.nodestomaps.sensor'\n    compileSdk 35\n    defaultConfig { applicationId 'org.nodestomaps.sensor'; minSdk 26; targetSdk 35; versionCode 1; versionName '0.1.0' }\n    compileOptions { sourceCompatibility JavaVersion.VERSION_1_8; targetCompatibility JavaVersion.VERSION_1_8 }\n}\n"},{"file":"apps/android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml","body":"<manifest xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\">\n  <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion=\"26\" android:targetSdkVersion=\"35\"/>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.CAMERA\"/>\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.INTERNET\"/>\n  <uses-feature android:name=\"android.hardware.camera.any\" android:required=\"true\"/>\n  <uses-feature android:name=\"android.hardware.sensor.gyroscope\" android:required=\"false\"/>\n  <application android:label=\"Nodes to Maps Sensor\" android:theme=\"@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar\" android:allowBackup=\"false\" android:usesCleartextTraffic=\"false\" android:supportsRtl=\"true\">\n    <activity android:name=\".MainActivity\" android:exported=\"true\" android:screenOrientation=\"portrait\">\n      <intent-filter><action android:name=\"android.intent.action.MAIN\"/><category android:name=\"android.intent.category.LAUNCHER\"/></intent-filter>\n    </activity>\n  </application>\n</manifest>\n"},{"file":"apps/android/app/src/main/java/org/nodestomaps/sensor/CameraFeed.java","body":"package org.nodestomaps.sensor;\n\nimport android.annotation.SuppressLint;\nimport android.content.Context;\nimport android.graphics.*;\nimport android.hardware.camera2.*;\nimport android.hardware.camera2.params.StreamConfigurationMap;\nimport android.media.*;\nimport android.os.*;\nimport android.util.Size;\nimport java.io.ByteArrayOutputStream;\nimport java.nio.ByteBuffer;\nimport java.util.*;\n\n/** Fixed camera ID and native, unrotated YUV geometry. Calibration applies to these pixels. */\nfinal class CameraFeed implements AutoCloseable {\n    interface Listener { void frame(byte[] jpeg,int width,int height,long timeNs); void failed(String reason); boolean ready(); }\n    final String id;\n    final Size size;\n    final int orientation;\n    final boolean realtime;\n    final String focusMode;\n    private final CameraManager manager;\n    private final Handler handler;\n    private final Listener listener;\n    private final CameraCharacteristics characteristics;\n    private ImageReader reader;\n    private CameraDevice device;\n    private CameraCaptureSession session;\n    private volatile boolean closed;\n    private long lastFrame;\n\n    CameraFeed(Context context,Handler handler,Listener listener) throws CameraAccessException {\n        manager=(CameraManager)context.getSystemService(Context.CAMERA_SERVICE);\n        this.handler=handler; this.listener=listener;\n        String chosen=null;\n        for(String camera:manager.getCameraIdList()) {\n            CameraCharacteristics c=manager.getCameraCharacteristics(camera);\n            Integer facing=c.get(CameraCharacteristics.LENS_FACING);\n            if(facing!=null && facing==CameraCharacteristics.LENS_FACING_BACK) {\n                if(chosen==null) chosen=camera;\n                Integer clock=c.get(CameraCharacteristics.SENSOR_INFO_TIMESTAMP_SOURCE);\n                if(clock!=null && clock==CameraCharacteristics.SENSOR_INFO_TIMESTAMP_SOURCE_REALTIME) {chosen=camera;break;}\n            }\n        }\n        if(chosen==null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"A rear camera is required.\");\n        id=chosen; characteristics=manager.getCameraCharacteristics(id);\n        Integer clock=characteristics.get(CameraCharacteristics.SENSOR_INFO_TIMESTAMP_SOURCE);\n        realtime=clock!=null && clock==CameraCharacteristics.SENSOR_INFO_TIMESTAMP_SOURCE_REALTIME;\n        Integer angle=characteristics.get(CameraCharacteristics.SENSOR_ORIENTATION); orientation=angle==null?0:angle;\n        StreamConfigurationMap map=characteristics.get(CameraCharacteristics.SCALER_STREAM_CONFIGURATION_MAP);\n        Size[] sizes=map==null?null:map.getOutputSizes(ImageFormat.YUV_420_888);\n        Size selected=null;\n        if(sizes!=null) for(Size s:sizes) if(s.getWidth()<=640 && s.getHeight()<=640 && s.getWidth()%2==0 && s.getHeight()%2==0\n                && (selected==null || s.getWidth()*s.getHeight()>selected.getWidth()*selected.getHeight())) selected=s;\n        if(selected==null) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Camera has no supported YUV stream with both edges <=640 px.\");\n        size=selected;\n        int[] modes=characteristics.get(CameraCharacteristics.CONTROL_AF_AVAILABLE_MODES);\n        focusMode=contains(modes,CaptureRequest.CONTROL_AF_MODE_CONTINUOUS_VIDEO)?\"continuous-video\":\"off/fixed\";\n    }\n    private static boolean contains(int[] values,int v) { if(values!=null)for(int x:values)if(x==v)return true;return false; }\n\n    @SuppressLint(\"MissingPermission\") void start() throws CameraAccessException {\n        reader=ImageReader.newInstance(size.getWidth(),size.getHeight(),ImageFormat.YUV_420_888,3);\n        reader.setOnImageAvailableListener(source->{\n            try(Image image=source.acquireLatestImage()) {\n                if(image==null || closed || !listener.ready())return;\n                long now=SystemClock.elapsedRealtimeNanos(); if(now-lastFrame<100_000_000L)return;lastFrame=now;\n                long stamp=realtime?image.getTimestamp():now;\n                byte[] nv21=toNv21(image);\n                ByteArrayOutputStream encoded=new ByteArrayOutputStream();\n                if(!new YuvImage(nv21,ImageFormat.NV21,size.getWidth(),size.getHeight(),null)\n                        .compressToJpeg(new Rect(0,0,size.getWidth(),size.getHeight()),75,encoded)) throw new IllegalStateException(\"JPEG encoding failed\");\n                listener.frame(encoded.toByteArray(),size.getWidth(),size.getHeight(),stamp);\n            } catch(Exception ex) {if(!closed)listener.failed(\"Camera conversion: \"+ex.getMessage());}\n        },handler);\n        manager.openCamera(id,new CameraDevice.StateCallback(){\n            public void onOpened(CameraDevice camera){\n                if(closed){camera.close();return;}device=camera;\n                try {\n                    camera.createCaptureSession(Collections.singletonList(reader.getSurface()),new CameraCaptureSession.StateCallback(){\n                        public void onConfigured(CameraCaptureSession capture){\n                            if(closed){capture.close();return;}session=capture;\n                            try {\n                                CaptureRequest.Builder request=device.createCaptureRequest(CameraDevice.TEMPLATE_RECORD);\n                                request.addTarget(reader.getSurface());\n                                request.set(CaptureRequest.CONTROL_AF_MODE,focusMode.equals(\"continuous-video\")?CaptureRequest.CONTROL_AF_MODE_CONTINUOUS_VIDEO:CaptureRequest.CONTROL_AF_MODE_OFF);\n                                request.set(CaptureRequest.CONTROL_VIDEO_STABILIZATION_MODE,CaptureRequest.CONTROL_VIDEO_STABILIZATION_MODE_OFF);\n                                int[] ois=characteristics.get(CameraCharacteristics.LENS_INFO_AVAILABLE_OPTICAL_STABILIZATION);\n                                if(ois!=null && ois.length>0)request.set(CaptureRequest.LENS_OPTICAL_STABILIZATION_MODE,CaptureRequest.LENS_OPTICAL_STABILIZATION_MODE_OFF);\n                                capture.setRepeatingRequest(request.build(),null,handler);\n                            }catch(Exception ex){listener.failed(\"Camera request: \"+ex.getMessage());}\n                        }\n                        public void onConfigureFailed(CameraCaptureSession capture){listener.failed(\"Camera capture configuration failed.\");}\n                    },handler);\n                }catch(Exception ex){listener.failed(\"Camera session: \"+ex.getMessage());}\n            }\n            public void onDisconnected(CameraDevice camera){camera.close();if(!closed)listener.failed(\"Camera disconnected.\");}\n            public void onError(CameraDevice camera,int error){camera.close();if(!closed)listener.failed(\"Camera error \"+error);}\n        },handler);\n    }\n\n    static byte[] toNv21(Image image) {\n        int w=image.getWidth(),h=image.getHeight(); byte[] result=new byte[w*h*3/2];\n        Image.Plane[] planes=image.getPlanes();\n        for(int p=0;p<3;p++) {\n            ByteBuffer bytes=planes[p].getBuffer(); int rows=p==0?h:h/2,cols=p==0?w:w/2;\n            int rowStride=planes[p].getRowStride(),pixelStride=planes[p].getPixelStride(),base=bytes.position();\n            for(int y=0;y<rows;y++)for(int x=0;x<cols;x++) {\n                int target=p==0?y*w+x:w*h+y*w+2*x+(p==1?1:0);\n                result[target]=bytes.get(base+y*rowStride+x*pixelStride);\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n    public void close(){closed=true;if(session!=null)session.close();if(device!=null)device.close();if(reader!=null)reader.close();}\n}\n"},{"file":"apps/android/app/src/main/java/org/nodestomaps/sensor/MainActivity.java","body":"package org.nodestomaps.sensor;\n\nimport android.Manifest;\nimport android.app.Activity;\nimport android.content.pm.PackageManager;\nimport android.graphics.*;\nimport android.hardware.*;\nimport android.os.*;\nimport android.text.InputType;\nimport android.view.*;\nimport android.widget.*;\nimport org.json.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\n/** Foreground-only sensor sender. No recording, analytics, account or persistent pairing data. */\npublic final class MainActivity extends Activity implements SensorEventListener {\n    private EditText address,token,pin;\n    private TextView status,metrics,capabilities;\n    private ImageView preview;\n    private Button start,stop;\n    private volatile HandlerThread worker;\n    private Handler handler;\n    private SensorManager sensors;\n    private CameraFeed camera;\n    private SensorSocket socket;\n    private volatile boolean running,clockReady;\n    private volatile int epoch;\n    private int syncIndex;\n    private double sentAt,bestRtt=Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY,bestOffset;\n    private final Map<Integer,Long> lastSensor=new HashMap<>();\n    private long images,accels,gyros,dropped,imageSeq,accelSeq,gyroSeq;\n\n    public void onCreate(Bundle state) {\n        super.onCreate(state);\n        sensors=(SensorManager)getSystemService(SENSOR_SERVICE);\n        ScrollView scroll=new ScrollView(this);scroll.setFitsSystemWindows(true);LinearLayout content=new LinearLayout(this);content.setOrientation(LinearLayout.VERTICAL);content.setPadding(28,30,28,30);content.setBackgroundColor(Color.rgb(247,245,236));scroll.addView(content);setContentView(scroll);\n        label(content,\"NODES TO MAPS / ANDROID SENSOR KIT\",14);\n        label(content,\"Your phone.\\nA robotics sensor.\",30);\n        label(content,\"Pair with your own laptop. Camera and sensor data travel only to that endpoint.\",16);\n        address=field(content,\"Secure receiver address\",\"wss://LAPTOP:8443/ws/android\",false);\n        token=field(content,\"Session token printed on the laptop\",\"Pairing token\",true);\n        pin=field(content,\"Server certificate SHA-256 printed on the laptop\",\"64 hexadecimal digits\",false);\n        label(content,\"USB: wss://127.0.0.1:8443/ws/android after adb reverse. Wi-Fi: use the laptop’s address on your phone hotspot.\",14);\n        start=new Button(this);start.setText(\"Start camera + sensors\");content.addView(start);start.setOnClickListener(v->begin());\n        stop=new Button(this);stop.setText(\"Stop & release sensors\");stop.setEnabled(false);content.addView(stop);stop.setOnClickListener(v->end(\"Stopped. Camera and sensors released.\"));\n        status=label(content,\"Not connected. Sensors are inactive.\",16);\n        capabilities=label(content,\"Camera mode and timestamp source appear after Start.\",14);\n        preview=new ImageView(this);preview.setScaleType(ImageView.ScaleType.FIT_CENTER);content.addView(preview,new LinearLayout.LayoutParams(-1,320));\n        metrics=label(content,\"Images 0 · Accel 0 · Gyro 0 · Skipped 0\",16);\n        label(content,\"10 fps image send target · each motion sensor up to 50 Hz. Preview uses the same unrotated pixels as ROS. Hold one orientation for calibration and mapping. Capture stops when this screen leaves the foreground.\",14);\n        label(content,\"Core project: sparse monocular mapping. Camera–IMU fusion requires measured calibration and a qualified timestamp source.\",14);\n    }\n    private TextView label(LinearLayout parent,String text,int size){TextView v=new TextView(this);v.setText(text);v.setTextSize(size);v.setTextColor(Color.rgb(24,57,46));v.setPadding(0,10,0,14);parent.addView(v);return v;}\n    private EditText field(LinearLayout parent,String label,String hint,boolean secret){label(parent,label,15);EditText v=new EditText(this);v.setSingleLine(true);v.setHint(hint);v.setTextSize(15);v.setInputType(secret?InputType.TYPE_CLASS_TEXT|InputType.TYPE_TEXT_VARIATION_PASSWORD:InputType.TYPE_CLASS_TEXT|InputType.TYPE_TEXT_VARIATION_VISIBLE_PASSWORD);v.setSaveEnabled(false);parent.addView(v);return v;}\n    private static JSONObject object(Object... pairs) throws JSONException {JSONObject o=new JSONObject();for(int i=0;i<pairs.length;i+=2)o.put((String)pairs[i],pairs[i+1]);return o;}\n    private double now(){return SystemClock.elapsedRealtimeNanos()/1e6;}\n    private void fail(int session,String reason){runOnUiThread(()->{if(epoch==session&&running)end(reason);});}\n    private void showCounts(int session){runOnUiThread(()->{if(epoch==session)metrics.setText(\"Images \"+images+\" · Accel \"+accels+\" · Gyro \"+gyros+\" · Skipped \"+dropped);});}\n    private void begin(){\n        if(running)return;\n        if(checkSelfPermission(Manifest.permission.CAMERA)!=PackageManager.PERMISSION_GRANTED){requestPermissions(new String[]{Manifest.permission.CAMERA},1);status.setText(\"Grant camera permission, then tap Start.\");return;}\n        if(token.getText().toString().trim().isEmpty()){status.setText(\"Enter the session token printed by phone_bridge.\");return;}\n        int session=++epoch;\n        final String pairingToken=token.getText().toString().trim();\n        try {\n            worker=new HandlerThread(\"camera-and-motion\");worker.start();handler=new Handler(worker.getLooper());\n            camera=new CameraFeed(this,handler,new CameraFeed.Listener(){\n                public boolean ready(){return running&&epoch==session&&clockReady&&socket!=null&&!socket.congested();}\n                public void failed(String reason){fail(session,reason);}\n                public void frame(byte[] jpeg,int width,int height,long timeNs){\n                    SensorSocket destination=socket;\n                    if(epoch!=session || !running || destination==null || !clockReady || destination.congested()){dropped++;return;}\n                    try {\n                        boolean accepted=destination.sendSensor(object(\"type\",\"image\",\"seq\",imageSeq++,\"t_ms\",timeNs/1e6,\"width\",width,\"height\",height,\"jpeg\",android.util.Base64.encodeToString(jpeg,android.util.Base64.NO_WRAP)).toString());\n                        if(accepted){images++;runOnUiThread(()->{if(epoch==session&&running){Bitmap bitmap=BitmapFactory.decodeByteArray(jpeg,0,jpeg.length);preview.setImageBitmap(bitmap);}});}else dropped++;\n                        showCounts(session);\n                    }catch(Exception ex){fail(session,\"Image packet: \"+ex.getMessage());}\n                }\n            });\n            capabilities.setText(\"Rear camera \"+camera.id+\" · \"+camera.size+\" · native unrotated image\\nClock: \"+(camera.realtime?\"REALTIME — camera and IMU share elapsed-realtime base\":\"UNKNOWN — image callback estimate; camera-only exercise\")+\"\\nFocus: \"+camera.focusMode+\" · stabilization requested OFF\");\n            final CameraFeed capture=camera;final Handler sessionHandler=handler;final SensorSocket[] connection=new SensorSocket[1];\n            connection[0]=new SensorSocket(address.getText().toString(),pin.getText().toString(),new SensorSocket.Listener(){\n                public void opened(){if(epoch!=session||!running)return;try{if(!connection[0].send(object(\"type\",\"hello\",\"version\",2,\"client\",\"android-camera2\",\"token\",pairingToken,\"camera_id\",capture.id,\"width\",capture.size.getWidth(),\"height\",capture.size.getHeight(),\"sensor_orientation\",capture.orientation,\"camera_clock\",capture.realtime?\"realtime\":\"callback_estimate\",\"focus_mode\",capture.focusMode).toString()))throw new IllegalStateException(\"Handshake queue full\");}catch(Exception ex){fail(session,ex.getMessage());}}\n                public void failed(String reason){fail(session,reason);}\n                public void text(String text){if(epoch==session&&running)sessionHandler.post(()->receive(session,text));}\n            });\n            socket=connection[0];\n            images=accels=gyros=dropped=imageSeq=accelSeq=gyroSeq=0;lastSensor.clear();syncIndex=0;bestRtt=Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY;clockReady=false;running=true;\n            start.setEnabled(false);stop.setEnabled(true);address.setEnabled(false);token.setEnabled(false);pin.setEnabled(false);\n            getWindow().addFlags(WindowManager.LayoutParams.FLAG_KEEP_SCREEN_ON);status.setText(\"Connecting with the pinned server certificate…\");socket.connect();\n        }catch(Exception ex){end(\"Setup: \"+ex.getMessage());}\n    }\n    private void receive(int session,String text){\n        if(!running||epoch!=session)return;\n        try {\n            JSONObject message=new JSONObject(text);String type=message.getString(\"type\");\n            if(type.equals(\"ready\")){sendSync();}\n            else if(type.equals(\"sync\")){\n                if(!String.valueOf(syncIndex).equals(message.getString(\"id\")))throw new IllegalStateException(\"Unexpected clock reply\");\n                double t1=now(),rtt=t1-sentAt;if(rtt<bestRtt){bestRtt=rtt;bestOffset=message.getDouble(\"server_ms\")-(sentAt+t1)/2;}\n                syncIndex++;if(syncIndex<7)sendSync();else if(bestRtt>2000)throw new IllegalStateException(\"Clock RTT too large. Check the connection.\");\n                else socket.send(object(\"type\",\"clock\",\"offset_ms\",bestOffset,\"rtt_ms\",bestRtt).toString());\n            } else if(type.equals(\"clock_ready\")){\n                clockReady=true;\n                Sensor a=sensors.getDefaultSensor(Sensor.TYPE_ACCELEROMETER),g=sensors.getDefaultSensor(Sensor.TYPE_GYROSCOPE);\n                boolean hasA=a!=null&&sensors.registerListener(this,a,20_000,handler),hasG=g!=null&&sensors.registerListener(this,g,20_000,handler);\n                camera.start();\n                runOnUiThread(()->{if(epoch==session&&running)status.setText(String.format(Locale.ROOT,\"Streaming · clock RTT %.1f ms · accel %s · gyro %s\",bestRtt,hasA?\"available\":\"missing\",hasG?\"available\":\"missing\"));});\n            } else if(type.equals(\"error\"))fail(session,\"Receiver: \"+message.optString(\"message\"));\n        }catch(Exception ex){fail(session,\"Protocol: \"+ex.getMessage());}\n    }\n    private void sendSync() throws JSONException {sentAt=now();if(!socket.send(object(\"type\",\"sync\",\"id\",String.valueOf(syncIndex),\"t0\",sentAt).toString()))throw new IllegalStateException(\"Sync queue full\");}\n    public void onSensorChanged(SensorEvent event){\n        if(Thread.currentThread()!=worker)return;\n        int session=epoch;SensorSocket current=socket;\n        if(!running||!clockReady||current==null||current.congested())return;\n        int type=event.sensor.getType();if(type!=Sensor.TYPE_ACCELEROMETER&&type!=Sensor.TYPE_GYROSCOPE)return;\n        if(SystemClock.elapsedRealtimeNanos()-event.timestamp>250_000_000L){dropped++;return;}\n        Long last=lastSensor.get(type);if(last!=null&&event.timestamp-last<20_000_000L)return;\n        if(event.values.length<3)return;for(int i=0;i<3;i++)if(Float.isNaN(event.values[i])||Float.isInfinite(event.values[i]))return;\n        lastSensor.put(type,event.timestamp);\n        try {\n            boolean accel=type==Sensor.TYPE_ACCELEROMETER;\n            if(current.sendSensor(object(\"type\",accel?\"accel\":\"gyro\",\"seq\",accel?accelSeq++:gyroSeq++,\"t_ms\",event.timestamp/1e6,\"values\",new JSONArray(new float[]{event.values[0],event.values[1],event.values[2]})).toString())){if(accel)accels++;else gyros++;}else dropped++;\n            if((accels+gyros)%25==0)showCounts(session);\n        }catch(Exception ex){fail(session,\"Motion packet: \"+ex.getMessage());}\n    }\n    public void onAccuracyChanged(Sensor sensor,int accuracy){}\n    private void end(String message){\n        running=false;clockReady=false;epoch++;\n        sensors.unregisterListener(this);\n        if(socket!=null){socket.close();socket=null;}if(camera!=null){camera.close();camera=null;}\n        if(worker!=null){worker.quitSafely();worker=null;}\n        getWindow().clearFlags(WindowManager.LayoutParams.FLAG_KEEP_SCREEN_ON);\n        if(start!=null){start.setEnabled(true);stop.setEnabled(false);address.setEnabled(true);token.setEnabled(true);pin.setEnabled(true);status.setText(message);preview.setImageDrawable(null);}\n    }\n    protected void onPause(){end(\"Paused. Camera and sensors released. Tap Start to reconnect.\");super.onPause();}\n}\n"},{"file":"apps/android/app/src/main/java/org/nodestomaps/sensor/SensorSocket.java","body":"package org.nodestomaps.sensor;\n\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.net.*;\nimport java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;\nimport java.security.*;\nimport java.security.cert.X509Certificate;\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.util.concurrent.*;\nimport javax.net.ssl.*;\n\n/** Small bounded RFC 6455 text client over a pinned TLS connection. No external libraries. */\nfinal class SensorSocket implements AutoCloseable {\n    interface Listener { void opened(); void text(String message); void failed(String message); }\n    private final URI endpoint;\n    private final String pin;\n    private final Listener listener;\n    private final BlockingQueue<byte[]> outgoing = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(16);\n    private final SecureRandom random = new SecureRandom();\n    private volatile boolean closed;\n    private volatile SSLSocket socket;\n    private DataOutputStream output;\n    private double sensorTokens=10;\n    private long budgetTime=System.nanoTime();\n    private final Object budgetLock=new Object();\n\n    SensorSocket(String address, String fingerprint, Listener listener) {\n        endpoint = URI.create(address.trim());\n        pin = fingerprint.replace(\":\", \"\").replace(\" \", \"\").toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);\n        if (!\"wss\".equals(endpoint.getScheme()) || endpoint.getHost() == null\n                || !\"/ws/android\".equals(endpoint.getPath()) || endpoint.getUserInfo() != null\n                || endpoint.getQuery() != null || endpoint.getFragment() != null)\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Use wss://LAPTOP:8443/ws/android\");\n        if (!pin.matches(\"[a-f0-9]{64}\")) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Paste the 64-digit certificate SHA-256 from the laptop.\");\n        this.listener = listener;\n    }\n\n    void connect() { new Thread(this::run, \"sensor-tls-reader\").start(); }\n    boolean send(String message) {\n        byte[] bytes = message.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);\n        return !closed && bytes.length <= 950_000 && outgoing.offer(bytes);\n    }\n    boolean congested() { return closed || outgoing.size() >= 3; }\n    boolean sendSensor(String message) {\n        synchronized(budgetLock) {\n            long now=System.nanoTime();\n            sensorTokens=Math.min(10,sensorTokens+(now-budgetTime)*120.0/1e9);budgetTime=now;\n            if(sensorTokens<1)return false;\n            sensorTokens-=1;\n            return send(message);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void run() {\n        try {\n            TrustManager[] trust = {new X509TrustManager() {\n                public X509Certificate[] getAcceptedIssuers() { return new X509Certificate[0]; }\n                public void checkClientTrusted(X509Certificate[] c, String a) throws java.security.cert.CertificateException { throw new java.security.cert.CertificateException(\"Client authentication unsupported\"); }\n                public void checkServerTrusted(X509Certificate[] chain, String auth) throws java.security.cert.CertificateException {\n                    try {\n                        if (chain.length == 0) throw new GeneralSecurityException(\"Missing certificate\");\n                        chain[0].checkValidity();\n                        byte[] digest = MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\").digest(chain[0].getEncoded());\n                        StringBuilder actual = new StringBuilder();\n                        for (byte b : digest) actual.append(String.format(Locale.ROOT, \"%02x\", b & 255));\n                        if (!MessageDigest.isEqual(actual.toString().getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII), pin.getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII)))\n                            throw new GeneralSecurityException(\"Server certificate pin mismatch\");\n                    } catch (GeneralSecurityException ex) { throw new java.security.cert.CertificateException(ex); }\n                }\n            }};\n            SSLContext tls = SSLContext.getInstance(\"TLS\"); tls.init(null, trust, random);\n            SSLSocket connection = (SSLSocket) tls.getSocketFactory().createSocket();\n            socket = connection;\n            if (closed) { connection.close(); return; }\n            int port = endpoint.getPort() < 0 ? 443 : endpoint.getPort();\n            connection.connect(new InetSocketAddress(endpoint.getHost(), port), 10_000);\n            connection.setSoTimeout(35_000);\n            // The exact leaf pin is the server identity. No global trust-store changes.\n            connection.startHandshake();\n            InputStream input = connection.getInputStream();\n            output = new DataOutputStream(connection.getOutputStream());\n            byte[] nonce = new byte[16]; random.nextBytes(nonce);\n            String key = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(nonce);\n            String host = endpoint.getHost().contains(\":\") ? \"[\" + endpoint.getHost() + \"]\" : endpoint.getHost();\n            String request = \"GET /ws/android HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: \" + host + \":\" + port\n                    + \"\\r\\nUpgrade: websocket\\r\\nConnection: Upgrade\\r\\nSec-WebSocket-Version: 13\\r\\nSec-WebSocket-Key: \" + key + \"\\r\\n\\r\\n\";\n            output.write(request.getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII)); output.flush();\n            ByteArrayOutputStream header = new ByteArrayOutputStream(); int suffix = 0;\n            while (suffix != 0x0d0a0d0a) {\n                int ch = input.read(); if (ch < 0 || header.size() > 16_384) throw new IOException(\"Invalid HTTP upgrade\");\n                header.write(ch); suffix = (suffix << 8) | ch;\n            }\n            String[] lines = header.toString(\"US-ASCII\").split(\"\\r\\n\");\n            if (!lines[0].matches(\"HTTP/1\\\\.[01] 101(?: .*)?\")) throw new IOException(\"Receiver upgrade failed: \" + lines[0]);\n            Map<String,String> headers = new HashMap<>();\n            for (int i=1;i<lines.length;i++) { int colon=lines[i].indexOf(':'); if(colon>0) headers.put(lines[i].substring(0,colon).toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT),lines[i].substring(colon+1).trim()); }\n            String accept=Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-1\").digest((key+\"258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-C5AB0DC85B11\").getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII)));\n            if (!accept.equals(headers.get(\"sec-websocket-accept\")) || !\"websocket\".equalsIgnoreCase(headers.get(\"upgrade\"))\n                    || !Arrays.asList(headers.getOrDefault(\"connection\", \"\").toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT).split(\"\\\\s*,\\\\s*\")).contains(\"upgrade\"))\n                throw new IOException(\"Invalid WebSocket upgrade response\");\n            new Thread(() -> { try {\n                long lastPing=System.nanoTime();\n                while(!closed) {\n                    byte[] packet=outgoing.poll(2,TimeUnit.SECONDS);\n                    if(closed)break;\n                    if(packet!=null)writeFrame(1,packet);\n                    if(System.nanoTime()-lastPing>10_000_000_000L){writeFrame(9,new byte[0]);lastPing=System.nanoTime();}\n                }\n            }\n                catch(InterruptedException ex) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }\n                catch(Exception ex) { if(!closed) listener.failed(\"Send failed: \"+ex.getMessage()); close(); }\n            },\"sensor-tls-writer\").start();\n            listener.opened();\n            DataInputStream data = new DataInputStream(input);\n            ByteArrayOutputStream fragmented = null;\n            while(!closed) {\n                int first=data.readUnsignedByte(), second=data.readUnsignedByte(), opcode=first&15;\n                boolean fin=(first&128)!=0;\n                if((first&112)!=0 || (second&128)!=0) throw new IOException(\"Unsupported server frame flags\");\n                long length=second&127;\n                if(length==126) length=data.readUnsignedShort(); else if(length==127) length=data.readLong();\n                if(length<0 || length>1_000_000 || (opcode>=8 && (!fin || length>125))) throw new IOException(\"Server frame exceeds limits\");\n                byte[] payload=new byte[(int)length]; data.readFully(payload);\n                if(opcode==8) throw new IOException(\"Receiver closed the session\");\n                if(opcode==9) { writeFrame(10,payload); continue; }\n                if(opcode==10) continue;\n                if(opcode==1 && fragmented==null) {\n                    if(fin) listener.text(new String(payload,StandardCharsets.UTF_8));\n                    else { fragmented=new ByteArrayOutputStream(); fragmented.write(payload); }\n                } else if(opcode==0 && fragmented!=null) {\n                    fragmented.write(payload); if(fragmented.size()>1_000_000) throw new IOException(\"Message too large\");\n                    if(fin) { listener.text(fragmented.toString(\"UTF-8\")); fragmented=null; }\n                } else throw new IOException(\"Expected text message\");\n            }\n        } catch(Exception ex) { if(!closed) listener.failed(ex.getMessage()==null?ex.getClass().getSimpleName():ex.getMessage()); }\n        finally { close(); }\n    }\n\n    private synchronized void writeFrame(int opcode, byte[] payload) throws IOException {\n        if(closed) throw new IOException(\"Session closed\");\n        output.writeByte(0x80|opcode);\n        if(payload.length<126) output.writeByte(0x80|payload.length);\n        else if(payload.length<=65535) {output.writeByte(0x80|126);output.writeShort(payload.length);}\n        else {output.writeByte(0x80|127);output.writeLong(payload.length);}\n        byte[] mask=new byte[4]; random.nextBytes(mask); output.write(mask);\n        byte[] masked=payload.clone(); for(int i=0;i<masked.length;i++) masked[i]^=mask[i%4];\n        output.write(masked); output.flush();\n    }\n    public void close() {\n        closed=true; outgoing.clear();\n        SSLSocket connection=socket; if(connection!=null) try {connection.close();} catch(IOException ignored) {}\n        // Wake a waiting writer; it exits after the closed check in writeFrame.\n        outgoing.offer(new byte[0]);\n    }\n}\n"},{"file":"apps/android/build-local.ps1","body":"param(\n  [Parameter(Mandatory=$true)][string]$Jdk,\n  [Parameter(Mandatory=$true)][string]$Platform,\n  [Parameter(Mandatory=$true)][string]$BuildTools\n)\n$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'\n$projectRoot = $PSScriptRoot\n$outputDir = Join-Path $projectRoot '.build'\n$classesDir = Join-Path $outputDir 'classes'\n$signingDir = Join-Path $projectRoot '.signing'\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $classesDir,$signingDir | Out-Null\nfunction Run-Tool([string]$Program, [string[]]$Arguments) {\n  & $Program @Arguments\n  if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw \"$Program failed: $LASTEXITCODE\" }\n}\n$javaFiles = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath (Join-Path $projectRoot 'app/src/main/java') -Filter '*.java' -Recurse | ForEach-Object FullName)\n$androidJar = Join-Path $Platform 'android.jar'\nRun-Tool (Join-Path $Jdk 'bin/javac.exe') (@('-encoding','UTF-8','-source','8','-target','8','-classpath',$androidJar,'-d',$classesDir) + $javaFiles)\n$classFiles = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $classesDir -Filter '*.class' -Recurse | ForEach-Object FullName)\nRun-Tool (Join-Path $Jdk 'bin/java.exe') (@('-cp',(Join-Path $BuildTools 'lib/d8.jar'),'com.android.tools.r8.D8','--lib',$androidJar,'--min-api','26','--output',$outputDir) + $classFiles)\n$unsignedApk = Join-Path $outputDir 'sensor-unsigned.apk'\n$manifestDoc = [xml](Get-Content -LiteralPath (Join-Path $projectRoot 'app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml') -Raw)\n$manifestDoc.manifest.SetAttribute('package','org.nodestomaps.sensor')\n$buildManifest = Join-Path $outputDir 'AndroidManifest.xml'\n$manifestDoc.Save($buildManifest)\nRun-Tool (Join-Path $BuildTools 'aapt2.exe') @('link','--manifest',$buildManifest,'-I',$androidJar,'--min-sdk-version','26','--target-sdk-version','35','--version-code','1','--version-name','0.1.0','-o',$unsignedApk)\nPush-Location $outputDir\ntry { Run-Tool (Join-Path $Jdk 'bin/jar.exe') @('uf',$unsignedApk,'classes.dex') } finally { Pop-Location }\n$alignedApk = Join-Path $outputDir 'sensor-aligned.apk'\nRun-Tool (Join-Path $BuildTools 'zipalign.exe') @('-f','4',$unsignedApk,$alignedApk)\n$keystore = Join-Path $signingDir 'classroom-debug.jks'\nif (!(Test-Path -LiteralPath $keystore)) {\n  Run-Tool (Join-Path $Jdk 'bin/keytool.exe') @('-genkeypair','-keystore',$keystore,'-storepass','android','-keypass','android','-alias','classroom','-keyalg','RSA','-keysize','2048','-validity','3650','-dname','CN=Nodes to Maps Classroom Debug')\n}\n$finalApk = Join-Path $outputDir 'nodes-to-maps-sensor-debug.apk'\nRun-Tool (Join-Path $Jdk 'bin/java.exe') @('-jar',(Join-Path $BuildTools 'lib/apksigner.jar'),'sign','--ks',$keystore,'--ks-key-alias','classroom','--ks-pass','pass:android','--key-pass','pass:android','--out',$finalApk,$alignedApk)\nRun-Tool (Join-Path $Jdk 'bin/java.exe') @('-jar',(Join-Path $BuildTools 'lib/apksigner.jar'),'verify','--verbose',$finalApk)\nGet-FileHash -LiteralPath $finalApk -Algorithm SHA256\nWrite-Output \"Classroom test APK: $finalApk\"\n"},{"file":"apps/android/build.gradle","body":"plugins { id 'com.android.application' version '8.7.3' apply false }\n"},{"file":"apps/android/gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties","body":"distributionBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME\ndistributionPath=wrapper/dists\ndistributionUrl=https\\://services.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-8.9-bin.zip\ndistributionSha256Sum=d725d707bfabd4dfdc958c624003b3c80accc03f7037b5122c4b1d0ef15cecab\nnetworkTimeout=10000\nvalidateDistributionUrl=true\nzipStoreBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME\nzipStorePath=wrapper/dists\n"},{"file":"apps/android/gradle.properties","body":"org.gradle.jvmargs=-Xmx2048m -Dfile.encoding=UTF-8\nandroid.useAndroidX=false\n"},{"file":"apps/android/settings.gradle","body":"pluginManagement { repositories { google(); mavenCentral(); gradlePluginPortal() } }\ndependencyResolutionManagement { repositoriesMode.set(RepositoriesMode.FAIL_ON_PROJECT_REPOS); repositories { google(); mavenCentral() } }\nrootProject.name = 'NodesToMapsSensor'\ninclude ':app'\n"},{"file":"maintenance/tools/calibration_to_orb.py","body":"\"\"\"Convert ROS monocular plumb_bob calibration to an ORB-SLAM3 settings file.\n\nNeeds python3-yaml. Never substitutes a guessed camera calibration.\n\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport math\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\n\n\ndef convert(c, fps):\n    k = c['camera_matrix']['data']\n    d = list(c['distortion_coefficients']['data'])\n    width, height = c['image_width'], c['image_height']\n    if c['distortion_model'] != 'plumb_bob' or len(d) not in (4, 5):\n        raise ValueError('Only 4/5-coefficient plumb_bob is supported by this converter')\n    if len(k) != 9 or not all(math.isfinite(float(v)) for v in k+d):\n        raise ValueError('Malformed calibration matrices')\n    if width <= 0 or height <= 0 or k[0] <= 0 or k[4] <= 0 or not 1 <= fps <= 30:\n        raise ValueError('Invalid dimensions, focal length or nominal frame rate')\n    d += [0.0] * (5-len(d))\n    values = {'File.version': '\"1.0\"', 'Camera.type': '\"PinHole\"',\n              'Camera1.fx': k[0], 'Camera1.fy': k[4], 'Camera1.cx': k[2], 'Camera1.cy': k[5],\n              'Camera1.k1': d[0], 'Camera1.k2': d[1], 'Camera1.p1': d[2], 'Camera1.p2': d[3], 'Camera1.k3': d[4],\n              'Camera.width': width, 'Camera.height': height, 'Camera.fps': fps, 'Camera.RGB': 0,\n              'ORBextractor.nFeatures': 1200, 'ORBextractor.scaleFactor': 1.2, 'ORBextractor.nLevels': 8,\n              'ORBextractor.iniThFAST': 20, 'ORBextractor.minThFAST': 7,\n              'Viewer.KeyFrameSize': 0.05, 'Viewer.KeyFrameLineWidth': 1, 'Viewer.GraphLineWidth': 0.9,\n              'Viewer.PointSize': 2, 'Viewer.CameraSize': 0.08, 'Viewer.CameraLineWidth': 3,\n              'Viewer.ViewpointX': 0, 'Viewer.ViewpointY': -0.7, 'Viewer.ViewpointZ': -1.8, 'Viewer.ViewpointF': 500}\n    return '%YAML:1.0\\n# Generated from measured ROS calibration; images are BGR.\\n'+''.join(f'{key}: {value}\\n' for key,value in values.items())\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument('input', type=Path)\n    parser.add_argument('output', type=Path)\n    parser.add_argument('--fps', type=float, default=10)\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    args.output.write_text(convert(yaml.safe_load(args.input.read_text()), args.fps))\n    print('Wrote measured calibration for ORB-SLAM3:', args.output)\n"}],"android":{"file":"downloads/nodes-to-maps-sensor-debug.apk","sha256":"abc1401706ace2eaa0cef64036e3589b6cec3d36d37e09e1b5c077d12a98ab89","version":"0.1.0","minAndroid":"8.0"}};
